[{:db/id 17592186402002 :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $D$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${D}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-eb6c5be4-36dc-53c7-bba2-d203e8249edd" :activity/id #uuid "eb6c5be4-36dc-53c7-bba2-d203e8249edd" :activity/title "Quiz 3" :activity/type :activity.type/assessment :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-15ba228c-73d6-5b27-b06b-8123523bb9ba" "step-dcb06b0e-9cf9-5cc0-bc6b-4c05909a7d23" "step-62869702-446a-5229-b3ad-6f5f53a978ef" "step-d76fac64-e76c-578f-8d90-9a9c135ee09b" "step-e77cc344-9a7e-5bda-bdd0-26ec1de9edf3" "step-68c78553-6e54-5eb2-8283-0a51eda5ae73"] :activity/first-step "step-15ba228c-73d6-5b27-b06b-8123523bb9ba" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-15ba228c-73d6-5b27-b06b-8123523bb9ba" :step/id #uuid "15ba228c-73d6-5b27-b06b-8123523bb9ba" :step/content 17592186402002 :step/next "step-dcb06b0e-9cf9-5cc0-bc6b-4c05909a7d23" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dcb06b0e-9cf9-5cc0-bc6b-4c05909a7d23" :step/id #uuid "dcb06b0e-9cf9-5cc0-bc6b-4c05909a7d23" :step/content 17592186342239 :step/next "step-62869702-446a-5229-b3ad-6f5f53a978ef" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-62869702-446a-5229-b3ad-6f5f53a978ef" :step/id #uuid "62869702-446a-5229-b3ad-6f5f53a978ef" :step/content 17592186262838 :step/next "step-d76fac64-e76c-578f-8d90-9a9c135ee09b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d76fac64-e76c-578f-8d90-9a9c135ee09b" :step/id #uuid "d76fac64-e76c-578f-8d90-9a9c135ee09b" :step/content 17592186501186 :step/next "step-e77cc344-9a7e-5bda-bdd0-26ec1de9edf3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e77cc344-9a7e-5bda-bdd0-26ec1de9edf3" :step/id #uuid "e77cc344-9a7e-5bda-bdd0-26ec1de9edf3" :step/content 17592186436001 :step/next "step-68c78553-6e54-5eb2-8283-0a51eda5ae73" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-68c78553-6e54-5eb2-8283-0a51eda5ae73" :step/id #uuid "68c78553-6e54-5eb2-8283-0a51eda5ae73" :step/content 17592186385429 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129282 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3d5c488c-135d-5c5b-b6fb-380729a9e440"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3d5c488c-135d-5c5b-b6fb-380729a9e440" :question/id #uuid "3d5c488c-135d-5c5b-b6fb-380729a9e440" :question/math-academy-id "q-161886" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(3,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-v \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}-1 \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(3,1)} & =(3{u}^{2}-1)\\,{|}_{u=3\\,v=1} \\\\  & =3(3)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|26|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =26\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a680a9f-c944-5dc6-b6be-a4a3dda024b5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a680a9f-c944-5dc6-b6be-a4a3dda024b5" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a680a9f-c944-5dc6-b6be-a4a3dda024b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-84d571a0-192f-5d6b-bfdc-6b4cf1be9b9e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3ba7e918-0922-576c-91b5-2e38e5a1b977" "answer-1354d1db-51ab-5f5c-944c-79110ffcc91b" "answer-84d571a0-192f-5d6b-bfdc-6b4cf1be9b9e" "answer-10477c22-e6c0-5396-865c-7efb2e452797" "answer-0edfd29e-3a12-5f5c-aa4d-c9d232e9d8ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ba7e918-0922-576c-91b5-2e38e5a1b977" :answer/id #uuid "3ba7e918-0922-576c-91b5-2e38e5a1b977" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13\\,\\delta A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1354d1db-51ab-5f5c-944c-79110ffcc91b" :answer/id #uuid "1354d1db-51ab-5f5c-944c-79110ffcc91b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\,\\delta A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84d571a0-192f-5d6b-bfdc-6b4cf1be9b9e" :answer/id #uuid "84d571a0-192f-5d6b-bfdc-6b4cf1be9b9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "26\\,\\delta A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10477c22-e6c0-5396-865c-7efb2e452797" :answer/id #uuid "10477c22-e6c0-5396-865c-7efb2e452797" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,\\delta A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0edfd29e-3a12-5f5c-aa4d-c9d232e9d8ce" :answer/id #uuid "0edfd29e-3a12-5f5c-aa4d-c9d232e9d8ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,\\delta A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-bb7e0a0d-8752-5c3e-8395-2d6ad354306a" :activity/id #uuid "bb7e0a0d-8752-5c3e-8395-2d6ad354306a" :activity/title "The Jacobian" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048219 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-7f2ac137-2220-566e-b47f-94fd0d92a847" "step-5798d0f4-36dc-5cf6-ac08-e0160e1c0ba7" "step-ee14058f-6ee6-5aca-9cfd-0d9b16daa12d" "step-1de14cf5-31b0-5c86-80ca-5ff5e29a6212" "step-a7392a57-2383-58a2-9b25-f401da0b37c1"] :activity/first-step "step-7f2ac137-2220-566e-b47f-94fd0d92a847" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-7f2ac137-2220-566e-b47f-94fd0d92a847" :step/id #uuid "7f2ac137-2220-566e-b47f-94fd0d92a847" :step/content 17592186490290 :step/next "step-5798d0f4-36dc-5cf6-ac08-e0160e1c0ba7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5798d0f4-36dc-5cf6-ac08-e0160e1c0ba7" :step/id #uuid "5798d0f4-36dc-5cf6-ac08-e0160e1c0ba7" :step/content 17592186389195 :step/next "step-ee14058f-6ee6-5aca-9cfd-0d9b16daa12d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ee14058f-6ee6-5aca-9cfd-0d9b16daa12d" :step/id #uuid "ee14058f-6ee6-5aca-9cfd-0d9b16daa12d" :step/content 17592186389209 :step/next "step-1de14cf5-31b0-5c86-80ca-5ff5e29a6212" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1de14cf5-31b0-5c86-80ca-5ff5e29a6212" :step/id #uuid "1de14cf5-31b0-5c86-80ca-5ff5e29a6212" :step/content 17592186281367 :step/next "step-a7392a57-2383-58a2-9b25-f401da0b37c1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a7392a57-2383-58a2-9b25-f401da0b37c1" :step/id #uuid "a7392a57-2383-58a2-9b25-f401da0b37c1" :step/content "question-3d5c488c-135d-5c5b-b6fb-380729a9e440" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186181446 :answer-field/choices 17592186181447] [:db/retract 17592186181446 :answer-field/choices 17592186181448] [:db/retract 17592186181446 :answer-field/choices 17592186181449] [:db/retract 17592186181446 :answer-field/choices 17592186181450] [:db/retract 17592186181446 :answer-field/choices 17592186181451] [:db/retract 17592186183463 :answer-field/choices 17592186183464] [:db/retract 17592186183463 :answer-field/choices 17592186183465] [:db/retract 17592186183463 :answer-field/choices 17592186183466] [:db/retract 17592186183463 :answer-field/choices 17592186183467] [:db/retract 17592186183463 :answer-field/choices 17592186183468] [:db/retract 17592186183517 :answer-field/choices 17592186183520] [:db/retract 17592186183517 :answer-field/choices 17592186183521] [:db/retract 17592186183517 :answer-field/choices 17592186183522] [:db/retract 17592186183517 :answer-field/choices 17592186183518] [:db/retract 17592186183517 :answer-field/choices 17592186183519] {:db/id 17592186184145 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-17d541e7-21d6-5b63-bfea-03f360c417cf" "question-42bf2d9b-a245-5919-95ec-686175cfea53"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186183951 :question/worked-solution "To find out how long it takes to serve eight customers, we need to multiply $2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}$ by $8.$\n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 4) + 1}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $8$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n8 = \\dfrac{8}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{\\color{red}4} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}8}{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{red}4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{blue}8$) have a common \nfactor of $4.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{1} & =\\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1}\\times 2 \\\\  & =9\\times 2 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes $18$ minutes to serve $8$ customers." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184146 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7c7467de-330b-5939-83a9-4571e046a2da"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186183949 :question/worked-solution "To find out the area used to grow corn, we need to calculate $2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3} \\times \\dfrac{3}{5}.$ \n\nFirst, we write $2 \\,\\dfrac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 3) + 2}{3} = \\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{\\color{red}3} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{red}3$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{blue}3$) have a common factor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{5} & =\\frac{8}{5}\\times \\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\times 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by converting this fraction to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n8\\div 5 = 1\\,\\textrm{R} \\, 3 = 1\\,\\dfrac{3}{5} \\, .\n$$\n\nSo, $1\\,\\dfrac{3}{5}$ acres would be used to grow corn." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184147 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-222813e8-d21a-59ba-b697-7bcb16bc7844" "question-35b950d1-e08b-5f6b-af7f-1977577a5d81"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186183950 :question/worked-solution "To find out how many ounces of cocoa it took to make the chocolate bars, we need to calculate $1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} \\times 2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}.$    \n\nFirst, we write $1\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 4) + 1}{4}= \\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}=\\dfrac{(2 \\times 3)+2}{3}= \\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\color{red}4} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}8}{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{red}4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{blue}8$) have a common factor of $4.$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{3} & =\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 2}{3\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10}{3} =3\\,\\textrm{R}\\, 1 = 3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, it took $3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{oz}$ of cocoa to make Casey's chocolate bars." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-17d541e7-21d6-5b63-bfea-03f360c417cf" :question/id #uuid "17d541e7-21d6-5b63-bfea-03f360c417cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-193579" :question/problem "Daniel reads $2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ chapters of a book every hour. How many chapters does he read in $3$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "To find out the number of chapters Daniel reads, we need to multiply $2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ by $3.$\n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(2\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}.\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($3$) and the second fraction's numerator ($3$) have a common factor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{1} & =\\frac{7}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{1}\\times 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{1} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Daniel reads $7$ chapters in three hours." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b97851e5-e9aa-5018-a200-c45258f97324"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b97851e5-e9aa-5018-a200-c45258f97324" :answer-field/id #uuid "b97851e5-e9aa-5018-a200-c45258f97324" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3ef7806d-fccb-5849-a7b6-053e4643b298" :answer-field/choices ["answer-689a4c42-ec46-5fb6-8385-9e072e54eb2f" "answer-92fb21d5-96d0-54c8-91f9-5f207e357e6e" "answer-3ef7806d-fccb-5849-a7b6-053e4643b298" "answer-e0e48f42-1be4-5a71-8f7e-2ca629c747e3" "answer-60cd3f11-ed12-5597-869e-0704c4145a3c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-689a4c42-ec46-5fb6-8385-9e072e54eb2f" :answer/id #uuid "689a4c42-ec46-5fb6-8385-9e072e54eb2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92fb21d5-96d0-54c8-91f9-5f207e357e6e" :answer/id #uuid "92fb21d5-96d0-54c8-91f9-5f207e357e6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ef7806d-fccb-5849-a7b6-053e4643b298" :answer/id #uuid "3ef7806d-fccb-5849-a7b6-053e4643b298" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0e48f42-1be4-5a71-8f7e-2ca629c747e3" :answer/id #uuid "e0e48f42-1be4-5a71-8f7e-2ca629c747e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60cd3f11-ed12-5597-869e-0704c4145a3c" :answer/id #uuid "60cd3f11-ed12-5597-869e-0704c4145a3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-42bf2d9b-a245-5919-95ec-686175cfea53" :question/id #uuid "42bf2d9b-a245-5919-95ec-686175cfea53" :question/math-academy-id "q-193555" :question/problem "Mario fills three bottles with water. If the capacity of every bottle is $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ liters, how much water did he use to fill the bottles?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the total volume of water used to fill the bottles, we need to multiply $3$ by $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nFirst, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}.\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($3$) and the second fraction's denominator ($3$) have a common factor of $3.$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{3}{3}\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =1\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mario used $4$ liters of water to fill the bottles." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8af8a66b-1ac8-5cdc-8665-c6b9c6575729"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8af8a66b-1ac8-5cdc-8665-c6b9c6575729" :answer-field/id #uuid "8af8a66b-1ac8-5cdc-8665-c6b9c6575729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c33617b4-bd39-53c7-a9c3-703ec4bf8bad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a3a8c3c3-b1ce-5a94-bbb7-0d2565ba4c59" "answer-c33617b4-bd39-53c7-a9c3-703ec4bf8bad" "answer-2b25eb19-bb5d-5b3e-ba1e-9236597e57a6" "answer-0bce7c2c-3cb9-58c1-8a84-8000fa8f71af" "answer-e2d56cab-7864-5573-8143-a97b2d71cecc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3a8c3c3-b1ce-5a94-bbb7-0d2565ba4c59" :answer/id #uuid "a3a8c3c3-b1ce-5a94-bbb7-0d2565ba4c59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c33617b4-bd39-53c7-a9c3-703ec4bf8bad" :answer/id #uuid "c33617b4-bd39-53c7-a9c3-703ec4bf8bad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b25eb19-bb5d-5b3e-ba1e-9236597e57a6" :answer/id #uuid "2b25eb19-bb5d-5b3e-