 given that $P(T>2.947)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.005}=2.947.\n$$\n\n![](images/bb/bbaa00deb18e38f1b201317615a790764519ad5d2c5b436fe13044a09c71beac.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 45\\pm 2.947\\cdot \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & 45\\pm 6.631\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-035310c3-afea-5fec-b2c8-a377fd8df157"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-035310c3-afea-5fec-b2c8-a377fd8df157" :answer-field/id #uuid "035310c3-afea-5fec-b2c8-a377fd8df157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6d5b0270-2dff-535f-9067-e5382d8aa019" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5297df39-68a6-5686-88c6-517cb9a39e37" "answer-77ca80e5-2fd1-549f-9694-19cd2d235c8e" "answer-8635bc80-2d87-5a03-8019-42b20d00f065" "answer-6d5b0270-2dff-535f-9067-e5382d8aa019" "answer-19649959-4003-53db-91d0-5d410eaa2707"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5297df39-68a6-5686-88c6-517cb9a39e37" :answer/id #uuid "5297df39-68a6-5686-88c6-517cb9a39e37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\pm 6.136" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77ca80e5-2fd1-549f-9694-19cd2d235c8e" :answer/id #uuid "77ca80e5-2fd1-549f-9694-19cd2d235c8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45\\pm 3.315" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8635bc80-2d87-5a03-8019-42b20d00f065" :answer/id #uuid "8635bc80-2d87-5a03-8019-42b20d00f065" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45\\pm 6.136" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d5b0270-2dff-535f-9067-e5382d8aa019" :answer/id #uuid "6d5b0270-2dff-535f-9067-e5382d8aa019" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45\\pm 6.631" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19649959-4003-53db-91d0-5d410eaa2707" :answer/id #uuid "19649959-4003-53db-91d0-5d410eaa2707" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "61\\pm 6.361" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188555 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=9$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $42$ and the sample standard deviation is $6,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=42,\\,n=9,\\,s=6.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\n![](images/84/84f060ec1a5e8a8b3db0c0866ebef9db1c242e944717a5abfd28447e0eddb9be.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}42 & \\pm 3.355\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ 42 & \\pm 6.71\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185610 :answer-field/correct "answer-40f5bce4-7c32-53bf-862f-b2d2ea8f79b9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-12e9d1eb-a1d6-5f2e-9ffa-84f52aa385fd" "answer-40f5bce4-7c32-53bf-862f-b2d2ea8f79b9" "answer-a34d9c75-e0b4-5580-94b7-f7a19bfeccb0" "answer-bf308865-2733-5a23-ba5e-bbeb4af2ef6b" "answer-e28abb86-7056-5dd2-8dc2-888c83ced68c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12e9d1eb-a1d6-5f2e-9ffa-84f52aa385fd" :answer/id #uuid "12e9d1eb-a1d6-5f2e-9ffa-84f52aa385fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42\\pm 3.212" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40f5bce4-7c32-53bf-862f-b2d2ea8f79b9" :answer/id #uuid "40f5bce4-7c32-53bf-862f-b2d2ea8f79b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42\\pm 6.71" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a34d9c75-e0b4-5580-94b7-f7a19bfeccb0" :answer/id #uuid "a34d9c75-e0b4-5580-94b7-f7a19bfeccb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42\\pm 7.196" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf308865-2733-5a23-ba5e-bbeb4af2ef6b" :answer/id #uuid "bf308865-2733-5a23-ba5e-bbeb4af2ef6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "99\\pm 3.212" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e28abb86-7056-5dd2-8dc2-888c83ced68c" :answer/id #uuid "e28abb86-7056-5dd2-8dc2-888c83ced68c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "99\\pm 7.196" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b03a1f2-3bac-59ff-a005-6c994dad1ca7" :question/id #uuid "6b03a1f2-3bac-59ff-a005-6c994dad1ca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-142505" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=121$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $12.5,$ and the sample standard deviation is $1.65,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.617)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $120$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=121\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=12.5,\\,n=121,\\,s=1.65.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.617)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{120,0.005}=2.617.\n$$\n\n![](images/18/18456a4a686aae65f078015f1669cad8e1316b66166567f22df35345ebd87fa4.png)\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.5 & \\pm 2.617\\cdot \\frac{1.65}{\\sqrt{121}} \\\\ 12.5 & \\pm 0.393\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-52a91344-29c8-5858-8b96-774cbbd1ece8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-52a91344-29c8-5858-8b96-774cbbd1ece8" :answer-field/id #uuid "52a91344-29c8-5858-8b96-774cbbd1ece8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b84f0546-d5eb-5ef1-8d85-f69877397c72" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a5e89b00-8ba0-5ba2-8bc4-b3de1b014e96" "answer-a69ce0fd-17c4-56cf-9b44-09ab31b94be1" "answer-312b5a90-f9ff-5cd3-a397-fb30d50599fb" "answer-b84f0546-d5eb-5ef1-8d85-f69877397c72" "answer-0650c1b6-ebba-5d47-b139-03e824ee2178"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5e89b00-8ba0-5ba2-8bc4-b3de1b014e96" :answer/id #uuid "a5e89b00-8ba0-5ba2-8bc4-b3de1b014e96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11.5\\pm 2.617" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a69ce0fd-17c4-56cf-9b44-09ab31b94be1" :answer/id #uuid "a69ce0fd-17c4-56cf-9b44-09ab31b94be1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11\\pm 0.548" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-312b5a90-f9ff-5cd3-a397-fb30d50599fb" :answer/id #uuid "312b5a90-f9ff-5cd3-a397-fb30d50599fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11\\pm 1.384" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b84f0546-d5eb-5ef1-8d85-f69877397c72" :answer/id #uuid "b84f0546-d5eb-5ef1-8d85-f69877397c72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.5\\pm 0.393" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0650c1b6-ebba-5d47-b139-03e824ee2178" :answer/id #uuid "0650c1b6-ebba-5d47-b139-03e824ee2178" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.5\\pm 0.448" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4ae17796-447a-5d04-9973-717e1d847688" :question/id #uuid "4ae17796-447a-5d04-9973-717e1d847688" :question/math-academy-id "q-142506" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=225$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $98,$ and the sample standard deviation is $30,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.598)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $224$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=225\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=98,\\,n=225,\\,s=30.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.598)=0.005.$ As a result,\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{224,0.005}=2.598.\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2b711e6fe35f64d886472fa38c5a65afd7473b344949d7effd49405946f3cee5.png)\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}98 & \\pm 2.598\\cdot \\frac{30}{\\sqrt{225}} \\\\ 98 & \\pm 5.196\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fe9a2f0e-9ecf-5c31-b505-d6c46aeae913"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fe9a2f0e-9ecf-5c31-b505-d6c46aeae913" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe9a2f0e-9ecf-5c31-b505-d6c46aeae913" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b3fd3412-40e3-568d-9e28-0ca3f5f3b050" :answer-field/choices ["answer-05c10d15-e976-5984-8aa6-6c11525b58bd" "answer-aec2e128-f4aa-5c9d-9489-2ac45fc400b1" "answer-941b3c53-f51f-5225-8e09-99e78b4ed268" "answer-8a23c656-13bd-5339-8580-b3bf92d9bf3c" "answer-b3fd3412-40e3-568d-9e28-0ca3f5f3b050"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05c10d15-e976-5984-8aa6-6c11525b58bd" :answer/id #uuid "05c10d15-e976-5984-8aa6-6c11525b58bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "225\\pm 1.319" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aec2e128-f4aa-5c9d-9489-2ac45fc400b1" :answer/id #uuid "aec2e128-f4aa-5c9d-9489-2ac45fc400b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "225\\pm 4.122" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-941b3c53-f51f-5225-8e09-99e78b4ed268" :answer/id #uuid "941b3c53-f51f-5225-8e09-99e78b4ed268" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "98\\pm 13.189" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a23c656-13bd-5339-8580-b3bf92d9bf3c" :answer/id #uuid "8a23c656-13bd-5339-8580-b3bf92d9bf3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "98\\pm 4.122" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3fd3412-40e3-568d-9e28-0ca3f5f3b050" :answer/id #uuid "b3fd3412-40e3-568d-9e28-0ca3f5f3b050" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "98\\pm 5.196" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2d606c2e-8088-532b-9f1f-331fecee3381" :question/id #uuid "2d606c2e-8088-532b-9f1f-331fecee3381" :question/math-academy-id "q-142518" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=81$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $12,$ and the sample standard deviation is $5,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.664)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $80$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.  \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=12,\\,n=81,\\,s=5.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.664)=0.05.$ As a result,  \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{80,0.05}=1.664.\n$$\n\n![](images/c1/c1bf3a9f49bb4b4fb24d4fb39bdf0b02743efd1c280875332e5f3bbc1539d953.png)\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & \\pm 1.664\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\ 12 & \\pm 0.924\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-80164f95-be54-5d4a-8c56-3abf9a42e850"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-80164f95-be54-5d4a-8c56-3abf9a42e850" :answer-field/id #uuid "80164f95-be54-5d4a-8c56-3abf9a42e850" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cfca92cd-2321-565c-b98f-3c04a14a8f7c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b8f4c0b9-3e9c-50a4-845e-b51b352ed73a" "answer-cfca92cd-2321-565c-b98f-3c04a14a8f7c" "answer-ab01bf03-621b-5f1b-906d-1df991599f9f" "answer-75fad598-5541-56fa-b53a-a91cb54eaa0d" "answer-93b5491c-6aa1-5900-81e9-ad3ec4f7dae9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8f4c0b9-3e9c-50a4-845e-b51b352ed73a" :answer/id #uuid "b8f4c0b9-3e9c-50a4-845e-b51b352ed73a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\pm 0.462" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfca92cd-2321-565c-b98f-3c04a14a8f7c" :answer/id #uuid "cfca92cd-2321-565c-b98f-3c04a14a8f7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\pm 0.924" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab01bf03-621b-5f1b-906d-1df991599f9f" :answer/id #uuid "ab01bf03-621b-5f1b-906d-1df991599f9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\pm 3.698" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75fad598-5541-56fa-b53a-a91cb54eaa0d" :answer/id #uuid "75fad598-5541-56fa-b53a-a91cb54eaa0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\pm 0.462" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93b5491c-6aa1-5900-81e9-ad3ec4f7dae9" :answer/id #uuid "93b5491c-6aa1-5900-81e9-ad3ec4f7dae9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\pm 1.849" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b0647362-6c93-5a80-87bb-0e4349d8ef9c" :question/id #uuid "b0647362-6c93-5a80-87bb-0e4349d8ef9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-142515" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=400$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $57,$ and the sample standard deviation is $26,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.649)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $399$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=400\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=57,\\,n=400,\\,s=26.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.649)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{399,0.05}=1.649.\n$$\n\n![](images/7d/7d2cc71801ad3826dddfb670518ad038ccbf11f363569896fe3c3b673e5c58a6.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}57 & \\pm 1.649\\cdot \\frac{26}{\\sqrt{400}} \\\\ 57 & \\pm 2.144\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6957eb55-ecd2-59f6-bebd-02343910a899"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6957eb55-ecd2-59f6-bebd-02343910a899" :answer-field/id #uuid "6957eb55-ecd2-59f6-bebd-02343910a899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7646f297-8c51-517d-bde6-5c799274e0c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-63a4386b-2b97-59a9-8364-19b069a3dffb" "answer-f5ccec8d-661c-5c0e-96c8-d2eb4c7a9ec3" "answer-ecc4a441-63c0-551c-81a2-0c4aefefca13" "answer-7646f297-8c51-517d-bde6-5c799274e0c8" "answer-bb647bb7-56bb-5640-8415-c8b33e620036"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63a4386b-2b97-59a9-8364-19b069a3dffb" :answer/id #uuid "63a4386b-2b97-59a9-8364-19b069a3dffb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "26\\pm 2.144" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5ccec8d-661c-5c0e-96c8-d2eb4c7a9ec3" :answer/id #uuid "f5ccec8d-661c-5c0e-96c8-d2eb4c7a9ec3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "26\\pm 3.722" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecc4a441-63c0-551c-81a2-0c4aefefca13" :answer/id #uuid "ecc4a441-63c0-551c-81a2-0c4aefefca13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "57\\pm 0.536" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7646f297-8c51-517d-bde6-5c799274e0c8" :answer/id #uuid "7646f297-8c51-517d-bde6-5c799274e0c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "57\\pm 2.144" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb647bb7-56bb-5640-8415-c8b33e620036" :answer/id #uuid "bb647bb7-56bb-5640-8415-c8b33e620036" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "83\\pm 1.072" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-79c0fa2f-3630-597c-948c-a11745cb42f9" :question/id #uuid "79c0fa2f-3630-597c-948c-a11745cb42f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-142512" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=256$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $105,$ and the sample standard deviation is $36,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.969)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $255$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=256\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=105,\\,n=256,\\,s=36.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.969)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{255,0.025}=1.969.\n$$\n\n![](images/d1/d108130264501813e8bfed332f345d8d005095127c5d9d8e169a1c2ae83b2ec7.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}105 & \\pm 1.969\\cdot \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{256}} \\\\ 105 & \\pm 4.430\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eff581c0-ce0f-5c9d-a28d-8d8080f6ee11"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eff581c0-ce0f-5c9d-a28d-8d8080f6ee11" :answer-field/id #uuid "eff581c0-ce0f-5c9d-a28d-8d8080f6ee11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a0fdd3db-8f16-5e37-859e-43fec8afa143" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ba1b836e-ac29-5d06-9c58-b288eddf7c1e" "answer-aaf5abd3-f689-5199-bfae-8dce1adc46fd" "answer-611540a5-49ab-59e6-9454-8829d53dac0a" "answer-a0fdd3db-8f16-5e37-859e-43fec8afa143" "answer-f2d8b850-b2ae-5b40-a400-ab9ed8f5c91d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba1b836e-ac29-5d06-9c58-b288eddf7c1e" :answer/id #uuid "ba1b836e-ac29-5d06-9c58-b288eddf7c1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "102\\pm 1.108" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aaf5abd3-f689-5199-bfae-8dce1adc46fd" :answer/id #uuid "aaf5abd3-f689-5199-bfae-8dce1adc46fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "102\\pm 2.215" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-611540a5-49ab-59e6-9454-8829d53dac0a" :answer/id #uuid "611540a5-49ab-59e6-9454-8829d53dac0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "105\\pm 1.108" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0fdd3db-8f16-5e37-859e-43fec8afa143" :answer/id #uuid "a0fdd3db-8f16-5e37-859e-43fec8afa143" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "105\\pm 4.430" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2d8b850-b2ae-5b40-a400-ab9ed8f5c91d" :answer/id #uuid "f2d8b850-b2ae-5b40-a400-ab9ed8f5c91d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "105\\pm 8.860" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d3b129d2-0cd5-58e9-b5e8-9079b387fb53" :question/id #uuid "d3b129d2-0cd5-58e9-b5e8-9079b387fb53" :question/math-academy-id "q-142921" :question/problem "The body mass index (BMI) of Dr. Lee's patients is normally distributed. A random sample of $9$ patients yields a mean BMI of $25.5\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}$ with a standard deviation of $2.6\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}.$ Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean BMI $\\mu $ among all of Dr. Lee's patients. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=25.5\\,\\,n=9,\\,s=2.6\\,.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{8,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{8,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=9-1=8$ degrees of freedom. \n\n![](images/ce/ce7d5fc9bbf2c9e28a0862247e6f762cad8a718401828070e96041e7f82b1891.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{8,0.05}=1.860:$   \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $1.860$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 25.5\\pm 1.860\\cdot \\frac{2.6}{\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\  & 25.5\\pm 1.612\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0aff946d-6fe9-5a23-b7aa-c4e702a4b97f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0aff946d-6fe9-5a23-b7aa-c4e702a4b97f" :answer-field/id #uuid "0aff946d-6fe9-5a23-b7aa-c4e702a4b97f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-65847e64-c39f-539f-8e40-4781fe4afa82" :answer-field/choices ["answer-286823f5-e1ca-5ff4-b00f-3035726d945d" "answer-bd4d75c3-5101-5fb0-a2ee-63489e79479d" "answer-420d3790-6d42-5aa4-a5f8-46e7f063546b" "answer-324f3c3c-0d5a-54d1-9898-12a9ac39319f" "answer-65847e64-c39f-539f-8e40-4781fe4afa82"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-286823f5-e1ca-5ff4-b00f-3035726d945d" :answer/id #uuid "286823f5-e1ca-5ff4-b00f-3035726d945d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "23.5\\pm 1.092\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd4d75c3-5101-5fb0-a2ee-63489e79479d" :answer/id #uuid "bd4d75c3-5101-5fb0-a2ee-63489e79479d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24.5\\pm 0.512\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-420d3790-6d42-5aa4-a5f8-46e7f063546b" :answer/id #uuid "420d3790-6d42-5aa4-a5f8-46e7f063546b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24.5\\pm 1.012\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-324f3c3c-0d5a-54d1-9898-12a9ac39319f" :answer/id #uuid "324f3c3c-0d5a-54d1-9898-12a9ac39319f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25.5\\pm 0.612\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65847e64-c39f-539f-8e40-4781fe4afa82" :answer/id #uuid "65847e64-c39f-539f-8e40-4781fe4afa82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25.5\\pm 1.612\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3c887832-1efe-5629-bd9f-7e1600f20497" :question/id #uuid "3c887832-1efe-5629-bd9f-7e1600f20497" :question/math-academy-id "q-143026" :question/problem "A thread manufacturer tests the tensile strength of a sample containing $9$ pieces of a particular type of thread.\nThe mean tensile strength of the sample is $7.8\\,\\text{lb}$ with a standard deviation of $0.06\\,\\text{lb}.$\nAssuming that tensile strengths are normally distributed, find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean tensile strength  $\\mu $ of this thread. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=7.8\\,\\text{lb},\\,n=9,\\,s=0.06\\,\\text{lb}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{8,0.005}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{8,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=9-1=8$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/11/1199336fffafda6d04b4e9562aec6b1bbc1726005a54180c8e8ed4935981ddd1.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{8,0.005}=3.355:$  \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $3.355$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |\n\n   \n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.8 & \\pm 3.355\\cdot \\frac{0.06}{\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{lb} \\\\ 7.8 & \\pm 0.067\\,\\text{lb}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5e42767-966c-51b4-b910-abefd154b0bf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5e42767-966c-51b4-b910-abefd154b0bf" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e42767-966c-51b4-b910-abefd154b0bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d700fb9d-ad2a-5ff1-97fd-6f0cc927fc6c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d700fb9d-ad2a-5ff1-97fd-6f0cc927fc6c" "answer-1c7b8c6a-761f-5a82-a1c2-b4c5a35747db" "answer-22d45bab-0972-53aa-8775-f34db848eb48" "answer-76d09209-de2a-584f-84af-58436e90ac9d" "answer-8d89c99c-293f-5b5b-82ff-6fdc2e3a206c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d700fb9d-ad2a-5ff1-97fd-6f0cc927fc6c" :answer/id #uuid "d700fb9d-ad2a-5ff1-97fd-6f0cc927fc6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.8\\pm 0.067\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c7b8c6a-761f-5a82-a1c2-b4c5a35747db" :answer/id #uuid "1c7b8c6a-761f-5a82-a1c2-b4c5a35747db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.8\\pm 0.076\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22d45bab-0972-53aa-8775-f34db848eb48" :answer/id #uuid "22d45bab-0972-53aa-8775-f34db848eb48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.8\\pm 0.08\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76d09209-de2a-584f-84af-58436e90ac9d" :answer/id #uuid "76d09209-de2a-584f-84af-58436e90ac9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\pm 0.076\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d89c99c-293f-5b5b-82ff-6fdc2e3a206c" :answer/id #uuid "8d89c99c-293f-5b5b-82ff-6fdc2e3a206c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\pm 0.08\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a68a1f9-858f-5d69-bc49-809a211decce" :question/id #uuid "7a68a1f9-858f-5d69-bc49-809a211decce" :question/math-academy-id "q-142917" :question/problem "A random sample of $100$ students from a particular university yields a mean GPA of $3.1$ with a sample standard deviation of $0.48.$ Find a  $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean GPA $\\mu $ of all students.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $99$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $100$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $101$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.625\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=3.1,\\,n=100,\\,s=0.48.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{99,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{99,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=100-1=99$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/1b/1bb4e66b68bbc1fb55a36abafb4f184fb6b25b16314e989a47fe6b3a835cea65.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{99,0.05}=1.660:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $99$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $1.660$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $100$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $101$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.625\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.1 & \\pm 1.66\\cdot \\frac{0.48}{\\sqrt{100}}\\,\\text{grade points} \\\\ 3.1 & \\pm 0.08\\text{ grade points}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8c864314-d95a-52b4-bfa6-7db070ad0c2a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8c864314-d95a-52b4-bfa6-7db070ad0c2a" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c864314-d95a-52b4-bfa6-7db070ad0c2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-38911557-7e16-582d-8b7f-6cb45159b13c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-03970a14-aff7-5c52-af6e-86f7e484a35c" "answer-86a3d3a6-4ce1-5abf-ac49-d7437dd30bdb" "answer-38911557-7e16-582d-8b7f-6cb45159b13c" "answer-ce95bb58-ce07-523a-8996-cfcfab471ce7" "answer-6b30daa1-2d5c-55b7-a422-1ffc2ef92800"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03970a14-aff7-5c52-af6e-86f7e484a35c" :answer/id #uuid "03970a14-aff7-5c52-af6e-86f7e484a35c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.48\\pm 0.08\\text{ grade points}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86a3d3a6-4ce1-5abf-ac49-d7437dd30bdb" :answer/id #uuid "86a3d3a6-4ce1-5abf-ac49-d7437dd30bdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100\\pm 0.04\\text{ grade points}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38911557-7e16-582d-8b7f-6cb45159b13c" :answer/id #uuid "38911557-7e16-582d-8b7f-6cb45159b13c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.1\\pm 0.08\\text{ grade points}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce95bb58-ce07-523a-8996-cfcfab471ce7" :answer/id #uuid "ce95bb58-ce07-523a-8996-cfcfab471ce7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.1\\pm 0.48\\text{ grade points}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b30daa1-2d5c-55b7-a422-1ffc2ef92800" :answer/id #uuid "6b30daa1-2d5c-55b7-a422-1ffc2ef92800" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.58\\pm 0.18\\text{ grade points}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188554 :question/problem "A farmer grows a particular variety of apples. He sampled $81$ apples from the harvest and found that the sample mean weight of apples is $120\\,\\text{g}$ and the sample standard deviation is $18\\,\\text{g}.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean weight $\\mu $ for the entire harvest. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $79$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.640\\,$ |\n| $80$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.639\\,$ |\n| $81$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.373\\,$ | $\\,2.638\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where  \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=120\\,\\text{g},\\,n=81,\\,s=18\\,\\text{g}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{80,0.025}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{80,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=81-1=80$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/69/69f7cbb0e67e69c2ef95f9033f41ecd37a0ed21de94bcb903af17284aeb01155.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{80,0.005}=2.639:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $79$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.640$ |\n| $80$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.639$ |\n| $81$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.373$ | $2.638$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & \\pm 2.639\\cdot \\frac{18}{\\sqrt{81}}\\,\\text{g} \\\\ 120 & \\pm 5.278\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185604 :answer-field/correct "answer-3f6e7dfb-0ac9-59a1-8797-a08ebedff424" :answer-field/choices ["answer-376a2dce-046e-5e42-a479-11f7c9e9d10b" "answer-3e0430a5-38b2-5298-951f-2121518d9e2b" "answer-3f6e7dfb-0ac9-59a1-8797-a08ebedff424" "answer-2a22da69-4c5a-57b0-8f3c-1a6dbcece4e9" "answer-a91a6c68-6420-5739-a652-82cc2f49f035"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-376a2dce-046e-5e42-a479-11f7c9e9d10b" :answer/id #uuid "376a2dce-046e-5e42-a479-11f7c9e9d10b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120\\pm 1.962\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e0430a5-38b2-5298-951f-2121518d9e2b" :answer/id #uuid "3e0430a5-38b2-5298-951f-2121518d9e2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120\\pm 3.925\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f6e7dfb-0ac9-59a1-8797-a08ebedff424" :answer/id #uuid "3f6e7dfb-0ac9-59a1-8797-a08ebedff424" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120\\pm 5.278\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a22da69-4c5a-57b0-8f3c-1a6dbcece4e9" :answer/id #uuid "2a22da69-4c5a-57b0-8f3c-1a6dbcece4e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\pm 3.925\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a91a6c68-6420-5739-a652-82cc2f49f035" :answer/id #uuid "a91a6c68-6420-5739-a652-82cc2f49f035" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\pm 7.152\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b9cb4d01-534f-524c-85ee-ddefd795826f" :question/id #uuid "b9cb4d01-534f-524c-85ee-ddefd795826f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143033" :question/problem "A sample of the different prices of a given product in $64$ randomly selected stores in a given state yields a mean price of $$37.15$ with a sample standard deviation of $$2.00.$  Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean price of the product  $\\mu $ across the state. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $62$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.670\\,$ | $\\,1.999\\,$ | $\\,2.388\\,$ | $\\,2.657\\,$ |\n| $63$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.387\\,$ | $\\,2.656\\,$ |\n| $64$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.386\\,$ | $\\,2.655\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=$37.15,\\,n=64,\\,s=$2.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{99,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{63,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=64-1=63$ degrees of freedom. \n\n![](images/43/43ddb73bfc30887b0de7b7debf98407dbcea330ebe55c485982d5c05c0681c9a.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{63,0.05}=1.669:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $62$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.670\\,$ | $\\,1.999\\,$ | $\\,2.388\\,$ | $\\,2.657\\,$ |\n| $63$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $1.669$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.387\\,$ | $\\,2.656\\,$ |\n| $64$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.386\\,$ | $\\,2.655\\,$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37.15 & \\pm 1.669\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{64}}\\text{ dollars} \\\\ 37.15 & \\pm 0.417\\text{ dollars}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-780401c4-b210-558d-b3c0-2045c484e1c6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-780401c4-b210-558d-b3c0-2045c484e1c6" :answer-field/id #uuid "780401c4-b210-558d-b3c0-2045c484e1c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-07cd20ee-aac1-5423-b371-9c47c16002c1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f58221a-5581-5cd8-b941-e7019e00303f" "answer-edbb0ce9-191b-52b5-9c96-2d993645ed7b" "answer-07cd20ee-aac1-5423-b371-9c47c16002c1" "answer-e1c73524-2cab-5905-94b7-f488fb83a72a" "answer-a23739fa-8c2c-585d-a041-01f577921abb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f58221a-5581-5cd8-b941-e7019e00303f" :answer/id #uuid "7f58221a-5581-5cd8-b941-e7019e00303f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pm 0.209\\text{ dollars}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edbb0ce9-191b-52b5-9c96-2d993645ed7b" :answer/id #uuid "edbb0ce9-191b-52b5-9c96-2d993645ed7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "37.15\\pm 0.209\\text{ dollars}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07cd20ee-aac1-5423-b371-9c47c16002c1" :answer/id #uuid "07cd20ee-aac1-5423-b371-9c47c16002c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "37.15\\pm 0.417\\text{ dollars}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1c73524-2cab-5905-94b7-f488fb83a72a" :answer/id #uuid "e1c73524-2cab-5905-94b7-f488fb83a72a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "37.15\\pm 0.834\\text{ dollars}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a23739fa-8c2c-585d-a041-01f577921abb" :answer/id #uuid "a23739fa-8c2c-585d-a041-01f577921abb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64\\pm 0.417\\text{ dollars}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189369 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a normal population with unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Recall that a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the sample mean, $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nOne thing to realize is that this situation is unrealistic: it's doubtful that we'll not know the population mean yet know the population variance.\n\nA more realistic situation is that $\\sigma^2$ is *unknown*, and we must estimate it using the sample data. To estimate the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ we use the sample variance $S^2,$ given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n} \\sim T_{n-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $T_{n-1}$ is a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189395 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=9$ drawn from a normal population. The sample mean is $\\overline{x}=30,$ and the sample standard deviation is $s=3.3.$ \nWe wish to compute a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nRecall that a confidence interval is an interval of the form \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-E, \\: \\overline{x}+E),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean, and\n\n- $E$ is the margin of error.\n\nIn our example, the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is also normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\left(\\mu,\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nBy transforming $\\overline{X}$ in the manner described earlier, we have that the random variable $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n},\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nWe wish to find a $t$-interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $T$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/63/63973d7f59a993d42b7b8145f3354e9e7d7dba3c0393c756b6a95754eb4ead2e.png)\n\n  \n\nSince we wish to find a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we define our parameter $\\alpha$ as \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ is precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as $\\pm t_{n-1, \\alpha/2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/b5/b557afdbd649aa159addd9fe01021a7c3561f94b0168fd874876980d8bb7c3f4.png)\n\n  \n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{8,0.025}) = P(T < -t_{8,0.025}) = 0.025.\n$$\n\n  \n\nUsing a table of values for the $t$-distribution, we find that $t_{8,0.025} \\approx 2.306.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(T > 2.306) = P(T < -2.306) = 0.025.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.306<T<2.306)=0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is a $95\\%$ probability that our random variable $T$ will lie in the interval $(-2.306, 2.306).$\n\nSimilar to the case where $\\sigma^2$ was known, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population mean $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2.306 & <T<2.306 \\\\ -2.306 & <\\frac{\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu }{s/\\sqrt{n}}<2.306 \\\\ -2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & <\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu <2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ -\\overset{―}{x}-2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & <-\\mu <-\\overset{―}{x}+2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}+2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & >\\mu >\\overset{―}{x}-2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}-2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & <\\mu <\\overset{―}{x}+2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x}-2.306 \\cdot\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x}+2.306 \\cdot\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}  \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n$$\nn=9, \\qquad\n\\overline{x}=30, \\qquad\ns=3.3,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that our $95\\%$ confidence interval for $\\mu$ is \n\n$$\n\\big( 30- 2.537, \\: 30 + 2.537 \\big).\n$$\n\n \n\nDescribing this confidence interval using confidence limits, we have\n\n$$\n30 \\pm 2.537.\n$$\n\nLet's now describe the general procedure." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189370 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and sample variance $s^2.$ Then, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} +t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T > t_{n-1, \\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $T \\sim T_{n-1}.$ \n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are called **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean\n\n- $\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the standard error of the mean\n\n- $t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $t$-score\n\n- $E = t_{n-1, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/10/10cd4a425a9668611335a7e80561944d147faaa9152f91fb9ed87378e8c092eb.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{[math]t[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{[math]t[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}, & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression for the confidence interval is very similar to when the population variance was known. The only difference is that we now use the $t$-scores instead of $z$-scores and the sample standard deviation $s$ instead of the population standard deviation $\\sigma.$\n\nFinally, since computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189394 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've assumed that the underlying population is normally distributed. However, if we have a *sufficiently large* sample, then according to the central limit theorem, we have the approximation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\approx N(0,1).\n$$\n\nMoreover, since $n$ is sufficiently large, $\\sigma^2$ can be approximated as $S^2.$ Therefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n} \\approx T_{n-1}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the population is *not* normally distributed, then the sample size $n$ must be sufficiently large for this approximation to apply. Typically, $n\\geq 30$ is sufficient.\n\nIn such situations, we can use our formula for the confidence limits of the population mean $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188450 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101009 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d734e86c-2f4c-5a69-bdfa-2077440bd884"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d734e86c-2f4c-5a69-bdfa-2077440bd884" :question/id #uuid "d734e86c-2f4c-5a69-bdfa-2077440bd884" :question/math-academy-id "q-44192" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbf33e6d3a9e1efb4d0217f9076b0eb1381ea7d7fa9efa7ef7759b1c0e20175c.png)\n\nThe line shown above passes through the points $(0,3)$ and $(2,0).$ What is the equation of the line?" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,3).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=3.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,3)$ and $(2,0),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-3}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and the $y$-intercept $b=3$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-98f6c907-31fa-5398-b9b9-cd18dddbc8ab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-98f6c907-31fa-5398-b9b9-cd18dddbc8ab" :answer-field/id #uuid "98f6c907-31fa-5398-b9b9-cd18dddbc8ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d5c65083-63b9-5502-80e5-5b8c67db5cc3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d4d94deb-f953-5c29-852d-1230348acbe6" "answer-4b074387-ea1b-58a9-8455-29e4ebdf026b" "answer-540d2aca-caaa-5a05-b71c-086789c0ec35" "answer-d5c65083-63b9-5502-80e5-5b8c67db5cc3" "answer-a68bbf07-cefa-5dff-9f31-335abf2135e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4d94deb-f953-5c29-852d-1230348acbe6" :answer/id #uuid "d4d94deb-f953-5c29-852d-1230348acbe6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b074387-ea1b-58a9-8455-29e4ebdf026b" :answer/id #uuid "4b074387-ea1b-58a9-8455-29e4ebdf026b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3x+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-540d2aca-caaa-5a05-b71c-086789c0ec35" :answer/id #uuid "540d2aca-caaa-5a05-b71c-086789c0ec35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5c65083-63b9-5502-80e5-5b8c67db5cc3" :answer/id #uuid "d5c65083-63b9-5502-80e5-5b8c67db5cc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a68bbf07-cefa-5dff-9f31-335abf2135e0" :answer/id #uuid "a68bbf07-cefa-5dff-9f31-335abf2135e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101258 :question/problem "![](images/05/051c906be77d7e12a778449883a630c3b24f92b84ae7dac3d93b1787824f9c0c.png)\n\nConsider the line shown above, which has a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$ The equation of the line is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ We know that the slope is $m=\\frac{2}{3},$ so we just need to work out $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line passes through the point $(3,3).$ Substituting this into $y=mx+b$ with $m=\\frac{2}{3}$ and solving for $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+b \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{2}{3}(3)+b \\\\ 3 & =2+b \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186098879 :answer-field/correct "answer-155d28bd-5a1b-53d1-a062-ce10c04fe049" :answer-field/choices ["answer-155d28bd-5a1b-53d1-a062-ce10c04fe049"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-155d28bd-5a1b-53d1-a062-ce10c04fe049" :answer/id #uuid "155d28bd-5a1b-53d1-a062-ce10c04fe049" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101010 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9340bec6-b3d6-57e0-89a1-d4e7fb7357f7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9340bec6-b3d6-57e0-89a1-d4e7fb7357f7" :question/id #uuid "9340bec6-b3d6-57e0-89a1-d4e7fb7357f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-179508" :question/problem "Written in the form $y=mx+b,$ what is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(6,-2)$ and $(0,2)?$\n\nThe equation of the line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-(-2)}{0-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+2}{0-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,2),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=2.$  \n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and the $y$-intercept $b=2$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55a4f4df-f97d-5fe3-9f2f-2745efb5c93c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55a4f4df-f97d-5fe3-9f2f-2745efb5c93c" :answer-field/id #uuid "55a4f4df-f97d-5fe3-9f2f-2745efb5c93c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0c85bc99-52ec-513c-89c2-e3beaf6434b6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c85bc99-52ec-513c-89c2-e3beaf6434b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c85bc99-52ec-513c-89c2-e3beaf6434b6" :answer/id #uuid "0c85bc99-52ec-513c-89c2-e3beaf6434b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}x+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-1f1e2e98-3f29-5a8d-b619-48f3bdf693dc" :activity/id #uuid "1f1e2e98-3f29-5a8d-b619-48f3bdf693dc" :activity/title "Equations of Lines in Slope-Intercept Form" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049716 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-07cd0f42-e686-5aa2-b3b0-7e07e394a2c4" "step-0c73f501-a6b3-5d8e-af7b-d2238fefcc83" "step-febf91cb-77cd-5e1e-bba8-c5ccf63dd284"] :activity/first-step "step-07cd0f42-e686-5aa2-b3b0-7e07e394a2c4" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-07cd0f42-e686-5aa2-b3b0-7e07e394a2c4" :step/id #uuid "07cd0f42-e686-5aa2-b3b0-7e07e394a2c4" :step/content "question-d734e86c-2f4c-5a69-bdfa-2077440bd884" :step/next "step-0c73f501-a6b3-5d8e-af7b-d2238fefcc83" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0c73f501-a6b3-5d8e-af7b-d2238fefcc83" :step/id #uuid "0c73f501-a6b3-5d8e-af7b-d2238fefcc83" :step/content 17592186101258 :step/next "step-febf91cb-77cd-5e1e-bba8-c5ccf63dd284" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-febf91cb-77cd-5e1e-bba8-c5ccf63dd284" :step/id #uuid "febf91cb-77cd-5e1e-bba8-c5ccf63dd284" :step/content "question-9340bec6-b3d6-57e0-89a1-d4e7fb7357f7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284631 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284641 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -27 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(81-\\lambda )-729 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-90\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -90) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=90.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284646 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=4$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{4},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -8 \\\\ -16 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}32 & -32 \\\\ -32 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}32-\\lambda  & -32 \\\\ -32 & -16-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (32-\\lambda )(-16-\\lambda )-1\\,024 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda -1\\,536 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda +32) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=48.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-6d08b23d-f09b-57ac-8094-5a3a4b9cb9fe" :activity/id #uuid "6d08b23d-f09b-57ac-8094-5a3a4b9cb9fe" :activity/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms: Determining Where Extrema are Attained" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048933 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-ac5650ea-7fcf-5017-b806-6806b8006acb" "step-203e3740-ec3e-5f05-bb23-784b804df740" "step-592400bd-60ae-541a-a9a4-3378b493d7a4" "step-2d9889da-3200-5318-891b-ae6984507147" "step-7bf3d4cf-e223-589d-aaf4-04a241fecd3c"] :activity/first-step "step-ac5650ea-7fcf-5017-b806-6806b8006acb" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-ac5650ea-7fcf-5017-b806-6806b8006acb" :step/id #uuid "ac5650ea-7fcf-5017-b806-6806b8006acb" :step/content 17592186269176 :step/next "step-203e3740-ec3e-5f05-bb23-784b804df740" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-203e3740-ec3e-5f05-bb23-784b804df740" :step/id #uuid "203e3740-ec3e-5f05-bb23-784b804df740" :step/content 17592186284631 :step/next "step-592400bd-60ae-541a-a9a4-3378b493d7a4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-592400bd-60ae-541a-a9a4-3378b493d7a4" :step/id #uuid "592400bd-60ae-541a-a9a4-3378b493d7a4" :step/content 17592186284641 :step/next "step-2d9889da-3200-5318-891b-ae6984507147" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2d9889da-3200-5318-891b-ae6984507147" :step/id #uuid "2d9889da-3200-5318-891b-ae6984507147" :step/content 17592186284645 :step/next "step-7bf3d4cf-e223-589d-aaf4-04a241fecd3c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7bf3d4cf-e223-589d-aaf4-04a241fecd3c" :step/id #uuid "7bf3d4cf-e223-589d-aaf4-04a241fecd3c" :step/content 17592186284646 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186084752 :answer-field/choices 17592186084753] [:db/retract 17592186084752 :answer-field/choices 17592186084754] [:db/retract 17592186084752 :answer-field/choices 17592186084755] [:db/retract 17592186084752 :answer-field/choices 17592186084756] [:db/retract 17592186084752 :answer-field/choices 17592186084757] {:db/id 17592186087686 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-26dd17ae-021a-54b3-9443-27a8619f8c0b" "question-f9e5b1d4-9811-5c7c-998e-01070d311e7e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087433 :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=cx+7$ is $c.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=5x-3$ is $5.$\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $c=5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087687 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b444fd71-00fa-57c3-8df1-8666ae2725d8" "question-9815706a-98d4-5baf-aff8-15181269835f" "question-63caf14b-4a45-5fc8-9446-996c19cf7a2b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087434 :question/problem "Which of the following lines is parallel to $12x - y=3?$\n\nI. $y=4x-8$\n\nII. $y=12x+1$\n\nIII. $y=3x-12$" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $12x-y=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-y & =3 \\\\ -y & =-12x+3 \\\\ y & =12x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $12.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $12$ is $y = 12x+1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087688 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2f90abbd-1fee-558b-aa1d-be0b1bb7d5ca" "question-0a195cf2-04b2-54a7-a4cb-7f79ce0266f0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087435 :question/problem "The lines $y=2x$ and $kx-2y=-1$ are parallel. What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y=2x$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $2.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx-2y=-1,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-2y & =-1 \\\\ -2y & =-kx-1 \\\\ 2y & =kx+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{2}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $\\dfrac{k}{2}=2.$ Consequently, $k=4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-26dd17ae-021a-54b3-9443-27a8619f8c0b" :question/id #uuid "26dd17ae-021a-54b3-9443-27a8619f8c0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-215722" :question/problem "The lines $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4$ and $y=mx+2$ are parallel.  What is the value of $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4$ is $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=mx+2$ is $m.$\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f09cc482-8990-5f8f-aec3-23bc08a4425c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f09cc482-8990-5f8f-aec3-23bc08a4425c" :answer-field/id #uuid "f09cc482-8990-5f8f-aec3-23bc08a4425c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-73f0fb36-6abf-5a5a-bfdf-05576227c4f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-73f0fb36-6abf-5a5a-bfdf-05576227c4f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73f0fb36-6abf-5a5a-bfdf-05576227c4f3" :answer/id #uuid "73f0fb36-6abf-5a5a-bfdf-05576227c4f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f9e5b1d4-9811-5c7c-998e-01070d311e7e" :question/id #uuid "f9e5b1d4-9811-5c7c-998e-01070d311e7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-44391" :question/problem "The lines $y=\\frac{8}{5}x+9$ and $y=mx+3$ are parallel. The value of $m=$" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{8}{5}x+9$ is $\\frac{8}{5}.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=mx+3$ is $m.$\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{8}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c00507c7-749d-533c-becb-5a6fefcc307c"] :db/ensure [:question/v