wer-f1b5fe75-82cb-53ca-9624-82b3c3a6b868" :answer/id #uuid "f1b5fe75-82cb-53ca-9624-82b3c3a6b868" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61fe2478-10d2-5bca-920d-9f0daaf0b6ca" :answer/id #uuid "61fe2478-10d2-5bca-920d-9f0daaf0b6ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=4x+8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-63caf14b-4a45-5fc8-9446-996c19cf7a2b" :question/id #uuid "63caf14b-4a45-5fc8-9446-996c19cf7a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-28288" :question/problem "A line $l$ is parallel to $4x-2y=6.$ A possible equation for $l$ is" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $4x-2y=6,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =-4x+6 \\\\ y & =2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $2.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $2$ is $y=2x+6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15dedf20-d4e5-5216-862b-d27b1914301a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15dedf20-d4e5-5216-862b-d27b1914301a" :answer-field/id #uuid "15dedf20-d4e5-5216-862b-d27b1914301a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a82da13f-ae7d-59c1-a9b0-c02ec5938b43" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ec81f36b-a032-5f51-afa7-bba1b6434ad6" "answer-341534a0-7fae-50cc-ab8a-3b36904892d2" "answer-a82da13f-ae7d-59c1-a9b0-c02ec5938b43" "answer-e8390c49-841d-5569-afdf-c7be1ff51f02" "answer-124a7bb7-ae6e-503b-b2b6-2d040b8f853d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec81f36b-a032-5f51-afa7-bba1b6434ad6" :answer/id #uuid "ec81f36b-a032-5f51-afa7-bba1b6434ad6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3x-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-341534a0-7fae-50cc-ab8a-3b36904892d2" :answer/id #uuid "341534a0-7fae-50cc-ab8a-3b36904892d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-x+8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a82da13f-ae7d-59c1-a9b0-c02ec5938b43" :answer/id #uuid "a82da13f-ae7d-59c1-a9b0-c02ec5938b43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8390c49-841d-5569-afdf-c7be1ff51f02" :answer/id #uuid "e8390c49-841d-5569-afdf-c7be1ff51f02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=4x-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-124a7bb7-ae6e-503b-b2b6-2d040b8f853d" :answer/id #uuid "124a7bb7-ae6e-503b-b2b6-2d040b8f853d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=6x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088003 :question/problem "Which of the following lines is parallel to $8x-y=2?$" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $8x-y=2,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-y & =2 \\\\ -y & =-8x+2 \\\\ y & =8x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $8.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $8$ is $y=8x-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084752 :answer-field/correct "answer-5b7d9d0a-4938-5758-bef7-3f54c308a828" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c1ce75d-066c-56fa-a447-9bc0e4fb0ecf" "answer-a0ba89fb-60f7-5fc6-a5b8-1badc793089e" "answer-da3addca-0b1d-53d7-9827-4676fc539386" "answer-b4a7c51a-8f42-5ba3-bd46-8379e75ac83f" "answer-5b7d9d0a-4938-5758-bef7-3f54c308a828"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c1ce75d-066c-56fa-a447-9bc0e4fb0ecf" :answer/id #uuid "5c1ce75d-066c-56fa-a447-9bc0e4fb0ecf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2x+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0ba89fb-60f7-5fc6-a5b8-1badc793089e" :answer/id #uuid "a0ba89fb-60f7-5fc6-a5b8-1badc793089e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da3addca-0b1d-53d7-9827-4676fc539386" :answer/id #uuid "da3addca-0b1d-53d7-9827-4676fc539386" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=16x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4a7c51a-8f42-5ba3-bd46-8379e75ac83f" :answer/id #uuid "b4a7c51a-8f42-5ba3-bd46-8379e75ac83f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b7d9d0a-4938-5758-bef7-3f54c308a828" :answer/id #uuid "5b7d9d0a-4938-5758-bef7-3f54c308a828" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=8x-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088068 :question/problem "The lines $8x-2y=-2$ and $kx-5y=15$ are parallel. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $8x-2y=-2,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-2y & =-2 \\\\ -2y & =-8x-2 \\\\ 2y & =8x+2 \\\\ y & =4x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $4.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx-5y=15,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-5y & =15 \\\\ -5y & =-kx+15 \\\\ 5y & =kx-15 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{5}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\frac{k}{5}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $\\frac{k}{5}=4.$ We solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{5} & =4 \\\\ k & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186085129 :answer-field/correct "answer-67310dbd-b4ba-5314-aa75-e34ad505fc34" :answer-field/choices ["answer-67310dbd-b4ba-5314-aa75-e34ad505fc34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67310dbd-b4ba-5314-aa75-e34ad505fc34" :answer/id #uuid "67310dbd-b4ba-5314-aa75-e34ad505fc34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f90abbd-1fee-558b-aa1d-be0b1bb7d5ca" :question/id #uuid "2f90abbd-1fee-558b-aa1d-be0b1bb7d5ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-110102" :question/problem "For what value of $k$ are the lines $2x-4y=8$ and $kx+2y=12$ parallel?" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $2x-4y=8,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y & =8 \\\\ -4y & =-2x+8 \\\\ 4y & =2x-8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx+2y=12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx+2y & =12 \\\\ 2y & =-kx+12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{k}{2}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\frac{k}{2}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $-\\frac{k}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ We solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e2cbc20-a3be-526b-a577-9ccc1fc2af26"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e2cbc20-a3be-526b-a577-9ccc1fc2af26" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e2cbc20-a3be-526b-a577-9ccc1fc2af26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3d5ed668-45b9-5e68-aacd-a1d7b77b0d34" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d5ed668-45b9-5e68-aacd-a1d7b77b0d34" "answer-6a194ac4-0015-5288-8e78-47cdee62ba55" "answer-35eec1dd-4c49-50dd-9f3d-b76b51f36587" "answer-a7c2211f-deb4-54e0-a62a-93580ba3e9eb" "answer-68fe6cc0-1600-5a47-9f88-95202793571d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d5ed668-45b9-5e68-aacd-a1d7b77b0d34" :answer/id #uuid "3d5ed668-45b9-5e68-aacd-a1d7b77b0d34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a194ac4-0015-5288-8e78-47cdee62ba55" :answer/id #uuid "6a194ac4-0015-5288-8e78-47cdee62ba55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35eec1dd-4c49-50dd-9f3d-b76b51f36587" :answer/id #uuid "35eec1dd-4c49-50dd-9f3d-b76b51f36587" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7c2211f-deb4-54e0-a62a-93580ba3e9eb" :answer/id #uuid "a7c2211f-deb4-54e0-a62a-93580ba3e9eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68fe6cc0-1600-5a47-9f88-95202793571d" :answer/id #uuid "68fe6cc0-1600-5a47-9f88-95202793571d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0a195cf2-04b2-54a7-a4cb-7f79ce0266f0" :question/id #uuid "0a195cf2-04b2-54a7-a4cb-7f79ce0266f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-110095" :question/problem "The lines $y=-3x+1$ and $kx-2y=12$ are parallel. The value of $k=$" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y=-3x+1$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-3.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx-2y=12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-2y & =12 \\\\ -2y & =-kx+12 \\\\ 2y & =kx-12 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{2}x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\frac{k}{2}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $\\frac{k}{2}=-3.$ Consequently, $k=-6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-657d9ac1-f6be-595b-b713-0c7e495af6bf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-657d9ac1-f6be-595b-b713-0c7e495af6bf" :answer-field/id #uuid "657d9ac1-f6be-595b-b713-0c7e495af6bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cc3ee1a9-76b7-5435-96c4-77dce8a3e24a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7106821d-c7b4-548f-a75f-09d537e7d3d7" "answer-cc3ee1a9-76b7-5435-96c4-77dce8a3e24a" "answer-d7e707d3-9f9a-5e23-abbd-a7d263426dc6" "answer-6575d776-7f8b-5b97-9011-4e59f2270332" "answer-e90a4bce-59f2-5446-ae05-de09cf654ac9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7106821d-c7b4-548f-a75f-09d537e7d3d7" :answer/id #uuid "7106821d-c7b4-548f-a75f-09d537e7d3d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc3ee1a9-76b7-5435-96c4-77dce8a3e24a" :answer/id #uuid "cc3ee1a9-76b7-5435-96c4-77dce8a3e24a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7e707d3-9f9a-5e23-abbd-a7d263426dc6" :answer/id #uuid "d7e707d3-9f9a-5e23-abbd-a7d263426dc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6575d776-7f8b-5b97-9011-4e59f2270332" :answer/id #uuid "6575d776-7f8b-5b97-9011-4e59f2270332" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e90a4bce-59f2-5446-ae05-de09cf654ac9" :answer/id #uuid "e90a4bce-59f2-5446-ae05-de09cf654ac9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088989 :tutorial/content "Two distinct lines that never intersect are said to be **parallel.** For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope. \n\nFor example, the lines $y=2x+4$ and $y=2x-5$ have the same slope of $m=2,$ so they are parallel. Indeed, looking at the graph, we see that the two lines never cross.\n\n![](images/c7/c7d36372d9646d0ce10eff5d35a4f9b8e56a7b37b333c5d53ac243065ee751af.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the lines $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+4$ and $y=2x-5$ have different slopes, so they are *not* parallel. Indeed, looking at the graph, we see that the two lines *do* cross.\n\n![](images/92/92c1ee39ccbbbf20dc3f4a7f76a1114d975fb0826eeac13d273b4b4fd9a83105.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087910 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186113682 :answer-field/choices 17592186113683] [:db/retract 17592186113682 :answer-field/choices 17592186113684] [:db/retract 17592186113682 :answer-field/choices 17592186113685] [:db/retract 17592186113682 :answer-field/choices 17592186113686] [:db/retract 17592186113682 :answer-field/choices 17592186113687] {:db/id 17592186115373 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ee7a7310-6a9a-5bb5-9fe8-376a138abe62" "question-f1d959d4-e84b-537b-9d31-22a9bf3bca3a" "question-7ce0dbae-abd1-5d37-8894-76e5b590f727" "question-787955c7-f519-56e2-a7ff-67abb164ab3b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115374 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4779b0e3-7c68-54eb-98ad-ac554dad498b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115375 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9f051584-9c22-538b-be73-a90ad4399f9f" "question-ea65973e-51d0-5969-ad2d-3fe183a87ba6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ee7a7310-6a9a-5bb5-9fe8-376a138abe62" :question/id #uuid "ee7a7310-6a9a-5bb5-9fe8-376a138abe62" :question/math-academy-id "q-336490" :question/problem "A processing tank initially holds $750\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $900\\,\\text{g}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA solution containing $18\\,\\text{g/L}$ of dissolved sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of sugar $Q$ in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the mass of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $18\\,\\text{g/L}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =108\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $750\\,\\text{L}$ of water.\n\n- A solution enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $6t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed water solution leaves the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $6t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n750+6t-6t=750\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the water solution at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{125}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =108-\\frac{Q}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{125}Q=108.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7ae92825-baa7-50ef-a722-e714030d9c79"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7ae92825-baa7-50ef-a722-e714030d9c79" :answer-field/id #uuid "7ae92825-baa7-50ef-a722-e714030d9c79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cc3c4ce1-d24f-5575-8831-e677209a91ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cc3c4ce1-d24f-5575-8831-e677209a91ea" "answer-57ee86c4-0d10-514b-b039-aff2c42d25db" "answer-af3af680-805e-5037-96ce-f61a6f8c4eb4" "answer-2878ec78-2159-5e78-bce0-0d1fc6bda183" "answer-85f04d18-e494-5d62-84fa-11120096dd73"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc3c4ce1-d24f-5575-8831-e677209a91ea" :answer/id #uuid "cc3c4ce1-d24f-5575-8831-e677209a91ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{125}Q=108" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57ee86c4-0d10-514b-b039-aff2c42d25db" :answer/id #uuid "57ee86c4-0d10-514b-b039-aff2c42d25db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{125}Q=18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af3af680-805e-5037-96ce-f61a6f8c4eb4" :answer/id #uuid "af3af680-805e-5037-96ce-f61a6f8c4eb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{750}Q=108" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2878ec78-2159-5e78-bce0-0d1fc6bda183" :answer/id #uuid "2878ec78-2159-5e78-bce0-0d1fc6bda183" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{1}{125}Q=108" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85f04d18-e494-5d62-84fa-11120096dd73" :answer/id #uuid "85f04d18-e494-5d62-84fa-11120096dd73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{1}{750}Q=108" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f1d959d4-e84b-537b-9d31-22a9bf3bca3a" :question/id #uuid "f1d959d4-e84b-537b-9d31-22a9bf3bca3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336618" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $500\\,\\text{L}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt.\nA brine solution containing $0.12\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt per liter is poured into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of salt $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the brine begins to be added?" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.12\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.12\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.12\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =2.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $500\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Brine enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n500+20t-20t=500\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.4-\\frac{Q}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3cab02cb-f822-5c64-8397-0b6ad78c90d0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3cab02cb-f822-5c64-8397-0b6ad78c90d0" :answer-field/id #uuid "3cab02cb-f822-5c64-8397-0b6ad78c90d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46ba7316-4366-577b-b9c9-597098084ad0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6749d971-1ea4-5ebc-8ae4-5383ae7d993c" "answer-46ba7316-4366-577b-b9c9-597098084ad0" "answer-8e81d3f3-a95d-5bda-9070-b35e77fae07c" "answer-2c028762-bdd0-5cdd-bfee-795ad4f58f1a" "answer-9e610baf-df41-5215-9dbb-da365544c39b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6749d971-1ea4-5ebc-8ae4-5383ae7d993c" :answer/id #uuid "6749d971-1ea4-5ebc-8ae4-5383ae7d993c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46ba7316-4366-577b-b9c9-597098084ad0" :answer/id #uuid "46ba7316-4366-577b-b9c9-597098084ad0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=2.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e81d3f3-a95d-5bda-9070-b35e77fae07c" :answer/id #uuid "8e81d3f3-a95d-5bda-9070-b35e77fae07c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{30}Q=2.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c028762-bdd0-5cdd-bfee-795ad4f58f1a" :answer/id #uuid "2c028762-bdd0-5cdd-bfee-795ad4f58f1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{1}{25}Q=2.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e610baf-df41-5215-9dbb-da365544c39b" :answer/id #uuid "9e610baf-df41-5215-9dbb-da365544c39b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{1}{30}Q=2.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115790 :question/problem "A tank initially holds $450\\,\\text{L}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $21\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of salt $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water begins to be added?" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $21\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Fresh water enters the tank at a rate of $21\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $21t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $24t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450+21t-24t=(450-3t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{150-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{8Q}{150-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{8}{150-t}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186113682 :answer-field/correct "answer-593e6b0f-9429-58f4-ab8a-d54a13efac3d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a9bde946-8234-56ad-a449-9f98067585ea" "answer-8c6ba66c-65ae-5c2d-8bc4-666da8c2262c" "answer-593e6b0f-9429-58f4-ab8a-d54a13efac3d" "answer-d2eece5a-449f-5e71-af3d-ce4c9d2c638c" "answer-ff6cce44-bdf6-5fe5-84f8-e588ec42083d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9bde946-8234-56ad-a449-9f98067585ea" :answer/id #uuid "a9bde946-8234-56ad-a449-9f98067585ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{150-t}Q=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c6ba66c-65ae-5c2d-8bc4-666da8c2262c" :answer/id #uuid "8c6ba66c-65ae-5c2d-8bc4-666da8c2262c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{7}{150-t}Q=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-593e6b0f-9429-58f4-ab8a-d54a13efac3d" :answer/id #uuid "593e6b0f-9429-58f4-ab8a-d54a13efac3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{8}{150-t}Q=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2eece5a-449f-5e71-af3d-ce4c9d2c638c" :answer/id #uuid "d2eece5a-449f-5e71-af3d-ce4c9d2c638c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{7}{150-t}Q=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff6cce44-bdf6-5fe5-84f8-e588ec42083d" :answer/id #uuid "ff6cce44-bdf6-5fe5-84f8-e588ec42083d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{8}{150-t}Q=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7ce0dbae-abd1-5d37-8894-76e5b590f727" :question/id #uuid "7ce0dbae-abd1-5d37-8894-76e5b590f727" :question/math-academy-id "q-336551" :question/problem "A reactor initially holds $520\\,\\text{L}$ of a chemical solution containing $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of reactant.\nA pure solvent is poured into the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of reactant $Q,$ in kilograms, in the reactor $t$ minutes after the solvent begins to be added?" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that the incoming solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\n- The reactor initially contains $520\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Solvent enters the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the reactor.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the reactor.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n520+16t-20t=(520-4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of reactant in the reactor, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{130-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{5Q}{130-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{5}{130-t}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1662d401-dcb6-5bdc-a316-bdbd0f68ae58"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1662d401-dcb6-5bdc-a316-bdbd0f68ae58" :answer-field/id #uuid "1662d401-dcb6-5bdc-a316-bdbd0f68ae58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a5578ef5-8d19-5d4c-b66c-9c6ec6cca07e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-04471311-f4ef-59ad-9ec9-d63eb7e07d09" "answer-22f86d72-4bf1-5704-b4af-30c482c9cb28" "answer-a5578ef5-8d19-5d4c-b66c-9c6ec6cca07e" "answer-ccfeee8c-4609-5a2c-af46-82d30ea29879" "answer-7db2fb8e-6325-5f40-9417-fc0e3a01a5bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04471311-f4ef-59ad-9ec9-d63eb7e07d09" :answer/id #uuid "04471311-f4ef-59ad-9ec9-d63eb7e07d09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{130-t}Q=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22f86d72-4bf1-5704-b4af-30c482c9cb28" :answer/id #uuid "22f86d72-4bf1-5704-b4af-30c482c9cb28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{130-t}Q=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5578ef5-8d19-5d4c-b66c-9c6ec6cca07e" :answer/id #uuid "a5578ef5-8d19-5d4c-b66c-9c6ec6cca07e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{5}{130-t}Q=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccfeee8c-4609-5a2c-af46-82d30ea29879" :answer/id #uuid "ccfeee8c-4609-5a2c-af46-82d30ea29879" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{4}{130-t}Q=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7db2fb8e-6325-5f40-9417-fc0e3a01a5bc" :answer/id #uuid "7db2fb8e-6325-5f40-9417-fc0e3a01a5bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{5}{130-t}Q=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-787955c7-f519-56e2-a7ff-67abb164ab3b" :question/id #uuid "787955c7-f519-56e2-a7ff-67abb164ab3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336622" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $280\\,\\text{L}$ of a chemical solution containing $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved chemical.\nA chemical solution containing $0.20\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved chemical per liter is poured into the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of dissolved chemical $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added?" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dissolved chemical in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a chemical solution containing $0.20\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved chemical enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved chemical* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.20\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.20\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =3.2\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $280\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Chemical solution enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n280+16t-16t=280\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved chemical in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dissolved chemical* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{35}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =3.2-\\frac{2Q}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{35}Q=3.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-23c464d0-a940-5cbe-ac3b-db43ce37099c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-23c464d0-a940-5cbe-ac3b-db43ce37099c" :answer-field/id #uuid "23c464d0-a940-5cbe-ac3b-db43ce37099c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-520f62b8-d63d-5371-96d8-62c3e5df7765" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0227caa1-4c42-5eb8-ac85-3986c6a9d588" "answer-7b78dea8-b14f-5f74-a0d7-da6c0fe28734" "answer-520f62b8-d63d-5371-96d8-62c3e5df7765" "answer-43ec94b3-4df2-52b9-a2ad-f9deb82de611" "answer-2b05d523-77bc-5466-bb19-c3abe62adb5c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0227caa1-4c42-5eb8-ac85-3986c6a9d588" :answer/id #uuid "0227caa1-4c42-5eb8-ac85-3986c6a9d588" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=3.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b78dea8-b14f-5f74-a0d7-da6c0fe28734" :answer/id #uuid "7b78dea8-b14f-5f74-a0d7-da6c0fe28734" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{35}Q=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-520f62b8-d63d-5371-96d8-62c3e5df7765" :answer/id #uuid "520f62b8-d63d-5371-96d8-62c3e5df7765" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{35}Q=3.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43ec94b3-4df2-52b9-a2ad-f9deb82de611" :answer/id #uuid "43ec94b3-4df2-52b9-a2ad-f9deb82de611" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{1}{20}Q=3.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b05d523-77bc-5466-bb19-c3abe62adb5c" :answer/id #uuid "2b05d523-77bc-5466-bb19-c3abe62adb5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{2}{35}Q=3.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186374821 :answer-field/correct "answer-0468a81b-2239-5f53-84a9-7481ee0cfb4e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0468a81b-2239-5f53-84a9-7481ee0cfb4e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0468a81b-2239-5f53-84a9-7481ee0cfb4e" :answer/id #uuid "0468a81b-2239-5f53-84a9-7481ee0cfb4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18e^{-t/20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4779b0e3-7c68-54eb-98ad-ac554dad498b" :question/id #uuid "4779b0e3-7c68-54eb-98ad-ac554dad498b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336685" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $14\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt.\nFresh water is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of salt in the tank $20$ minutes after the fresh water pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that fresh water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; water enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and drains out at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n420+12t-12t=420\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{35}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{Q}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{1}{35}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{35} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{35})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{35}\\int 1\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{35}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-\\frac{t}{35}+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/35},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $14\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=14,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =14 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/35} & =14 \\\\ A & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=14{e}^{-t/35}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank $20$ minutes after the fresh water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(20)=14{e}^{-20/35}≈7.91\\,\\text{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-752081da-3806-5149-83db-ac2d9b472911"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-752081da-3806-5149-83db-ac2d9b472911" :answer-field/id #uuid "752081da-3806-5149-83db-ac2d9b472911" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-457c4add-95f2-5630-82a0-e8ce4e4d81a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6005b2ab-465f-5cd8-bb5d-d54c1eeed27d" "answer-d94e5053-03cf-5ba6-ada7-c621dbe03ed6" "answer-457c4add-95f2-5630-82a0-e8ce4e4d81a3" "answer-0064f3e5-7e46-560e-af4f-5f1bc6505542" "answer-b5ea94bf-a047-5c90-b7d0-f7b804cd2312"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6005b2ab-465f-5cd8-bb5d-d54c1eeed27d" :answer/id #uuid "6005b2ab-465f-5cd8-bb5d-d54c1eeed27d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.35\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d94e5053-03cf-5ba6-ada7-c621dbe03ed6" :answer/id #uuid "d94e5053-03cf-5ba6-ada7-c621dbe03ed6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.72\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-457c4add-95f2-5630-82a0-e8ce4e4d81a3" :answer/id #uuid "457c4add-95f2-5630-82a0-e8ce4e4d81a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.91\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0064f3e5-7e46-560e-af4f-5f1bc6505542" :answer/id #uuid "0064f3e5-7e46-560e-af4f-5f1bc6505542" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8.10\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5ea94bf-a047-5c90-b7d0-f7b804cd2312" :answer/id #uuid "b5ea94bf-a047-5c90-b7d0-f7b804cd2312" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8.48\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115791 :question/problem "A tank initially holds $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind an equation for the amount of salt $Q(t),$ in pounds, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water is first added.\n\n$Q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNote that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume of water in the tank remains $120\\,\\text{gal}.$\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{30}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{Q}{30} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{30} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{30})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{30}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-t/30+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/30},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/30} & =20 \\\\ A & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=20{e}^{-t/30}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186113688 :answer-field/correct "answer-5689f169-0965-553d-bf8e-a087afa36fe6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5689f169-0965-553d-bf8e-a087afa36fe6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5689f169-0965-553d-bf8e-a087afa36fe6" :answer/id #uuid "5689f169-0965-553d-bf8e-a087afa36fe6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20e^{-t/30}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9f051584-9c22-538b-be73-a90ad4399f9f" :question/id #uuid "9f051584-9c22-538b-be73-a90ad4399f9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336843" :question/problem "A $300\\,\\text{gal}$-capacity tank initially holds $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of water containing $45\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Find the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of water; clean water enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed water leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=(180+2t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{90+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{90+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{90+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(90+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $45\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=45,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =45 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(90+0)^{2}} & =45 \\\\ A & =45(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{45(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{90}{90+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 180+2{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =45{(\\frac{90}{90+60})}^{2} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{90}{150})}^{2} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =16.20\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-396eb760-1ba4-5c61-a4fd-b36963318cca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-396eb760-1ba4-5c61-a4fd-b36963318cca" :answer-field/id #uuid "396eb760-1ba4-5c61-a4fd-b36963318cca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1fd464ab-03dd-5c29-a547-e17631549a84" :answer-field/choices ["answer-91dfca2e-8991-5e9e-966d-e682738680c3" "answer-41e819ff-cd32-5d60-ba16-54665c890b1d" "answer-1fd464ab-03dd-5c29-a547-e17631549a84" "answer-8ba3054a-6459-569b-81c3-907904cb6174" "answer-92fa06dd-98f7-529e-8d06-b10b01e3a805"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91dfca2e-8991-5e9e-966d-e682738680c3" :answer/id #uuid "91dfca2e-8991-5e9e-966d-e682738680c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15.12\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41e819ff-cd32-5d60-ba16-54665c890b1d" :answer/id #uuid "41e819ff-cd32-5d60-ba16-54665c890b1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15.84\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fd464ab-03dd-5c29-a547-e17631549a84" :answer/id #uuid "1fd464ab-03dd-5c29-a547-e17631549a84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16.20\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ba3054a-6459-569b-81c3-907904cb6174" :answer/id #uuid "8ba3054a-6459-569b-81c3-907904cb6174" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16.78\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92fa06dd-98f7-529e-8d06-b10b01e3a805" :answer/id #uuid "92fa06dd-98f7-529e-8d06-b10b01e3a805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17.05\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ea65973e-51d0-5969-ad2d-3fe183a87ba6" :question/id #uuid "ea65973e-51d0-5969-ad2d-3fe183a87ba6" :question/math-academy-id "q-336840" :question/problem "A $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity water tank initially holds $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water containing $210\\,\\text{g}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,200+90t-60t=30(240+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{240+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{240+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{240+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{240+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|240+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(240+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $210\\,\\text{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=210,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =210 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(240+0)^{2}} & =210 \\\\ A & =210(240)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{210(240)^{2}}{(240+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =210{(\\frac{240}{240+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 30(240+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 240+{t}_{o} & =350 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =110\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(110) & =210{(\\frac{240}{240+110})}^{2} \\\\  & =210{(\\frac{24}{35})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈98.74\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4dc2b09f-c2d7-5d6c-8b4d-48c2f11ae6c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4dc2b09f-c2d7-5d6c-8b4d-48c2f11ae6c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "4dc2b09f-c2d7-5d6c-8b4d-48c2f11ae6c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-78a9c22a-d2cf-5556-83bc-973ce5081e81" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f405b4b6-a9a5-5b98-9592-9a6a03980d43" "answer-db9870bb-ca97-5434-9e2f-f0f1821c5287" "answer-af336e1d-577f-5f06-a6fb-d40be71dd596" "answer-dc627a42-82c5-5373-81cf-850a6f244c32" "answer-78a9c22a-d2cf-5556-83bc-973ce5081e81"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f405b4b6-a9a5-5b98-9592-9a6a03980d43" :answer/id #uuid "f405b4b6-a9a5-5b98-9592-9a6a03980d43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100.65\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db9870bb-ca97-5434-9e2f-f0f1821c5287" :answer/id #uuid "db9870bb-ca97-5434-9e2f-f0f1821c5287" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "102.12\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af336e1d-577f-5f06-a6fb-d40be71dd596" :answer/id #uuid "af336e1d-577f-5f06-a6fb-d40be71dd596" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "94.90\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc627a42-82c5-5373-81cf-850a6f244c32" :answer/id #uuid "dc627a42-82c5-5373-81cf-850a6f244c32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "96.83\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78a9c22a-d2cf-5556-83bc-973ce5081e81" :answer/id #uuid "78a9c22a-d2cf-5556-83bc-973ce5081e81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "98.74\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116425 :tutorial/content "Consider a container which initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance.\n\n- Another solution, containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of the substance per gallon, flows *into* the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$\n\n- At the same time, the completely mixed solution flows *out* of the container at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min}.$\n\n![](images/31/312d11bb0a00f6f668b653b00a7eecda55cdb23c5a7c09076734774b7c05a50b.png)\n\nHow can we model the amount $Q$ (in pounds) of substance in the container at time $t?$\n\nTo model $Q(t),$ we can analyze how it changes over time.\nThe rate of change of the amount of substance in the container is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out},\n$$\n\n where:\n\n- $Q'_\\text{in}$ is the rate at which the substance *enters* the container. A solution containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ of the substance enters the container at a rate of $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =be\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $Q'_\\text{out}$ is the rate at which the substance *leaves* the container. To determine the rate of exit, we first find the volume of solution in the container at time $t.$\n\nStarting from the initial volume $V_0,$ we add the volume that enters, $et,$ and subtract the volume that leaves, $ft.$ Thus, the volume of solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n(V_0 + et - ft)\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of substance in the container, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\n So, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ the substance leaves the container at the rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(f\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(f\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft})\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = be - f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right) = be}\n$$\n\nLet's see this in a more concrete example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116426 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant.\nA solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nLet's find a differential equation that describes the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dissolved contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nrepresents the rate at which this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of dissolved contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Solution enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450 + 8t - 8t = 450\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved contaminant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{450}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *dissolved contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{225}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{4Q}{225}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{225}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116428 :tutorial/content "So far, we have seen that if a container initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance, and\n\n- a solution with concentration $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ flows into the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ while\n\n- the completely mixed solution flows out at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min},$\n\nthen the amount $Q(t)$ of the substance in the container satisfies the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\\right) = be.\n$$\n\nFor the rest of this lesson, we'll focus on the special case where the input solution contains *no substance*, that is, where $b=0.$ In this case, the right-hand side vanishes, and the differential equation becomes *separable*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\\right) = 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac1Q\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = - \\dfrac{f}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is negative, meaning the amount of substance in the container decreases over time. We can now solve the differential equation directly by separation of variables.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider our previous example: a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant, and a solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nWe found that the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank after $t$ minutes is modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{4}{225}Q = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{1}{Q} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{4}{225}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by integrating both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}\\,dt & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{225})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\,dQ & =-\\frac{4}{225}\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{4t}{225}+C \\\\ Q & ={e}^{-4t/225+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-4t/225}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nFinally, since the tank initially contains $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-4(0)/225} & =6 \\\\ A & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{Q(t) = 6e^{-4t/225}}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116427 :tutorial/content "Previously, we saw that the volume $V$ of solution at time $t$ in a container with initial volume $V_0,$ input rate $e,$ and output rate $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0 + (e - f)t.\n$$\n\nThere are three possible behaviors for the volume depending on the rates:\n\n- The volume *remains constant* when the input rate equals the output rate, $e=f.$ In this case, \n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0.\n$$\n\n- The volume *decreases* when the output rate is greater than the input rate, $f > e > 0,$ and the container begins to empty. Let $t_e$ be the time when the container empties: $V(t_e) = 0.$ Solving for $t_e,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{e}) & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e} \\\\ 0 & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the container empties at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_e = \\dfrac{V_0}{f - e}}.$\n\n- The volume *increases* when the input rate is greater than the output rate, $e > f > 0,$ and the container begins filling until it reaches its capacity $V_\\text{cap}.$ Let $t_o$ be the time when the container reaches capacity: $V(t_o) = V_\\text{cap}.$ Solving for $t_o,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & ={V}_{\\text{cap}} \\\\ {V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{o} & ={V}_{\\text{cap}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the container overflows at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_o = \\dfrac{V_\\text{cap} - V_0}{e - f}}.$\n\nNow, let's apply one of these formulas in a concrete example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115463 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "activity-bed47464-d195-5767-9ce4-9199db42db0b" :activity/id #uuid "bed47464-d195-5767-9ce4-9199db42db0b" :activity/title "Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix in the General Case" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049929 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-99e0e481-5439-58dc-8ccd-b79db8c29b1f" "step-ef95d7ac-f555-57b3-8f7a-1c43afc31919" "step-fa9eb203-df95-5fc2-b810-2b8d0e2ec533" "step-cdeac1bd-e04d-50c6-8cf8-c212fe8dd397" "step-beb9b581-1102-564c-88bd-42c46b17e24c"] :activity/first-step "step-99e0e481-5439-58dc-8ccd-b79db8c29b1f" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-99e0e481-5439-58dc-8ccd-b79db8c29b1f" :step/id #uuid "99e0e481-5439-58dc-8ccd-b79db8c29b1f" :step/content 17592186125060 :step/next "step-ef95d7ac-f555-57b3-8f7a-1c43afc31919" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ef95d7ac-f555-57b3-8f7a-1c43afc31919" :step/id #uuid "ef95d7ac-f555-57b3-8f7a-1c43afc31919" :step/content 17592186323928 :step/next "step-fa9eb203-df95-5fc2-b810-2b8d0e2ec533" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fa9eb203-df95-5fc2-b810-2b8d0e2ec533" :step/id #uuid "fa9eb203-df95-5fc2-b810-2b8d0e2ec533" :step/content 17592186360739 :step/next "step-cdeac1bd-e04d-50c6-8cf8-c212fe8dd397" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cdeac1bd-e04d-50c6-8cf8-c212fe8dd397" :step/id #uuid "cdeac1bd-e04d-50c6-8cf8-c212fe8dd397" :step/content 17592186360742 :step/next "step-beb9b581-1102-564c-88bd-42c46b17e24c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-beb9b581-1102-564c-88bd-42c46b17e24c" :step/id #uuid "beb9b581-1102-564c-88bd-42c46b17e24c" :step/content 17592186360743 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186092407 :activity/steps 17592186093000] [:db/retract 17592186092407 :activity/steps 17592186093001] [:db/retract 17592186092407 :activity/steps 17592186093178] [:db/retract 17592186092407 :activity/steps 17592186093008] [:db/retract 17592186093001 :step/next 17592186093000] [:db/retract 17592186093000 :step/next 17592186093178] [:db/retract 17592186093178 :step/next 17592186093008] {:db/id "knowledge-point-6d7b67c1-aa22-5106-9f09-19e361dc25c0" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d7b67c1-aa22-5106-9f09-19e361dc25c0" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-f100ae8f-6865-5741-a125-4d55e90e2e43" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b43654ae-4e3f-5d53-a4fb-91c5e392ca63" "question-62ed462d-094a-533c-8c38-e7a5c2d0177d"] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Multiplying Negative Numbers" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186050012 :topic/knowledge-points ["knowledge-point-6d7b67c1-aa22-5106-9f09-19e361dc25c0" "knowledge-point-aec0cd94-20e4-5748-b9c1-4de7b1854a8e" "knowledge-point-f589fffd-f5c4-590e-9ed1-01c20390700f" "knowledge-point-5b6c3c0b-b541-5b1f-810b-d9c4ffa1fe9c" "knowledge-point-6d7b67c1-aa22-5106-9f09-19e361dc25c0" "knowledge-point-6d7b67c1-aa22-5106-9f09-19e361dc25c0" "knowledge-point-aec0cd94-20e4-5748-b9c1-4de7b1854a8e" "knowledge-point-aec0cd94-20e4-5748-b9c1-4de7b1854a8e" "knowledge-point-f589fffd-f5c4-590e-9ed1-01c20390700f" "knowledge-point-f589fffd-f5c4-590e-9ed1-01c20390700f" "knowledge-point-5b6c3c0b-b541-5b1f-810b-d9c4ffa1fe9c" "knowledge-point-5b6c3c0b-b541-5b1f-810b-d9c4ffa1fe9c"] :topic/title "Solving Problems Using Multiplication and Division of Rational Numbers" :db/ensure [:topic/identity-validate]} {:db/id "question-f100ae8f-6865-5741-a125-4d55e90e2e43" :question/id #uuid "f100ae8f-6865-5741-a125-4d55e90e2e43" :question/math-academy-id "e-22713" :question/problem "A customer opens a bank account with a balance of ![](images/b6/b6d32477e84b569c63644d22eac5c4ac7628d3513fa649efe3b53af4dd46bd63.svg). The account allows overdrafts, and the customer withdraws ![](images/3f/3fb4b29875dcbc25b7a9836d939831543811af3f8b7959c34bbe2ef7c5739187.svg) each day for ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) days. What is the account balance after ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) days?" :question/worked-solution "The bank balance decreases by ![](images/3f/3fb4b29875dcbc25b7a9836d939831543811af3f8b7959c34bbe2ef7c5739187.svg) each day.\n\nSince the change is a decrease and occurs for ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) days, the total change can be written as repeated subtraction of ![](images/34/3474a36d6b0c9f191464fa9f990df13762af9a0c6b5e8138242a195cc33120e6.svg)\n\n![](images/f5/f5fbbe1aa003f39beaa1c8bbc83d1ba09f5588bb3e2a061287093e214f2ffb78.svg)\n\nRepeated subtraction can also be written using multiplication:\n\n![](images/4d/4db89374d470b00b0463b35e087efbb5ebfd8cbc0e0a408a9bfaf052cd9c5a32.svg)\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n![](images/0a/0a1462b31c3a16cb37e282f254ad252b061aff0534e9bdf5972144d7de7e5a8a.svg)\n\nTherefore, the account balance after ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) days is ![](images/27/27f2d0115060366cb914179f4e2e6e9b0f9ddb29a6530984a105d7a0f5f29f71.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "knowledge-point-aec0cd94-20e4-5748-b9c1-4de7b1854a8e" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aec0cd94-20e4-5748-b9c1-4de7b1854a8e" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-5f3cfae7-b7d6-56b2-a245-6ec44b76e462" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d71152b6-16b1-5e4e-a992-55dfd11f6205" "question-e8734cff-4115-50f2-9d56-00580f475192"] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Multiplying Negative Fractions" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5f3cfae7-b7d6-56b2-a245-6ec44b76e462" :question/id #uuid "5f3cfae7-b7d6-56b2-a245-6ec44b76e462" :question/math-academy-id "e-22714" :question/problem "The temperature of a liquid decreases by $\\dfrac{4}{5}\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ each minute for $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" :question/worked-solution "The temperature decreases by $\\dfrac{4}{5}\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\dfrac{4}{5}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2} \\times \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2} \\times \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)\n= -\\dfrac{3 \\times 4}{2 \\times 5}\n= -\\dfrac{12}{10}\n= -\\dfrac{6}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-\\dfrac{6}{5}\\,^\\circ\\text{C}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "knowledge-point-f589fffd-f5c4-590e-9ed1-01c20390700f" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f589fffd-f5c4-590e-9ed1-01c20390700f" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-7be163d0-2103-5467-9bba-a4090855cc8b" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7b837a54-b40c-5dbd-9c93-92d2c8b04271" "question-361640a2-f197-5749-a9e6-5d3176000185"] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Multiplying Negative Fractions and Mixed Numbers" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7be163d0-2103-5467-9bba-a4090855cc8b" :question/id #uuid "7be163d0-2103-5467-9bba-a4090855cc8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-22729" :question/problem "A submarine descends $1\\,\\dfrac{3}{8}\\,\\text{m}$ each minute from sea level. What is the submarine's elevation after $\\dfrac{8}{3}$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "The submarine descends $1\\,\\dfrac{3}{8}\\,\\text{m}$ each minute.\n\nSince the elevation decreases, the change each minute is $-1\\,\\dfrac{3}{8}.$\n\nThe descent lasts $\\dfrac{8}{3}$ minutes, so the total change in elevation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3} \\times \\left(-1\\,\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right).\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the mixed number as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n-1\\,\\dfrac{3}{8} = -\\dfrac{1\\times8 + 3}{8} = -\\dfrac{11}{8}\n$$\n\nSo the expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3} \\times \\left(-\\dfrac{11}{8}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3} \\times \\left(-\\dfrac{11}{8}\\right)\n= -\\dfrac{8 \\times 11}{3 \\times 8}\n= -\\dfrac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the submarine's elevation after $\\dfrac{8}{3}$ minutes is $-\\dfrac{11}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "knowledge-point-5b6c3c0b-b541-5b1f-810b-d9c4ffa1fe9c" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b6c3c0b-b541-5b1f-810b-d9c4ffa1fe9c" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-9c400da4-eba5-58aa-8ee5-a33269adfa2a" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e034c9b1-b463-56a2-afff-761ad637fb35" "question-cf763c67-fa75-5f72-8661-230a30b13c07"] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Dividing Negative Numbers" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9c400da4-eba5-58aa-8ee5-a33269adfa2a" :question/id #uuid "9c400da4-eba5-58aa-8ee5-a33269adfa2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-22728" :question/problem "Over the course of $6$ hours, the temperature of a liquid decreases by $9.6^\\circ\\text{C}$ in total. Assuming the temperature decreases at a constant rate, what is the change in temperature each hour?" :question/worked-solution "Since the temperature decreases by $9.6^\\circ\\text{C}$ over $6$ hours, the total change is $-9.6^\\circ\\text{C}.$\n\nThis change occurs evenly over $6$ hours, so we divide the total change by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n-9.6 \\div 6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-9.6 \\div 6 = -1.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature changes by $-1.6^\\circ\\text{C}$ each hour." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b43654ae-4e3f-5d53-a4fb-91c5e392ca63" :question/id #uuid "b43654ae-4e3f-5d53-a4fb-91c5e392ca63" :question/math-academy-id "q-357178" :question/problem "The temperature inside a freezer drops by $2.5{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ every hour for $4$ hours. What is the total change in temperature after $4$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "The temperature decreases by $2.5{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each hour.\n\nSince the change is a decrease and occurs for $4$ hours, the total change can be written as repeated subtraction of $2.5:$\n\n$$\n{{-\\,2.5-2.5-2.5-2.5}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nRepeated subtraction can also be written using multiplication:\n\n$$\n4\\times (-2.5)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n4\\times (-2.5)=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature after $4$ hours is $-10{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c409ab7a-8f68-58a9-b465-46b591b77113"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c409ab7a-8f68-58a9-b465-46b591b77113" :answer-field/id #uuid "c409ab7a-8f68-58a9-b465-46b591b77113" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ed396f58-48ed-5ba7-a6e4-ffec135a596a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed396f58-48ed-5ba7-a6e4-ffec135a596a" "answer-b492ba8a-773f-5ff9-8fc4-080ff2e1ee05" "answer-323f42d9-16a4-5acf-9cc5-8859e990fe96" "answer-3eeb4fae-9881-59af-88ed-061e86ee17f6" "answer-b6e6aa31-02dc-578b-9a36-596c23908306"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed396f58-48ed-5ba7-a6e4-ffec135a596a" :answer/id #uuid "ed396f58-48ed-5ba7-a6e4-ffec135a596a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b492ba8a-773f-5ff9-8fc4-080ff2e1ee05" :answer/id #uuid "b492ba8a-773f-5ff9-8fc4-080ff2e1ee05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12.5{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-323f42d9-16a4-5acf-9cc5-8859e990fe96" :answer/id #uuid "323f42d9-16a4-5acf-9cc5-8859e990fe96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6.5{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3eeb4fae-9881-59af-88ed-061e86ee17f6" :answer/id #uuid "3eeb4fae-9881-59af-88ed-061e86ee17f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6e6aa31-02dc-578b-9a36-596c23908306" :answer/id #uuid "b6e6aa31-02dc-578b-9a36-596c23908306" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.5{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-62ed462d-094a-533c-8c38-e7a5c2d0177d" :question/id #uuid "62ed462d-094a-533c-8c38-e7a5c2d0177d" :question/math-academy-id "q-357179" :question/problem "The temperature in a city decreases by $1.2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each hour for $5$ hours. What is the total change in temperature after $5$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "The temperature decreases by $1.2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each hour.\n\nSince the change is a decrease and occurs for $5$ hours, the total change can be written as repeated subtraction of $1.2:$\n\n$$\n{{-\\,1.2-1.2-1.2-1.2-1.2}_{⏟}}_{5\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nRepeated subtraction can also be written using multiplication:\n\n$$\n5\\times (-1.2)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n5\\times (-1.2)=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature after $5$ hours is $-6{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae5ea86b-1b58-58c4-94fc-072bc0079348"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae5ea86b-1b58-58c4-94fc-072bc0079348" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae5ea86b-1b58-58c4-94fc-072bc0079348" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4a594372-b35f-56e0-8a4d-c8bc0a7cac29" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd16380a-95d8-5088-91a0-1e14c23a1ed1" "answer-8dbd0a5d-1b3f-5e92-8b30-75467294492e" "answer-4a594372-b35f-56e0-8a4d-c8bc0a7cac29" "answer-540050c3-f925-5073-ba35-875e0a5410ba" "answer-9930447e-e336-59d4-8781-ef16c146a7ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd16380a-95d8-5088-91a0-1e14c23a1ed1" :answer/id #uuid "bd16380a-95d8-5088-91a0-1e14c23a1ed1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4.8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8dbd0a5d-1b3f-5e92-8b30-75467294492e" :answer/id #uuid "8dbd0a5d-1b3f-5e92-8b30-75467294492e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6.2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a594372-b35f-56e0-8a4d-c8bc0a7cac29" :answer/id #uuid "4a594372-b35f-56e0-8a4d-c8bc0a7cac29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-540050c3-f925-5073-ba35-875e0a5410ba" :answer/id #uuid "540050c3-f925-5073-ba35-875e0a5410ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9930447e-e336-59d4-8781-ef16c146a7ea" :answer/id #uuid "9930447e-e336-59d4-8781-ef16c146a7ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d71152b6-16b1-5e4e-a992-55dfd11f6205" :question/id #uuid "d71152b6-16b1-5e4e-a992-55dfd11f6205" :question/math-academy-id "q-357085" :question/problem "The temperature of a liquid decreases by $\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $\\frac{8}{3}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" :question/worked-solution "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $\\frac{8}{3}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\times (-\\frac{3}{4}).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\times (-\\frac{3}{4})=-\\frac{8\\times 3}{3\\times 4}=-\\frac{8}{4}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2536844b-ad4a-5ed6-bce8-3971a2720b4c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2536844b-ad4a-5ed6-bce8-3971a2720b4c" :answer-field/id #uuid "2536844b-ad4a-5ed6-bce8-3971a2720b4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f0c9430f-5d3d-51ad-9d23-0d3aa66de7ad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f0c9430f-5d3d-51ad-9d23-0d3aa66de7ad" "answer-cf62465d-ee5a-56f9-9205-cdbc67e9ed15" "answer-8297df33-3945-557a-8b12-47cba0ef0ebe" "answer-e3b02612-0eeb-5065-9a7a-a11e62b8557c" "answer-e9c706d3-0dd0-5ebe-87cc-56a68f59d32f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0c9430f-5d3d-51ad-9d23-0d3aa66de7ad" :answer/id #uuid "f0c9430f-5d3d-51ad-9d23-0d3aa66de7ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf62465d-ee5a-56f9-9205-cdbc67e9ed15" :answer/id #uuid "cf62465d-ee5a-56f9-9205-cdbc67e9ed15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8297df33-3945-557a-8b12-47cba0ef0ebe" :answer/id #uuid "8297df33-3945-557a-8b12-47cba0ef0ebe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3b02612-0eeb-5065-9a7a-a11e62b8557c" :answer/id #uuid "e3b02612-0eeb-5065-9a7a-a11e62b8557c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9c706d3-0dd0-5ebe-87cc-56a68f59d32f" :answer/id #uuid "e9c706d3-0dd0-5ebe-87cc-56a68f59d32f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e8734cff-4115-50f2-9d56-00580f475192" :question/id #uuid "e8734cff-4115-50f2-9d56-00580f475192" :question/math-academy-id "q-357194" :question/problem "A hot air balloon descends $\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{m}$ each second. What is the total change in altitude after $\\frac{9}{7}$ seconds?" :question/worked-solution "The hot air balloon descends $\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{m}$ each second.\n\nSince the altitude decreases, the change each second is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nThe descent lasts $\\frac{9}{7}$ seconds, so the total change in altitude is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{7}\\times (-\\frac{7}{3}).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{7}\\times (-\\frac{7}{3})=-\\frac{9\\times 7}{7\\times 3}=-\\frac{9}{3}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in altitude is $-3\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7180b989-67d5-5e43-9459-6d581302e067"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7180b989-67d5-5e43-9459-6d581302e067" :answer-field/id #uuid "7180b989-67d5-5e43-9459-6d581302e067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0792a188-0269-58cf-97bd-516b8fe89cfb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0792a188-0269-58cf-97bd-516b8fe89cfb" "answer-ca9bde92-27d8-57f7-b7da-25ad1a8a9ba1" "answer-57cf6cec-0ed9-5d2e-af3f-5ae7420f7f35" "answer-2881adf2-dc78-54da-838f-2275d55fed05" "answer-035b5582-aa17-529b-b101-df7971bd9920"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0792a188-0269-58cf-97bd-516b8fe89cfb" :answer/id #uuid "0792a188-0269-58cf-97bd-516b8fe89cfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca9bde92-27d8-57f7-b7da-25ad1a8a9ba1" :answer/id #uuid "ca9bde92-27d8-57f7-b7da-25ad1a8a9ba1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{20}{7}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57cf6cec-0ed9-5d2e-af3f-5ae7420f7f35" :answer/id #uuid "57cf6cec-0ed9-5d2e-af3f-5ae7420f7f35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{76}{21}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2881adf2-dc78-54da-838f-2275d55fed05" :answer/id #uuid "2881adf2-dc78-54da-838f-2275d55fed05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-035b5582-aa17-529b-b101-df7971bd9920" :answer/id #uuid "035b5582-aa17-529b-b101-df7971bd9920" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{76}{21}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7b837a54-b40c-5dbd-9c93-92d2c8b04271" :question/id #uuid "7b837a54-b40c-5dbd-9c93-92d2c8b04271" :question/math-academy-id "q-357241" :question/problem "The temperature of a chemical solution decreases by $\\frac{4}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" :question/worked-solution "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{4}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the mixed number as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 4+3}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}).\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the product:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5})=-\\frac{15\\times 4}{4\\times 5}=-\\frac{60}{20}=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-3{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae216ed6-c5e7-5d88-9e19-dcdfe886b809"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae216ed6-c5e7-5d88-9e19-dcdfe886b809" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae216ed6-c5e7-5d88-9e19-dcdfe886b809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a46483a5-c5e1-536a-a0d5-b280e918e22b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a46483a5-c5e1-536a-a0d5-b280e918e22b" "answer-2268a62f-af3b-5554-9392-93550ebd3f67" "answer-8075dcdc-4e30-5eab-9850-ab4d6e913258" "answer-ab74afde-81f9-5e89-9f65-02cca1c6532c" "answer-34f958da-773f-515f-abd1-d2b6edb53bc8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a46483a5-c5e1-536a-a0d5-b280e918e22b" :answer/id #uuid "a46483a5-c5e1-536a-a0d5-b280e918e22b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2268a62f-af3b-5554-9392-93550ebd3f67" :answer/id #uuid "2268a62f-af3b-5554-9392-93550ebd3f67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8075dcdc-4e30-5eab-9850-ab4d6e913258" :answer/id #uuid "8075dcdc-4e30-5eab-9850-ab4d6e913258" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{3}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab74afde-81f9-5e89-9f65-02cca1c6532c" :answer/id #uuid "ab74afde-81f9-5e89-9f65-02cca1c6532c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34f958da-773f-515f-abd1-d2b6edb53bc8" :answer/id #uuid "34f958da-773f-515f-abd1-d2b6edb53bc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-361640a2-f197-5749-a9e6-5d3176000185" :question/id #uuid "361640a2-f197-5749-a9e6-5d3176000185" :question/math-academy-id "q-357243" :question/problem "The temperature inside a freezer decreases by $\\frac{3}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" :question/worked-solution "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{3}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times (-\\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the mixed number as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1\\times 4+1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the product:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{3}{5})=-\\frac{5\\times 3}{4\\times 5}=-\\frac{15}{20}=-\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-14daa8e3-e1bb-5707-81d3-5bbf9b88123d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-14daa8e3-e1bb-5707-81d3-5bbf9b88123d" :answer-field/id #uuid "14daa8e3-e1bb-5707-81d3-5bbf9b88123d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a9b9e3db-75fc-500b-be46-121b7aace8d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a9b9e3db-75fc-500b-be46-121b7aace8d4" "answer-134d188d-b9c3-552f-b921-1183370fb0c5" "answer-05436460-8fd0-5eaf-8d02-d2f20514c78b" "answer-21a0e148-8730-5869-a663-8249c3f4a7ac" "answer-bbaec04f-5284-5518-89e1-55d6d0d563f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9b9e3db-75fc-500b-be46-121b7aace8d4" :answer/id #uuid "a9b9e3db-75fc-500b-be46-121b7aace8d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-134d188d-b9c3-552f-b921-1183370fb0c5" :answer/id #uuid "134d188d-b9c3-552f-b921-1183370fb0c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05436460-8fd0-5eaf-8d02-d2f20514c78b" :answer/id #uuid "05436460-8fd0-5eaf-8d02-d2f20514c78b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21a0e148-8730-5869-a663-8249c3f4a7ac" :answer/id #uuid "21a0e148-8730-5869-a663-8249c3f4a7ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbaec04f-5284-5518-89e1-55d6d0d563f8" :answer/id #uuid "bbaec04f-5284-5518-89e1-55d6d0d563f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e034c9b1-b463-56a2-afff-761ad637fb35" :question/id #uuid "e034c9b1-b463-56a2-afff-761ad637fb35" :question/math-academy-id "q-357210" :question/problem "A diver descends a total of $32.5\\,\\text{m}$ over $5$ minutes at a constant rate. What is the change in elevation each minute?" :question/worked-solution "Since the diver's elevation decreases by $32.5\\,\\text{m}$ over $5$ minutes, the total change is $-32.5\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nThis change occurs evenly over $5$ minutes, so we divide the total change by $5:$\n\n$$\n-32.5÷5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-32.5÷5=-6.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in elevation each minute is $-6.5\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f7059ce5-8fc3-5070-9a81-481986539eb2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f7059ce5-8fc3-5070-9a81-481986539eb2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7059ce5-8fc3-5070-9a81-481986539eb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-468f4a53-73ca-5783-986e-b0ce29a8bef3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0526a325-4b35-5b86-a878-5e5333d9b326" "answer-bcf3493c-5645-5e25-b1b7-3b5b96d5ae7b" "answer-468f4a53-73ca-5783-986e-b0ce29a8bef3" "answer-806ff764-e2bf-53ab-a9cc-959d31c2dd1c" "answer-88f6a9ce-4009-53d8-bdfd-4c51b0d6115e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0526a325-4b35-5b86-a878-5e5333d9b326" :answer/id #uuid "0526a325-4b35-5b86-a878-5e5333d9b326" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-27.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcf3493c-5645-5e25-b1b7-3b5b96d5ae7b" :answer/id #uuid "bcf3493c-5645-5e25-b1b7-3b5b96d5ae7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-468f4a53-73ca-5783-986e-b0ce29a8bef3" :answer/id #uuid "468f4a53-73ca-5783-986e-b0ce29a8bef3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-806ff764-e2bf-53ab-a9cc-959d31c2dd1c" :answer/id #uuid "806ff764-e2bf-53ab-a9cc-959d31c2dd1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88f6a9ce-4009-53d8-bdfd-4c51b0d6115e" :answer/id #uuid "88f6a9ce-4009-53d8-bdfd-4c51b0d6115e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cf763c67-fa75-5f72-8661-230a30b13c07" :question/id #uuid "cf763c67-fa75-5f72-8661-230a30b13c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-357219" :question/problem "Over $6$ days, $$14.70$ is deducted from a transit card balance due to daily fares of equal size. What is the change in the balance each day?" :question/worked-solution "Since the balance decreases by $$14.70$ over $6$ days, the total change is $-$14.70.$\n\nThis change occurs evenly over $6$ days, so we divide the total change by $6:$\n\n$$\n-14.70÷6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-14.70÷6=-2.45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in the balance each day is $-$2.45.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e39eeddb-5bfa-5977-9c82-e89755970e04"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e39eeddb-5bfa-5977-9c82-e89755970e04" :answer-field/id #uuid "e39eeddb-5bfa-5977-9c82-e89755970e04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bccc070b-f1a6-5003-8b9e-144f4fd5600b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3caccba4-e28b-5060-9476-d0d56ad94536" "answer-bccc070b-f1a6-5003-8b9e-144f4fd5600b" "answer-833f4adb-8fe6-510b-969d-25198280fdef" "answer-7d8506be-22ad-55b5-bbe0-f44209833338" "answer-acdceadf-9079-5ff6-89bd-6306c8f35b37"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3caccba4-e28b-5060-9476-d0d56ad94536" :answer/id #uuid "3caccba4-e28b-5060-9476-d0d56ad94536" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-$1.50$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bccc070b-f1a6-5003-8b9e-144f4fd5600b" :answer/id #uuid "bccc070b-f1a6-5003-8b9e-144f4fd5600b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-$2.45$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-833f4adb-8fe6-510b-969d-25198280fdef" :answer/id #uuid "833f4adb-8fe6-510b-969d-25198280fdef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-$2.85$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d8506be-22ad-55b5-bbe0-f44209833338" :answer/id #uuid "7d8506be-22ad-55b5-bbe0-f44209833338" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-$3.45$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acdceadf-9079-5ff6-89bd-6306c8f35b37" :answer/id #uuid "acdceadf-9079-5ff6-89bd-6306c8f35b37" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-$4.90$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "tutorial-e61ac714-2900-508a-9e66-9731f70e9997" :tutorial/id #uuid "e61ac714-2900-508a-9e66-9731f70e9997" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15164 :tutorial/title "Introduction" :tutorial/content "Some real-world situations involve a quantity changing by the same amount repeatedly.\n\nWhen the same change occurs repeatedly, we can represent the total change using multiplication.\n\nFor example, suppose a submarine begins at sea level and descends $2\\,\\text{m}$ each minute for $4$ minutes.\n\nSince descending represents a decrease in elevation, the change each minute is $-2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nThe total change can be written as repeated subtraction:\n\n![](images/bd/bd6e5218d475c46dd7f0ac578340124954d00fa940c81e32bffa49638b0e79c3.svg)\n\nThis repeated subtraction can also be written using multiplication:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4} \\times (-2)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n4 \\times (-2) = -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4$ minutes, the submarine's elevation is $-8\\,\\text{m}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id "tutorial-2472ba13-0b69-56f2-a477-ea1b2a6fc7e3" :tutorial/id #uuid "2472ba13-0b69-56f2-a477-ea1b2a6fc7e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15182 :tutorial/title "Solving Problems Using Division of Rational Numbers" :tutorial/content "Sometimes we know the total change in a quantity and want to find how much the quantity changes during each equal time period.\n\nIn these situations, we use division.\n\nFor example, suppose a total of $\\[math]24$ is withdrawn from a bank account over $6$ days in equal amounts.\n\nSince money is withdrawn, the total change in the balance is $-\\[/math]24$.\n\nTo find the change each day, we divide the total change by the number of days:\n\n$$\n-24 \\div 6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-24 \\div 6 = -4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the balance changes by $-\\[math]4$ each day." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092407 :activity/title "Solving Problems Using Multiplication and Division of Rational Numbers" :activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :activity/steps ["step-71ab7d7f-9cc1-5cdf-9b66-2a332d38a8be" "step-75f9f2c1-6398-5c11-a04e-b2c1ba8f749f" "step-7899018c-d6d5-5a73-8bba-a499b1170d83" "step-f8abbf23-8275-57f4-b19a-767b1595b9d0" "step-f88bb973-cdd9-5dc3-a6f0-ee3f3807638e" "step-3a846be7-1beb-5415-afda-c7dbc9dfc761"] :activity/first-step "step-71ab7d7f-9cc1-5cdf-9b66-2a332d38a8be" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-71ab7d7f-9cc1-5cdf-9b66-2a332d38a8be" :step/id #uuid "71ab7d7f-9cc1-5cdf-9b66-2a332d38a8be" :step/content "tutorial-e61ac714-2900-508a-9e66-9731f70e9997" :step/math-academy-id 621932 :step/next "step-75f9f2c1-6398-5c11-a04e-b2c1ba8f749f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-75f9f2c1-6398-5c11-a04e-b2c1ba8f749f" :step/id #uuid "75f9f2c1-6398-5c11-a04e-b2c1ba8f749f" :step/content "knowledge-point-6d7b67c1-aa22-5106-9f09-19e361dc25c0" :step/math-academy-id 621933 :step/next "step-7899018c-d6d5-5a73-8bba-a499b1170d83" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7899018c-d6d5-5a73-8bba-a499b1170d83" :step/id #uuid "7899018c-d6d5-5a73-8bba-a499b1170d83" :step/content "knowledge-point-aec0cd94-20e4-5748-b9c1-4de7b1854a8e" :step/math-academy-id 621941 :step/next "step-f8abbf23-8275-57f4-b19a-767b1595b9d0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f8abbf23-8275-57f4-b19a-767b1595b9d0" :step/id #uuid "f8abbf23-8275-57f4-b19a-767b1595b9d0" :step/content "knowledge-point-f589fffd-f5c4-590e-9ed1-01c20390700f" :step/math-academy-id 621944 :step/next "step-f88bb973-cdd9-5dc3-a6f0-ee3f3807638e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f88bb973-cdd9-5dc3-a6f0-ee3f3807638e" :step/id #uuid "f88bb973-cdd9-5dc3-a6f0-ee3f3807638e" :step/content "tutorial-2472ba13-0b69-56f2-a477-ea1b2a6fc7e3" :step/math-academy-id 621978 :step/next "step-3a846be7-1beb-5415-afda-c7dbc9dfc761" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3a846be7-1beb-5415-afda-c7dbc9dfc761" :step/id #uuid "3a846be7-1beb-5415-afda-c7dbc9dfc761" :step/content "knowledge-point-5b6c3c0b-b541-5b1f-810b-d9c4ffa1fe9c" :step/math-academy-id 621937 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ab0bcefd-4d59-56aa-b8ec-f3d0b1dc4757" "question-75b5d8b1-9316-5672-a007-ededd1581f02" "question-d6efb51f-1cc6-56d3-90b2-77b3cfdb82a3" "question-19ebeffd-a45a-5a50-925e-63646f4bc31a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188193 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p,\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $120$ apricots from a population of $20\\,000$, where $75\\%$ of the apricots weigh more than $50$ g.\n\nII. A sample of $25$ gym members from a population containing $200$ members, where $80\\%$ of the gym's members work out for more than $100$ minutes each visit.\n\nIII. A sample of $45$ flights in an airport, given that $90\\%$ of flights arrive on time. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size." :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left( p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\leq 5\\% \\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =120 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 20\\,000 \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,✓ \\\\ np & =120(0.75) \\\\  & =90 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =120(1-0.75) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =25, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =10 \\\\  & <25.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied. \n\nFor the other conditions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =45(0.9) \\\\  & =40.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =45(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4.5 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p) \\ngtr 5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-84cf65c5-831d-5253-bb62-b35c4c8f232c" "question-6c90ea0b-854a-5f39-a73d-92e856698eb8" "question-d98ad49e-5d4d-5c1b-b380-745357c60a55" "question-11e7255b-c3e0-5351-a566-b77404e961ee" "question-a0e2a9c0-d660-51ad-8aa9-baeec300d0b3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188194 :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=84$ from a population where $42\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $50\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9292 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9306 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9319 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9418 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9429 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9441 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9525 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9535 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9545 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left( p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np > 5,$ and $n(1-p) > 5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n = 84,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p = 0.42.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp = 84(0.42) = 35.28 > 5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p) = 84(1-0.42) = 48.72 > 5,\n$$\n\nand $n=84$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.42,\\frac{0.42(1-0.42)}{84}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.42,0.0029).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p} > 0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.5) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.5-0.42}{\\sqrt{0.0029}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.49) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.49) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.49).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.49) & =0.9319.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.5) & =1-Φ(1.49) \\\\  & =1-0.9319 \\\\  & =0.0681.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7a5fcfb6-efc6-55da-8a4d-4dde0bab77e9" "question-c30b7d81-9bb5-5e5f-938c-eb285e73259b" "question-2735f324-7321-5142-98fc-d20d8884d660" "question-fff0efc8-e9b9-52d5-b287-771e1cbfdc28"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188195 :question/problem "A popular streaming service has a database of $100$ million songs, of which $64$ million have a duration of less than $3$ minutes. If a user randomly creates a playlist with $128$ songs, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $64$ and $96$ (inclusive) songs in the sample have a duration of less than $3$ minutes. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -3.4 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -3.3 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -3.2 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0007 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0007 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0006 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.07 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.08 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.09 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.5 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9949 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9951 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9952 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.6 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9962 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9963 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9964 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.7 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9972 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9973 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9974 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left( p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np > 5,$ and $n(1-p) > 5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n = 128,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p = \\dfrac{64\\,000\\,000}{100\\,000\\,000} = 0.64.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp = 128(0.64) = 81.92 > 5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p) = 128(1-0.64) = 46.08 > 5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=128$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.64,\\frac{0.64(1-0.64)}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.64,0.0018).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $64$ and $96$ of the songs in the playlist last less than $3$ minutes, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{64}{128}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{96}{128} \\\\ 0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.5 \\leq          \\widehat{\\,p} \\leq          0.75).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.75) & =P(\\frac{0.5-0.64}{\\sqrt{0.0018}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.75-0.64}{\\sqrt{0.0018}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-3.30\\le Z\\le 2.59) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.59)-P(Z\\le -3.30) \\\\  & =Φ(2.59)-Φ(-3.30)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(2.59)=0.9952,\\,Φ(-3.30)=0.0005.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.75) & =Φ(2.59)-Φ(-3.30) \\\\  & =0.9952-0.0005 \\\\  & =0.9947.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ab0bcefd-4d59-56aa-b8ec-f3d0b1dc4757" :question/id #uuid "ab0bcefd-4d59-56aa-b8ec-f3d0b1dc4757" :question/math-academy-id "q-146838" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ recently caught shrimps, where $55%$ of the shrimps in the sea weigh more than $10\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nII. A sample of $27$ companies, where $82%$ of the companies in the country employ more than $100$ people.\n\nIII. A sample of $100$ songs in a streaming service, given that $92%$ of the songs last less than $3$ minutes.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.55) \\\\  & =27.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.55) \\\\  & =22.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =27(0.82) \\\\  & =22.14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =27(1-0.82) \\\\  & =4.86 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100(0.92) \\\\  & =92 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100(1-0.92) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e00782d6-c22d-5c54-843e-daefb08e60d9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e00782d6-c22d-5c54-843e-daefb08e60d9" :answer-field/id #uuid "e00782d6-c22d-5c54-843e-daefb08e60d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-56fb8155-120b-5170-9145-1fd130c2ec2e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-619c4398-31f6-54ae-99e8-d78d34165a47" "answer-56fb8155-120b-5170-9145-1fd130c2ec2e" "answer-e8ba3ea6-389e-54cf-a4c9-3ac81a2a28ba" "answer-ac32c0bc-5323-5faa-b8d5-33267bce243a" "answer-4116f3f0-c977-5e04-8b37-cfee23a31d33"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-619c4398-31f6-54ae-99e8-d78d34165a47" :answer/id #uuid "619c4398-31f6-54ae-99e8-d78d34165a47" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56fb8155-120b-5170-9145-1fd130c2ec2e" :answer/id #uuid "56fb8155-120b-5170-9145-1fd130c2ec2e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8ba3ea6-389e-54cf-a4c9-3ac81a2a28ba" :answer/id #uuid "e8ba3ea6-389e-54cf-a4c9-3ac81a2a28ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac32c0bc-5323-5faa-b8d5-33267bce243a" :answer/id #uuid "ac32c0bc-5323-5faa-b8d5-33267bce243a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4116f3f0-c977-5e04-8b37-cfee23a31d33" :answer/id #uuid "4116f3f0-c977-5e04-8b37-cfee23a31d33" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-75b5d8b1-9316-5672-a007-ededd1581f02" :question/id #uuid "75b5d8b1-9316-5672-a007-ededd1581f02" :question/math-academy-id "q-146819" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=200$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $80%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=30$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =200 \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & <200\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.8) \\\\  & =32 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.8) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =30(0.7) \\\\  & =21 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =30(1-0.7) \\\\  & =9 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fa3a4acb-3217-5494-99eb-6860b8a21200"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fa3a4acb-3217-5494-99eb-6860b8a21200" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa3a4acb-3217-5494-99eb-6860b8a21200" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6ad952b8-2ed2-584a-967d-7f752886a23d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-508c1697-4000-5926-99b2-497ea4d6c810" "answer-d6c31986-9c09-50b3-be41-6a9593a36e04" "answer-0cd46750-aab1-5ab4-8f35-4bb7abe41d55" "answer-6ad952b8-2ed2-584a-967d-7f752886a23d" "answer-e34c4d92-333e-5b82-80d3-ad763eae7cfc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-508c1697-4000-5926-99b2-497ea4d6c810" :answer/id #uuid "508c1697-4000-5926-99b2-497ea4d6c810" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6c31986-9c09-50b3-be41-6a9593a36e04" :answer/id #uuid "d6c31986-9c09-50b3-be41-6a9593a36e04" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cd46750-aab1-5ab4-8f35-4bb7abe41d55" :answer/id #uuid "0cd46750-aab1-5ab4-8f35-4bb7abe41d55" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ad952b8-2ed2-584a-967d-7f752886a23d" :answer/id #uuid "6ad952b8-2ed2-584a-967d-7f752886a23d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e34c4d92-333e-5b82-80d3-ad763eae7cfc" :answer/id #uuid "e34c4d92-333e-5b82-80d3-ad763eae7cfc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d6efb51f-1cc6-56d3-90b2-77b3cfdb82a3" :question/id #uuid "d6efb51f-1cc6-56d3-90b2-77b3cfdb82a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-146816" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=100$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=60$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =100, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & <100.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =60 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,500 \\\\  & =75\\,✓ \\\\ np & =60(0.90) \\\\  & =54 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60(1-0.90) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,500 \\\\  & =75\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.9) \\\\  & =36 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a7338c4-7992-59c5-b27c-44f71458a952"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a7338c4-7992-59c5-b27c-44f71458a952" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a7338c4-7992-59c5-b27c-44f71458a952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4c291cdc-8fdf-5b18-b158-8a4495491a8c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2f6ee384-815c-53b4-9f2d-c31907cf3c91" "answer-f9a565c8-6e99-5075-9f5d-a43101206bcd" "answer-82d21d0a-8fa8-5493-aa93-34dbcc67ac2e" "answer-4c291cdc-8fdf-5b18-b158-8a4495491a8c" "answer-d97cc3ab-896e-5f3e-bae3-64970cbcd952"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f6ee384-815c-53b4-9f2d-c31907cf3c91" :answer/id #uuid "2f6ee384-815c-53b4-9f2d-c31907cf3c91" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9a565c8-6e99-5075-9f5d-a43101206bcd" :answer/id #uuid "f9a565c8-6e99-5075-9f5d-a43101206bcd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82d21d0a-8fa8-5493-aa93-34dbcc67ac2e" :answer/id #uuid "82d21d0a-8fa8-5493-aa93-34dbcc67ac2e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c291cdc-8fdf-5b18-b158-8a4495491a8c" :answer/id #uuid "4c291cdc-8fdf-5b18-b158-8a4495491a8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d97cc3ab-896e-5f3e-bae3-64970cbcd952" :answer/id #uuid "d97cc3ab-896e-5f3e-bae3-64970cbcd952" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188685 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=16$ from a population of size $N=100$ in which $25%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=32$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $10%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=32$  from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $75%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =16,\\, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =5 \\\\  & <16.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =32 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =32(0.1) \\\\  & =3.2 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =32 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =32(0.75) \\\\  & =24 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =32(1-0.75) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186186390 :answer-field/correct 17592186186391 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-19ebeffd-a45a-5a50-925e-63646f4bc31a" :question/id #uuid "19ebeffd-a45a-5a50-925e-63646f4bc31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-146809" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=52$ from a population of size $N=120$ in which $8%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=4$ from a population of size $N=200$ in which $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=20$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =52, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 120 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & <52.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =4 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =10\\,✓ \\\\ np & =4(0.7) \\\\  & =2.8 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =20 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25\\,✓ \\\\ np & =20(0.70) \\\\  & =14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20(1-0.70) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-19d798ec-e079-52f8-a343-6ba9d9d2c646"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-19d798ec-e079-52f8-a343-6ba9d9d2c646" :answer-field/id #uuid "19d798ec-e079-52f8-a343-6ba9d9d2c646" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d48cb210-f5a6-59f2-923a-069fc7f25fa6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9a40a784-700c-5e4c-9e97-c0835f0a60b8" "answer-f616020e-49fd-5757-a8e6-0c21672f2fdb" "answer-a3bf5c83-f1eb-519c-8394-47157850924b" "answer-1e4ff370-88d6-5364-ac58-0e5d98900a7b" "answer-d48cb210-f5a6-59f2-923a-069fc7f25fa6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a40a784-700c-5e4c-9e97-c0835f0a60b8" :answer/id #uuid "9a40a784-700c-5e4c-9e97-c0835f0a60b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f616020e-49fd-5757-a8e6-0c21672f2fdb" :answer/id #uuid "f616020e-49fd-5757-a8e6-0c21672f2fdb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3bf5c83-f1eb-519c-8394-47157850924b" :answer/id #uuid "a3bf5c83-f1eb-519c-8394-47157850924b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e4ff370-88d6-5364-ac58-0e5d98900a7b" :answer/id #uuid "1e4ff370-88d6-5364-ac58-0e5d98900a7b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d48cb210-f5a6-59f2-923a-069fc7f25fa6" :answer/id #uuid "d48cb210-f5a6-59f2-923a-069fc7f25fa6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-84cf65c5-831d-5253-bb62-b35c4c8f232c" :question/id #uuid "84cf65c5-831d-5253-bb62-b35c4c8f232c" :question/math-academy-id "q-146942" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population where $20%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $15%$ and $25%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=50,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.2.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=50(0.2)=10>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=50(1-0.2)=40>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=50$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.2,\\frac{0.2(1-0.2)}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.2,0.0032).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.15\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.25).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.15\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.25) & =P(\\frac{0.15-0.2}{\\sqrt{0.0032}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.25-0.2}{\\sqrt{0.0032}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.88\\le Z\\le 0.88) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.88)-P(Z\\le -0.88) \\\\  & =Φ(0.88)-Φ(-0.88)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3db1ac1a-fba9-53f0-abaa-ce90a089ca8c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3db1ac1a-fba9-53f0-abaa-ce90a089ca8c" :answer-field/id #uuid "3db1ac1a-fba9-53f0-abaa-ce90a089ca8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fc19f28c-42f1-5740-9078-6e1e7a33e836" :answer-field/choices ["answer-68727b43-f29b-53a1-a5d7-6dcd49a3a034" "answer-56867d82-3d07-5414-9450-8f0c79240546" "answer-aa9cff08-4800-5f04-8bf4-570544c083f1" "answer-fc19f28c-42f1-5740-9078-6e1e7a33e836" "answer-a3dbdab0-ba6b-5637-9d3a-42a4f9671754"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68727b43-f29b-53a1-a5d7-6dcd49a3a034" :answer/id #uuid "68727b43-f29b-53a1-a5d7-6dcd49a3a034" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.36)-Φ(-0.36)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56867d82-3d07-5414-9450-8f0c79240546" :answer/id #uuid "56867d82-3d07-5414-9450-8f0c79240546" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.78)-Φ(-0.78)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa9cff08-4800-5f04-8bf4-570544c083f1" :answer/id #uuid "aa9cff08-4800-5f04-8bf4-570544c083f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.80)-Φ(-0.80)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc19f28c-42f1-5740-9078-6e1e7a33e836" :answer/id #uuid "fc19f28c-42f1-5740-9078-6e1e7a33e836" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.88)-Φ(-0.88)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3dbdab0-ba6b-5637-9d3a-42a4f9671754" :answer/id #uuid "a3dbdab0-ba6b-5637-9d3a-42a4f9671754" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.96)-Φ(-0.69)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6c90ea0b-854a-5f39-a73d-92e856698eb8" :question/id #uuid "6c90ea0b-854a-5f39-a73d-92e856698eb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-147212" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=125$ from a population where $75%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $65%$ but less than $72%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2266\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2236\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2206\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2578\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2546\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2912\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2877\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2843\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0052\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0051\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0049\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0069\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0068\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0066\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0091\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0089\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0087\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.75.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=125(0.75)=93.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=125(1-0.75)=31.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=125$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.75,\\frac{0.75(1-0.75)}{125}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.75,0.0015).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.65<\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.72).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.65<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.72) & =P(\\frac{0.65-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}<Z\\le \\frac{0.72-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.58<Z\\le -0.77) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.77)-P(Z\\le -2.58) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.77)-Φ(-2.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.77) & =0.2206 \\\\ Φ(-2.58) & =0.0049.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.65<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.72) & =Φ(-0.77)-Φ(-2.58) \\\\  & =0.2206-0.0049 \\\\  & =0.2157.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b58d986a-b956-51c7-a441-3edd459a5e37"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b58d986a-b956-51c7-a441-3edd459a5e37" :answer-field/id #uuid "b58d986a-b956-51c7-a441-3edd459a5e37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c35ea702-ea78-5e82-b0cb-6e3428dbb539" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c8967056-a8d1-5db9-a7a2-0afcb5f80f4f" "answer-c35ea702-ea78-5e82-b0cb-6e3428dbb539" "answer-a0fa6a07-689e-56bd-934e-366f80119f2e" "answer-d2d33bab-f28f-555d-b84b-cc1cf9685d42" "answer-26c8f58f-95c5-542e-9b35-85744163dc9b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8967056-a8d1-5db9-a7a2-0afcb5f80f4f" :answer/id #uuid "c8967056-a8d1-5db9-a7a2-0afcb5f80f4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1752" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c35ea702-ea78-5e82-b0cb-6e3428dbb539" :answer/id #uuid "c35ea702-ea78-5e82-b0cb-6e3428dbb539" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2157" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0fa6a07-689e-56bd-934e-366f80119f2e" :answer/id #uuid "a0fa6a07-689e-56bd-934e-366f80119f2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2517" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2d33bab-f28f-555d-b84b-cc1cf9685d42" :answer/id #uuid "d2d33bab-f28f-555d-b84b-cc1cf9685d42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2715" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26c8f58f-95c5-542e-9b35-85744163dc9b" :answer/id #uuid "26c8f58f-95c5-542e-9b35-85744163dc9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5172" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d98ad49e-5d4d-5c1b-b380-745357c60a55" :question/id #uuid "d98ad49e-5d4d-5c1b-b380-745357c60a55" :question/math-academy-id "q-146972" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=75$ from a population where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $50%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=75,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=75(0.45)=33.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=75(1-0.45)=41.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=75$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{75}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0033).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.5) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0033}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le 0.87) \\\\  & =Φ(0.87)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b081ec7e-333d-5dd4-a804-4e114c60c34a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b081ec7e-333d-5dd4-a804-4e114c60c34a" :answer-field/id #uuid "b081ec7e-333d-5dd4-a804-4e114c60c34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9dca0f89-0c53-5160-acd7-38acb63d1d3b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b101809-2ccd-5ced-a664-5deefedab9db" "answer-2ff494bd-0b9f-5135-9d18-50e3d2df3439" "answer-50ba5d47-3502-5e34-8efb-9b1500551a36" "answer-9dca0f89-0c53-5160-acd7-38acb63d1d3b" "answer-729c9ecc-6c95-5e5f-ad08-f8c44fba093f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b101809-2ccd-5ced-a664-5deefedab9db" :answer/id #uuid "8b101809-2ccd-5ced-a664-5deefedab9db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.64)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ff494bd-0b9f-5135-9d18-50e3d2df3439" :answer/id #uuid "2ff494bd-0b9f-5135-9d18-50e3d2df3439" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.71)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50ba5d47-3502-5e34-8efb-9b1500551a36" :answer/id #uuid "50ba5d47-3502-5e34-8efb-9b1500551a36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.79)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dca0f89-0c53-5160-acd7-38acb63d1d3b" :answer/id #uuid "9dca0f89-0c53-5160-acd7-38acb63d1d3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.87)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-729c9ecc-6c95-5e5f-ad08-f8c44fba093f" :answer/id #uuid "729c9ecc-6c95-5e5f-ad08-f8c44fba093f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.93)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-11e7255b-c3e0-5351-a566-b77404e961ee" :question/id #uuid "11e7255b-c3e0-5351-a566-b77404e961ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-147071" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=99$ from a population where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $50%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=99,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=99(0.45)=44.55>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=99(1-0.45)=54.45>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=99$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{99}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0025).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0025}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.00) \\\\  & =Φ(1.00).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.00) & =0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5) & =Φ(0.8413) \\\\  & =0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-603f3c7e-5a6d-59bf-b0ec-1681329ffe35"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-603f3c7e-5a6d-59bf-b0ec-1681329ffe35" :answer-field/id #uuid "603f3c7e-5a6d-59bf-b0ec-1681329ffe35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d9b614cd-f118-5752-a14a-3e03c66ef5a7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b2ffbca-0cac-5940-a7dc-6a3aa9f30b1a" "answer-b739cc9f-d7c6-57b4-96f4-f38acc56c93f" "answer-d46951e0-79b5-5171-aac4-104568cfcf6a" "answer-d9b614cd-f118-5752-a14a-3e03c66ef5a7" "answer-e41e3f60-af2a-5d03-ae51-9b94e91c8682"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b2ffbca-0cac-5940-a7dc-6a3aa9f30b1a" :answer/id #uuid "8b2ffbca-0cac-5940-a7dc-6a3aa9f30b1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6954" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b739cc9f-d7c6-57b4-96f4-f38acc56c93f" :answer/id #uuid "b739cc9f-d7c6-57b4-96f4-f38acc56c93f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7816" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d46951e0-79b5-5171-aac4-104568cfcf6a" :answer/id #uuid "d46951e0-79b5-5171-aac4-104568cfcf6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7924" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9b614cd-f118-5752-a14a-3e03c66ef5a7" :answer/id #uuid "d9b614cd-f118-5752-a14a-3e03c66ef5a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8413" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e41e3f60-af2a-5d03-ae51-9b94e91c8682" :answer/id #uuid "e41e3f60-af2a-5d03-ae51-9b94e91c8682" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9723" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a0e2a9c0-d660-51ad-8aa9-baeec300d0b3" :question/id #uuid "a0e2a9c0-d660-51ad-8aa9-baeec300d0b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-146975" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=76$ from a population where $24%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $30%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9032\\,$ | $\\,0.9049\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=76,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.24.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=76(0.24)=18.24>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=76(1-0.24)=57.76>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=76$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.24,\\frac{0.24(1-0.24)}{76}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.24,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.3) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.3-0.24}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.22) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.22) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.22).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.22) & =0.8888.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.3) & =1-Φ(1.22) \\\\  & =1-0.8888 \\\\  & =0.1112.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fa9ea89e-2b67-50ed-9875-e233798706d2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fa9ea89e-2b67-50ed-9875-e233798706d2" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa9ea89e-2b67-50ed-9875-e233798706d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-43b7b2e8-0dd0-53f5-a8d4-5c5d905e9b5a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88a07982-8bca-5d34-ba03-ad213b50a5ca" "answer-43b7b2e8-0dd0-53f5-a8d4-5c5d905e9b5a" "answer-b0d11df6-7dc5-50f2-bbd9-0d41650103a0" "answer-a5ecc379-ae54-5c46-b4ed-94535e2a2e81" "answer-3009884b-eaac-58c4-9dd7-476278e483b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88a07982-8bca-5d34-ba03-ad213b50a5ca" :answer/id #uuid "88a07982-8bca-5d34-ba03-ad213b50a5ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0808" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43b7b2e8-0dd0-53f5-a8d4-5c5d905e9b5a" :answer/id #uuid "43b7b2e8-0dd0-53f5-a8d4-5c5d905e9b5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1112" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0d11df6-7dc5-50f2-bbd9-0d41650103a0" :answer/id #uuid "b0d11df6-7dc5-50f2-bbd9-0d41650103a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1615" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5ecc379-ae54-5c46-b4ed-94535e2a2e81" :answer/id #uuid "a5ecc379-ae54-5c46-b4ed-94535e2a2e81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2121" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3009884b-eaac-58c4-9dd7-476278e483b9" :answer/id #uuid "3009884b-eaac-58c4-9dd7-476278e483b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2626" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a5fcfb6-efc6-55da-8a4d-4dde0bab77e9" :question/id #uuid "7a5fcfb6-efc6-55da-8a4d-4dde0bab77e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-147448" :question/problem "An amusement park knows that $25%$ of its visitors ride the rollercoaster. The manager of the park conducts a random sample of $125$ visitors. Find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $40$ of them rode the rollercoaster. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.25.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=125(0.25)=31.25>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=125(1-0.25)=93.75>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=125$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,\\frac{0.25(1-0.25)}{125}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,0.0015).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $40$ visitors in the sample rode the rollercoaster, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}<\\frac{40}{125}=0.32.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.32).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.32-0.25}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(1.81).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6c885ba5-a648-5bca-b5b2-398b1f373335"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6c885ba5-a648-5bca-b5b2-398b1f373335" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c885ba5-a648-5bca-b5b2-398b1f373335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-07440603-54b5-5976-899e-ae9b9661d017" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f8d336e-ed5a-5688-ade1-9317c97d93ce" "answer-32730632-54f9-51bd-9b77-5eb922390acc" "answer-f139c347-a6cc-572a-946f-3b855b388e8a" "answer-07440603-54b5-5976-899e-ae9b9661d017" "answer-e4626d23-d099-557f-8b2c-0a74d5d57629"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f8d336e-ed5a-5688-ade1-9317c97d93ce" :answer/id #uuid "4f8d336e-ed5a-5688-ade1-9317c97d93ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.97)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32730632-54f9-51bd-9b77-5eb922390acc" :answer/id #uuid "32730632-54f9-51bd-9b77-5eb922390acc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1.47)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f139c347-a6cc-572a-946f-3b855b388e8a" :answer/id #uuid "f139c347-a6cc-572a-946f-3b855b388e8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1.65)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07440603-54b5-5976-899e-ae9b9661d017" :answer/id #uuid "07440603-54b5-5976-899e-ae9b9661d017" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1.81)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4626d23-d099-557f-8b2c-0a74d5d57629" :answer/id #uuid "e4626d23-d099-557f-8b2c-0a74d5d57629" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(2.11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c30b7d81-9bb5-5e5f-938c-eb285e73259b" :question/id #uuid "c30b7d81-9bb5-5e5f-938c-eb285e73259b" :question/math-academy-id "q-147482" :question/problem "A smartphone app has $500\\,000$ users, $150\\,000$ of which have a paid account. If an app developer randomly samples $300$ users, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $72$ and $90$ (inclusive) users in the sample have a paid account. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0091\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0089\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0087\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0119\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0116\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0113\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0154\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0150\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0146\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5000\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5040\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5080\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5398\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5438\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5478\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=300,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{150\\,000}{500\\,000}=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=300(0.3)=90>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=300(1-0.3)=210>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=300$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{300}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0007).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $72$ and $90$ of users in the sample have a paid account, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{72}{300}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{90}{300} \\\\ 0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =P(\\frac{0.24-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.3-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.27\\le Z\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0)-P(Z\\le -2.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0)=0.5000,\\,Φ(-2.27)=0.0116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27) \\\\  & =0.5000-0.0116 \\\\  & =0.4884.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f514e27c-296f-5be6-8d4d-2642d4f0a3fa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f514e27c-296f-5be6-8d4d-2642d4f0a3fa" :answer-field/id #uuid "f514e27c-296f-5be6-8d4d-2642d4f0a3fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7eb8dfe6-123f-5cfc-a78b-fa1793a96281" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd4a2dca-96f1-5a35-91e8-35bd1ef6be15" "answer-e120a658-b96e-5c48-8cf4-5d70b8be76ed" "answer-45e1015c-d45f-5639-97c2-7d9314f8ad2e" "answer-7eb8dfe6-123f-5cfc-a78b-fa1793a96281" "answer-03359e21-7a1b-5dec-803d-c1cd7423ca1d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd4a2dca-96f1-5a35-91e8-35bd1ef6be15" :answer/id #uuid "bd4a2dca-96f1-5a35-91e8-35bd1ef6be15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2687" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e120a658-b96e-5c48-8cf4-5d70b8be76ed" :answer/id #uuid "e120a658-b96e-5c48-8cf4-5d70b8be76ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3954" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45e1015c-d45f-5639-97c2-7d9314f8ad2e" :answer/id #uuid "45e1015c-d45f-5639-97c2-7d9314f8ad2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4275" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7eb8dfe6-123f-5cfc-a78b-fa1793a96281" :answer/id #uuid "7eb8dfe6-123f-5cfc-a78b-fa1793a96281" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4884" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03359e21-7a1b-5dec-803d-c1cd7423ca1d" :answer/id #uuid "03359e21-7a1b-5dec-803d-c1cd7423ca1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5329" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188681 :question/problem "In a school of $900$ students, $432$ of the students are male. If the school randomly samples $50$ students, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $20$ and $23$ (inclusive) of students in the sample are male. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1131\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1112\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1093\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1335\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1314\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1292\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1539\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1515\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3557\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3520\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3483\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3936\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3897\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3859\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4247\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=50,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{432}{900}=0.48.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=50(0.48)=24>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=50(1-0.48)=26>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=50$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.48,\\frac{0.48(1-0.48)}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.48,0.0050).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $20$ and $23$ of students in the sample are male, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{50}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{23}{50} \\\\ 0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.46).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.46) & =P(\\frac{0.4-0.48}{\\sqrt{0.0050}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.46-0.48}{\\sqrt{0.0050}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.13\\le Z\\le -0.28) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.28)-P(Z\\le -1.13) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.28)-Φ(-1.13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.28)=0.3897,\\,Φ(-1.13)=0.1292.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.46) & =Φ(-0.28)-Φ(-1.13) \\\\  & =0.3897-0.1292 \\\\  & =0.2605.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186186366 :answer-field/correct 17592186186369 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-2735f324-7321-5142-98fc-d20d8884d660" :question/id #uuid "2735f324-7321-5142-98fc-d20d8884d660" :question/math-academy-id "q-147504" :question/problem "It is known that $35%$ of the people arriving at a clothing store buy something. If the manager randomly samples $65$ customers, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $13$ but fewer than $26$ of them bought something. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=65,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.35.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=65(0.35)=22.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=65(1-0.35)=42.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=65$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.35,\\frac{0.35(1-0.35)}{65}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.35,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $13$ but fewer than $26$ people bought something, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{65}<\\overset{^}{\\,p} & <\\frac{26}{65} \\\\ 0.2<\\overset{^}{\\,p} & <0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.2\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.4) & =P(\\frac{0.2-0.35}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}<Z<\\frac{0.4-0.35}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.54<Z<0.85) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.85)-P(Z<-2.54) \\\\  & =Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.4) & =Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8db11675-d772-5e72-8d9a-3a6e110d5729"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8db11675-d772-5e72-8d9a-3a6e110d5729" :answer-field/id #uuid "8db11675-d772-5e72-8d9a-3a6e110d5729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f8d26bd1-f8e6-5285-9981-178e6521065c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-91695da2-de95-5431-872f-19b52844bdd1" "answer-b13dcd98-7237-5fd6-a1e6-940842cbdb82" "answer-f8d26bd1-f8e6-5285-9981-178e6521065c" "answer-a1c38604-6309-551b-ac28-d167af1123ef" "answer-be8b84ad-a507-55af-aa58-6bcf6faf6c0d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91695da2-de95-5431-872f-19b52844bdd1" :answer/id #uuid "91695da2-de95-5431-872f-19b52844bdd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.53)-Φ(-1.86)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b13dcd98-7237-5fd6-a1e6-940842cbdb82" :answer/id #uuid "b13dcd98-7237-5fd6-a1e6-940842cbdb82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.66)-Φ(-2.23)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8d26bd1-f8e6-5285-9981-178e6521065c" :answer/id #uuid "f8d26bd1-f8e6-5285-9981-178e6521065c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1c38604-6309-551b-ac28-d167af1123ef" :answer/id #uuid "a1c38604-6309-551b-ac28-d167af1123ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1.23)-Φ(-2.96)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be8b84ad-a507-55af-aa58-6bcf6faf6c0d" :answer/id #uuid "be8b84ad-a507-55af-aa58-6bcf6faf6c0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1.55)-Φ(-3.14)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fff0efc8-e9b9-52d5-b287-771e1cbfdc28" :question/id #uuid "fff0efc8-e9b9-52d5-b287-771e1cbfdc28" :question/math-academy-id "q-147479" :question/problem "A dried apricot producer knows that $70%$ of apricots weigh more than $40$ g. If the factory randomly samples $350$ apricots, find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $224$ apricots weigh more than $40$ g. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=350,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=350(0.7)=245>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=350(1-0.7)=105>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=350$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{350}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.0006).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $224$ apricots in the sample weigh more than $40$ g, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}<\\frac{224}{350}=0.64.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.64).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.64) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.64-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.0006}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-2.45) \\\\  & =Φ(-2.45).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3a76ecc-0207-5bbe-bd7d-f25da682e7e4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3a76ecc-0207-5bbe-bd7d-f25da682e7e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a76ecc-0207-5bbe-bd7d-f25da682e7e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2443380b-5fb7-5644-9da4-9247216c51c7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-250e3fc3-8889-5bac-9379-1c96182f3fda" "answer-47f4e987-a1cd-511c-b8f3-f4bb08cf984c" "answer-7f968a03-d0e7-595f-852d-c5d8937237d8" "answer-2443380b-5fb7-5644-9da4-9247216c51c7" "answer-d26bcec2-8907-5cfb-9e9c-5911985e9883"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-250e3fc3-8889-5bac-9379-1c96182f3fda" :answer/id #uuid "250e3fc3-8889-5bac-9379-1c96182f3fda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(-1.62)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47f4e987-a1cd-511c-b8f3-f4bb08cf984c" :answer/id #uuid "47f4e987-a1cd-511c-b8f3-f4bb08cf984c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(-1.85)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f968a03-d0e7-595f-852d-c5d8937237d8" :answer/id #uuid "7f968a03-d0e7-595f-852d-c5d8937237d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(-2.25)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2443380b-5fb7-5644-9da4-9247216c51c7" :answer/id #uuid "2443380b-5fb7-5644-9da4-9247216c51c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(-2.45)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d26bcec2-8907-5cfb-9e9c-5911985e9883" :answer/id #uuid "d26bcec2-8907-5cfb-9e9c-5911985e9883" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(-2.78)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189500 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/2c/2c717541104571d6dce45dc88e30c4ff2dd42471d2348fe05b7d91ce395adae4.svg)\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p).\n$$\n\nLet's assume the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size. Under this condition, we've seen that $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ can be considered to be (approximately) independent and identically distributed, which means that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p),\n$$\n\nwhere $B(n,p)$ denotes a binomial distribution, and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nRemember that $X$ represents the number of sample members with the characteristic.\n\nNow, let's assume that $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ so we can apply the normal approximation of the binomial distribution (i.e., the central limit theorem). Then,\n\n$$\nX  \\approx N\\big(np,np(1-p)\\big).\n$$\n\nFurthermore, we've also seen that we can construct an estimator $\\widehat{\\,p}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} =  \\dfrac{X}{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $X$ is approximately normally distributed, $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is also normally distributed. Therefore, the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\approx N\\bigg(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\bigg).\n$$\n\nFinally, since we usually don't know $p,$ we can use a particular estimate to approximate $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\,p}}]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}].$\n\n**Watch out!** In this lesson, $N$ denotes the size of a population, while $N(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ denotes the normal distribution." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189501 :tutorial/content "Let's summarize everything we know about the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n*Suppose we have a population of size $N$ in which a proportion $p$ has a particular characteristic. Consider a sample of size $n$ drawn from the population, where*\n\n- $n \\leq 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is smaller than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np > 5,$ *and*\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5$.\n\n*Then, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as the normally distributed random variable \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left( p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right).\n$$\n\n*\n\nThe flow chart below summarizes the steps that led us to this model.\n\n![](images/6b/6b9aa832ab1df23e40fe48e52c366d6e5d32a037376b549d6ecc880d2f3832c1.png)\n\nFinally, note that the conditions $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ have a particular interpretation in this context:\n\n- $np > 5$ means the expected number of sample members with the characteristic is greater than $5,$\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ means the expected number of sample members without the characteristic is greater than $5.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188515 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179763 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cf46068e-803c-5f09-a183-8df712f591aa" "question-3efd4daf-a1cf-5957-b4aa-c8ff94e19b4d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179764 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ad9cae8a-9ebc-5c61-bc98-540e57a392b2" "question-48442579-8421-52f2-adbc-f2ba91a631dd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179751 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-40cade47-943f-50c4-8cf4-c4d5f9b88bc8" "question-b5988724-f4b1-5a52-8719-b7ac5bee89d1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cf46068e-803c-5f09-a183-8df712f591aa" :question/id #uuid "cf46068e-803c-5f09-a183-8df712f591aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-52392" :question/problem "$2\\sqrt{y}+7\\sqrt{y}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{y}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{y}+7\\sqrt{y} & = \\\\ (2+7)\\sqrt{y} & = \\\\ 9\\sqrt{y} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8e776382-1a74-592a-ba5a-3f4a958b9bf7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8e776382-1a74-592a-ba5a-3f4a958b9bf7" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e776382-1a74-592a-ba5a-3f4a958b9bf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-248265cd-124a-59ae-9086-ddce3bd69527" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e85d285d-280a-5fa3-a0a7-67d21a36a2a7" "answer-bb99538f-3dd1-5ad6-bb88-f4133f077881" "answer-edad2f10-b7c5-582c-bb71-7ac407fbd566" "answer-248265cd-124a-59ae-9086-ddce3bd69527" "answer-6082565f-e6f0-5c5d-bb48-afa109edcba3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e85d285d-280a-5fa3-a0a7-67d21a36a2a7" :answer/id #uuid "e85d285d-280a-5fa3-a0a7-67d21a36a2a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb99538f-3dd1-5ad6-bb88-f4133f077881" :answer/id #uuid "bb99538f-3dd1-5ad6-bb88-f4133f077881" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\sqrt{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edad2f10-b7c5-582c-bb71-7ac407fbd566" :answer/id #uuid "edad2f10-b7c5-582c-bb71-7ac407fbd566" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-248265cd-124a-59ae-9086-ddce3bd69527" :answer/id #uuid "248265cd-124a-59ae-9086-ddce3bd69527" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\sqrt{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6082565f-e6f0-5c5d-bb48-afa109edcba3" :answer/id #uuid "6082565f-e6f0-5c5d-bb48-afa109edcba3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3efd4daf-a1cf-5957-b4aa-c8ff94e19b4d" :question/id #uuid "3efd4daf-a1cf-5957-b4aa-c8ff94e19b4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-52391" :question/problem "$3\\sqrt{x}-2\\sqrt{x}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{x}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{x}-2\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ (3-2)\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7f247b26-452d-5f9b-bfd1-ec0918630ed5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7f247b26-452d-5f9b-bfd1-ec0918630ed5" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f247b26-452d-5f9b-bfd1-ec0918630ed5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-12288c26-42df-51dd-808b-20f205e3ddd0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c2fc26f-d391-5c9e-920f-520d82144c0d" "answer-97ddaa21-49aa-5eb8-9cbb-6265ff11235f" "answer-00a3bbbb-3d58-5812-91fd-8458c0795915" "answer-12288c26-42df-51dd-808b-20f205e3ddd0" "answer-4dfca826-0d43-5c60-a846-18bed3fc4466"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c2fc26f-d391-5c9e-920f-520d82144c0d" :answer/id #uuid "4c2fc26f-d391-5c9e-920f-520d82144c0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97ddaa21-49aa-5eb8-9cbb-6265ff11235f" :answer/id #uuid "97ddaa21-49aa-5eb8-9cbb-6265ff11235f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\sqrt{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00a3bbbb-3d58-5812-91fd-8458c0795915" :answer/id #uuid "00a3bbbb-3d58-5812-91fd-8458c0795915" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12288c26-42df-51dd-808b-20f205e3ddd0" :answer/id #uuid "12288c26-42df-51dd-808b-20f205e3ddd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dfca826-0d43-5c60-a846-18bed3fc4466" :answer/id #uuid "4dfca826-0d43-5c60-a846-18bed3fc4466" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ad9cae8a-9ebc-5c61-bc98-540e57a392b2" :question/id #uuid "ad9cae8a-9ebc-5c61-bc98-540e57a392b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-111028" :question/problem "${n}^{2}\\sqrt{27n}+\\sqrt{48{n}^{5}}=$" :question/worked-solution "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}\\sqrt{27n}+\\sqrt{48{n}^{5}} & = \\\\ {n}^{2}\\sqrt{9\\cdot 3n}+\\sqrt{16{n}^{4}\\cdot 3n} & = \\\\ {n}^{2}\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{3n}+\\sqrt{16{n}^{4}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3n} & = \\\\ 3{n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n}+4{n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n}+4{n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n} & = \\\\ (3+4){n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n} & = \\\\ 7{n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2002b9bb-cf1e-5c50-866c-90d860508452"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2002b9bb-cf1e-5c50-866c-90d860508452" :answer-field/id #uuid "2002b9bb-cf1e-5c50-866c-90d860508452" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d0217c62-f412-503b-b6a7-7eeb12cb43cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bbcd05f3-4d31-538b-823a-86edda099981" "answer-013c5fbb-49f5-5cea-af95-36393c4411da" "answer-92318117-1a9a-54a3-b50a-b61d8666f7b4" "answer-ca597711-da0d-5cd9-ade8-6b530651d1e7" "answer-d0217c62-f412-503b-b6a7-7eeb12cb43cd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbcd05f3-4d31-538b-823a-