ction point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C).$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C)) \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)})}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x>0,$ $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\ne 0,$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f427581a-7c85-5678-8de9-9e496c491b50"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f427581a-7c85-5678-8de9-9e496c491b50" :answer-field/id #uuid "f427581a-7c85-5678-8de9-9e496c491b50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6d0e9c64-8b2c-5046-9a49-1b2ece1ed25e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-531fe1e5-e3d5-5649-b313-13cd6d8f7f98" "answer-4893c99a-7136-5bd1-aef4-b6e90b029371" "answer-bf0fae8f-9d6c-5176-a980-fcb53f0d4c6e" "answer-6d0e9c64-8b2c-5046-9a49-1b2ece1ed25e" "answer-dde6aaf3-b2a8-57d1-a021-79d4beafe63c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-531fe1e5-e3d5-5649-b313-13cd6d8f7f98" :answer/id #uuid "531fe1e5-e3d5-5649-b313-13cd6d8f7f98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4893c99a-7136-5bd1-aef4-b6e90b029371" :answer/id #uuid "4893c99a-7136-5bd1-aef4-b6e90b029371" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf0fae8f-9d6c-5176-a980-fcb53f0d4c6e" :answer/id #uuid "bf0fae8f-9d6c-5176-a980-fcb53f0d4c6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d0e9c64-8b2c-5046-9a49-1b2ece1ed25e" :answer/id #uuid "6d0e9c64-8b2c-5046-9a49-1b2ece1ed25e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dde6aaf3-b2a8-57d1-a021-79d4beafe63c" :answer/id #uuid "dde6aaf3-b2a8-57d1-a021-79d4beafe63c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cc13bee1-bf2a-51a5-a80d-77b695b1c60b" :question/id #uuid "cc13bee1-bf2a-51a5-a80d-77b695b1c60b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337315" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=C,$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(C) \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)})}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5b2e9024-0792-5fc6-9ce4-361c190fbfa4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5b2e9024-0792-5fc6-9ce4-361c190fbfa4" :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2e9024-0792-5fc6-9ce4-361c190fbfa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6054dcca-ae62-54cf-9cdd-57ed58a6a320" :answer-field/choices ["answer-04836823-2fb1-5c3d-a0c5-77a932219b92" "answer-a8371a77-d019-545f-94c5-55f27b9ed753" "answer-0761bbe1-fc86-571b-915d-d390a147033c" "answer-6054dcca-ae62-54cf-9cdd-57ed58a6a320" "answer-fcb0edf3-56a1-540a-ba78-687280e03d34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04836823-2fb1-5c3d-a0c5-77a932219b92" :answer/id #uuid "04836823-2fb1-5c3d-a0c5-77a932219b92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8371a77-d019-545f-94c5-55f27b9ed753" :answer/id #uuid "a8371a77-d019-545f-94c5-55f27b9ed753" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0761bbe1-fc86-571b-915d-d390a147033c" :answer/id #uuid "0761bbe1-fc86-571b-915d-d390a147033c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6054dcca-ae62-54cf-9cdd-57ed58a6a320" :answer/id #uuid "6054dcca-ae62-54cf-9cdd-57ed58a6a320" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcb0edf3-56a1-540a-ba78-687280e03d34" :answer/id #uuid "fcb0edf3-56a1-540a-ba78-687280e03d34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f66e36cd-9bf6-56e0-b87a-016d297333d7" :question/id #uuid "f66e36cd-9bf6-56e0-b87a-016d297333d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-337313" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x}{y}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{y-x{y}^{′}}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ y-x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{x})}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd6a4d2e-c7d0-561a-ba37-0ecf2d6f5d87"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd6a4d2e-c7d0-561a-ba37-0ecf2d6f5d87" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd6a4d2e-c7d0-561a-ba37-0ecf2d6f5d87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8d206d52-d945-58d6-9564-2bab92e38fd5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d206d52-d945-58d6-9564-2bab92e38fd5" "answer-80e52af3-e585-5a0d-aa22-4ea4ca9a03f1" "answer-f1216dab-2435-53da-876f-2a411b9c344f" "answer-5310661c-c40f-57d8-961e-c3c723529252" "answer-e51a135b-6f9a-5f82-ace0-3594f1b83adf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d206d52-d945-58d6-9564-2bab92e38fd5" :answer/id #uuid "8d206d52-d945-58d6-9564-2bab92e38fd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80e52af3-e585-5a0d-aa22-4ea4ca9a03f1" :answer/id #uuid "80e52af3-e585-5a0d-aa22-4ea4ca9a03f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1216dab-2435-53da-876f-2a411b9c344f" :answer/id #uuid "f1216dab-2435-53da-876f-2a411b9c344f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5310661c-c40f-57d8-961e-c3c723529252" :answer/id #uuid "5310661c-c40f-57d8-961e-c3c723529252" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e51a135b-6f9a-5f82-ace0-3594f1b83adf" :answer/id #uuid "e51a135b-6f9a-5f82-ace0-3594f1b83adf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0166ce2f-2829-5697-886e-176606ebec4c" :question/id #uuid "0166ce2f-2829-5697-886e-176606ebec4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337311" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{C},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{C}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{2}y) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{1}{C}) \\\\ 2xy+{x}^{2}{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{′} & =-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-60260284-1b87-5a9c-95ee-efdd32c08bc6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-60260284-1b87-5a9c-95ee-efdd32c08bc6" :answer-field/id #uuid "60260284-1b87-5a9c-95ee-efdd32c08bc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-69daf29a-b23e-53d1-ac1f-ef8529b1e935" :answer-field/choices ["answer-711d3051-555f-5251-b3e7-5ba581b6dcac" "answer-dba0a80a-5b15-55dd-8f84-6cb26b9b34c7" "answer-17ac5bc2-5b2c-5952-9696-90205b58421a" "answer-69daf29a-b23e-53d1-ac1f-ef8529b1e935" "answer-121dd7b4-2544-54e9-b82c-91427f263603"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-711d3051-555f-5251-b3e7-5ba581b6dcac" :answer/id #uuid "711d3051-555f-5251-b3e7-5ba581b6dcac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dba0a80a-5b15-55dd-8f84-6cb26b9b34c7" :answer/id #uuid "dba0a80a-5b15-55dd-8f84-6cb26b9b34c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{2y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17ac5bc2-5b2c-5952-9696-90205b58421a" :answer/id #uuid "17ac5bc2-5b2c-5952-9696-90205b58421a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69daf29a-b23e-53d1-ac1f-ef8529b1e935" :answer/id #uuid "69daf29a-b23e-53d1-ac1f-ef8529b1e935" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-121dd7b4-2544-54e9-b82c-91427f263603" :answer/id #uuid "121dd7b4-2544-54e9-b82c-91427f263603" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-33804b3c-aea9-58d0-9654-8a84f729c14e" :question/id #uuid "33804b3c-aea9-58d0-9654-8a84f729c14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337573" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x}{y}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{y-x{y}^{′}}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ y-x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{x})}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-x)\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =-\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3854e6c47f28ce601ef2395379bea788e954c4e4057331772b5840cd7073237.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a2c26a07-893b-5eda-88b9-2f21c73d94f1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a2c26a07-893b-5eda-88b9-2f21c73d94f1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c26a07-893b-5eda-88b9-2f21c73d94f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c320f8ae-2bdc-5750-bd04-80ef25d8f08e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-01243280-a8f6-5503-94bb-a930a8a2f0b9" "answer-5e9be344-8141-5390-b7cb-280192fa2e76" "answer-850f976f-2f35-5bd0-8500-d963993c3749" "answer-c320f8ae-2bdc-5750-bd04-80ef25d8f08e" "answer-24e069a3-4467-5da5-aa50-4d23695fc6e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01243280-a8f6-5503-94bb-a930a8a2f0b9" :answer/id #uuid "01243280-a8f6-5503-94bb-a930a8a2f0b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=Ky" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e9be344-8141-5390-b7cb-280192fa2e76" :answer/id #uuid "5e9be344-8141-5390-b7cb-280192fa2e76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xy=K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-850f976f-2f35-5bd0-8500-d963993c3749" :answer/id #uuid "850f976f-2f35-5bd0-8500-d963993c3749" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=Kx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c320f8ae-2bdc-5750-bd04-80ef25d8f08e" :answer/id #uuid "c320f8ae-2bdc-5750-bd04-80ef25d8f08e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24e069a3-4467-5da5-aa50-4d23695fc6e5" :answer/id #uuid "24e069a3-4467-5da5-aa50-4d23695fc6e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}y=K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120268 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $Cy={e}^{2x},$ where $C\\ne 0$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $Cy={e}^{2x}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(Cy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({e}^{2x}) \\\\ C{y}^{′} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\nCy={e}^{2x}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y})}=2y.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{2y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{1}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-1 \\\\ \\int 2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-1)\\,dx \\\\ \\int 2y\\,dy & =-\\int dx \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x+K \\\\ {y}^{2}+x & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $Cy={e}^{2x},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+x=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e9/e941d35788f4aee837220962511ca537528b04dec9efaa7f956a4ee571e14189.png)\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ on every curve in the family, so slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186117747 :answer-field/correct "answer-a7c90516-6e2c-58c1-9c24-827c686e2047" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15dba9ae-15c6-5ce1-8ff4-9e54c7022329" "answer-a1e3e84b-ebf5-571e-96f7-0d88af45ad6a" "answer-a7c90516-6e2c-58c1-9c24-827c686e2047" "answer-a3fa4360-f70a-5a96-86a1-e097d08537de" "answer-765226f3-3037-5537-a83b-e10c4e998f7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15dba9ae-15c6-5ce1-8ff4-9e54c7022329" :answer/id #uuid "15dba9ae-15c6-5ce1-8ff4-9e54c7022329" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{y}^{2}+x=K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1e3e84b-ebf5-571e-96f7-0d88af45ad6a" :answer/id #uuid "a1e3e84b-ebf5-571e-96f7-0d88af45ad6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7c90516-6e2c-58c1-9c24-827c686e2047" :answer/id #uuid "a7c90516-6e2c-58c1-9c24-827c686e2047" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}+x=K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3fa4360-f70a-5a96-86a1-e097d08537de" :answer/id #uuid "a3fa4360-f70a-5a96-86a1-e097d08537de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}+{e}^{2x}=K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-765226f3-3037-5537-a83b-e10c4e998f7c" :answer/id #uuid "765226f3-3037-5537-a83b-e10c4e998f7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}-x=K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120982 :tutorial/content "Recall that a *one-parameter family* of curves in the $xy$-plane is defined by\n\n$$\nF(x,y,C) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ denotes the parameter.\n\nThe **orthogonal trajectories** of a one-parameter family of curves form another one-parameter family of curves such that every curve in one family intersects at right angles every curve in the other.\n\nFor example, circles $x^2+y^2=C^2$ (with parameter $C$) and lines through the origin $y=Kx$ (with parameter $K$) are orthogonal trajectories.\n\n![](images/ca/ca980af3c5db940a9ab95567dfaa1a69ea8afb9f4f42f772bb446df67df129b8.png)\n\nNow, let $F(x,y,C) = 0$ be a family of curves with parameter $C.$ Suppose we can\n\n- implicitly differentiate it with respect to $x,$ and\n\n- eliminate $C$ using the derived and defining equations,\n\nto get an equation in terms of $x,$ $y,$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$  Next, assume we can solve for the derivative and obtain a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nthat explicitly gives the slopes of the curves satisfying this ODE (at least locally, where the slope is defined). \n\nFor a curve to intersect at right angles with every curve in the family, its slope must be the negative reciprocal of the slope of curves in the family. So, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves must be the solutions of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = -\\dfrac{1}{f(x,y)}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** This is true only where $f(x,y)\\neq 0.$ If $f=0$, then original curves have slope $0$ (horizontal tangent), and orthogonal curves should have infinite slope (vertical tangent)." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120985 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy^3 = C^2,$ where $C\\neq0$ is a parameter.\n\nOrthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy^3 = C^2.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(x{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ {y}^{3}+3x{y}^{2}{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3x{y}^{2}{y}^{′} & =-{y}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope $y'$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\ny'_\\perp = -\\dfrac{1}{y'} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\dfrac{y}{3x}\\right)}\n= \\dfrac{3x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{3x}{y}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C \\neq 0,$ the given family has $x\\neq0$ and $y\\neq0,$ so both $y'$ and $y'_\\perp$ are well-defined." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120028 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186065721 :answer-field/choices 17592186065723] [:db/retract 17592186065721 :answer-field/choices 17592186065725] {:db/id 17592186069048 :question/problem "$\\frac{7x+1}{7x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator have no common factors, so the expression can not be reduced. The expression \n\n$$\n\\frac{7x+1}{7x}\n$$\n\n is already in lowest terms." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065721 :answer-field/correct "answer-2181c5f8-b9fc-5a90-b0ca-d4fcb9f73264" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d24eea38-ad0b-5c27-9ca0-b6fa84d4d26b" "answer-2181c5f8-b9fc-5a90-b0ca-d4fcb9f73264"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d24eea38-ad0b-5c27-9ca0-b6fa84d4d26b" :answer/id #uuid "d24eea38-ad0b-5c27-9ca0-b6fa84d4d26b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2181c5f8-b9fc-5a90-b0ca-d4fcb9f73264" :answer/id #uuid "2181c5f8-b9fc-5a90-b0ca-d4fcb9f73264" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7x+1}{7x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068805 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0dc91759-1708-5aa4-a78f-8164b2208179" "question-624c7b11-aded-592e-be21-f98c6f89890c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0dc91759-1708-5aa4-a78f-8164b2208179" :question/id #uuid "0dc91759-1708-5aa4-a78f-8164b2208179" :question/math-academy-id "q-51526" :question/problem "What is $\\frac{10g+30}{8g+24}$ reduced to its lowest terms?" :question/worked-solution "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10g+30}{8g+24} & =\\frac{5(2g+6)}{4(2g+6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cancel{(2g+6)}}{4\\cancel{(2g+6)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3dfd035-8faf-5e9c-8f1e-539bc75374da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3dfd035-8faf-5e9c-8f1e-539bc75374da" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3dfd035-8faf-5e9c-8f1e-539bc75374da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-87a0ab98-9dd0-5490-bcc8-385d1c51bb82" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5450829c-b0a3-5655-bd1c-5951e0c37b62" "answer-fcd329fc-b34a-5fbb-bd9a-1392f560a2ec" "answer-3886ddee-8772-528b-bf4d-8bf02e891107" "answer-afcad7b6-af4e-59d6-82fd-8b0d5cf543c9" "answer-87a0ab98-9dd0-5490-bcc8-385d1c51bb82"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5450829c-b0a3-5655-bd1c-5951e0c37b62" :answer/id #uuid "5450829c-b0a3-5655-bd1c-5951e0c37b62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2g+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcd329fc-b34a-5fbb-bd9a-1392f560a2ec" :answer/id #uuid "fcd329fc-b34a-5fbb-bd9a-1392f560a2ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3886ddee-8772-528b-bf4d-8bf02e891107" :answer/id #uuid "3886ddee-8772-528b-bf4d-8bf02e891107" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5(2g+6)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afcad7b6-af4e-59d6-82fd-8b0d5cf543c9" :answer/id #uuid "afcad7b6-af4e-59d6-82fd-8b0d5cf543c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5(2g+6)^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87a0ab98-9dd0-5490-bcc8-385d1c51bb82" :answer/id #uuid "87a0ab98-9dd0-5490-bcc8-385d1c51bb82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-624c7b11-aded-592e-be21-f98c6f89890c" :question/id #uuid "624c7b11-aded-592e-be21-f98c6f89890c" :question/math-academy-id "q-52367" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{2x-6}{3-x}?$" :question/worked-solution "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-6}{3-x} & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{-(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (x-3)}{(-1)\\cdot (x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(x-3)}}{(-1)\\cdot \\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d7f57eb-669c-54ed-8aa2-d787aac81274"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d7f57eb-669c-54ed-8aa2-d787aac81274" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d7f57eb-669c-54ed-8aa2-d787aac81274" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cbc47ca7-0390-52f9-aa36-ff5d3975d9c3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cbc47ca7-0390-52f9-aa36-ff5d3975d9c3" "answer-070118e9-d838-5a52-8939-3d392169bc42" "answer-ac77b55f-c27a-5257-a8fa-649823e99715" "answer-daa76a02-84a6-5425-b200-2e9ee6c38aef" "answer-05f4f1f7-644a-5080-b0cf-201bf0a40bc2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbc47ca7-0390-52f9-aa36-ff5d3975d9c3" :answer/id #uuid "cbc47ca7-0390-52f9-aa36-ff5d3975d9c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-070118e9-d838-5a52-8939-3d392169bc42" :answer/id #uuid "070118e9-d838-5a52-8939-3d392169bc42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac77b55f-c27a-5257-a8fa-649823e99715" :answer/id #uuid "ac77b55f-c27a-5257-a8fa-649823e99715" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daa76a02-84a6-5425-b200-2e9ee6c38aef" :answer/id #uuid "daa76a02-84a6-5425-b200-2e9ee6c38aef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3-x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05f4f1f7-644a-5080-b0cf-201bf0a40bc2" :answer/id #uuid "05f4f1f7-644a-5080-b0cf-201bf0a40bc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-ccf00ea8-24df-5710-b43a-322d9402d168" :activity/id #uuid "ccf00ea8-24df-5710-b43a-322d9402d168" :activity/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions by Factoring" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049181 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-06ab3711-3d4c-5c2c-8653-bca368f0f08b" "step-244bf9be-1869-5c01-9bef-c171796be681" "step-906a3f57-f0f9-58fa-b96b-4062bc57287a"] :activity/first-step "step-06ab3711-3d4c-5c2c-8653-bca368f0f08b" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-06ab3711-3d4c-5c2c-8653-bca368f0f08b" :step/id #uuid "06ab3711-3d4c-5c2c-8653-bca368f0f08b" :step/content 17592186069048 :step/next "step-244bf9be-1869-5c01-9bef-c171796be681" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-244bf9be-1869-5c01-9bef-c171796be681" :step/id #uuid "244bf9be-1869-5c01-9bef-c171796be681" :step/content "question-0dc91759-1708-5aa4-a78f-8164b2208179" :step/next "step-906a3f57-f0f9-58fa-b96b-4062bc57287a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-906a3f57-f0f9-58fa-b96b-4062bc57287a" :step/id #uuid "906a3f57-f0f9-58fa-b96b-4062bc57287a" :step/content "question-624c7b11-aded-592e-be21-f98c6f89890c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175936 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3623d58e-6308-55c7-b844-a67fd7315f21"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3623d58e-6308-55c7-b844-a67fd7315f21" :question/id #uuid "3623d58e-6308-55c7-b844-a67fd7315f21" :question/math-academy-id "q-134167" :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{1,\\,4,\\,5,\\,6\\},\\,y=\\{4,\\,5,\\,2,\\,1\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1+4+5+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (4+5+2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(1-4)(4-3)+(4-4)(5-3)+(5-4)(2-3)+(6-4)(1-3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(1)+(0)(2)+(1)(-1)+(2)(-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-3+0-1-4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa3053ff-8bf6-5a5f-a48f-e812f51a8989"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa3053ff-8bf6-5a5f-a48f-e812f51a8989" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa3053ff-8bf6-5a5f-a48f-e812f51a8989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2d9c6ba6-69a9-5ee8-9708-70c11ce21cc5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2d9c6ba6-69a9-5ee8-9708-70c11ce21cc5" "answer-74ac785e-85e2-5e6e-bc31-bcc9001443e9" "answer-31a13e4e-c027-5991-a7d1-412a4ff0c00e" "answer-decfb95f-7b91-5304-b64a-c323fdf22255" "answer-3f2b6eb8-c4d1-56b2-89dc-4001ee07443f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d9c6ba6-69a9-5ee8-9708-70c11ce21cc5" :answer/id #uuid "2d9c6ba6-69a9-5ee8-9708-70c11ce21cc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74ac785e-85e2-5e6e-bc31-bcc9001443e9" :answer/id #uuid "74ac785e-85e2-5e6e-bc31-bcc9001443e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31a13e4e-c027-5991-a7d1-412a4ff0c00e" :answer/id #uuid "31a13e4e-c027-5991-a7d1-412a4ff0c00e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-decfb95f-7b91-5304-b64a-c323fdf22255" :answer/id #uuid "decfb95f-7b91-5304-b64a-c323fdf22255" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f2b6eb8-c4d1-56b2-89dc-4001ee07443f" :answer/id #uuid "3f2b6eb8-c4d1-56b2-89dc-4001ee07443f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176333 :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{4,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,5\\},\\,y=\\{6,\\,3,\\,12,\\,11\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=2$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=8.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-2)(6-8)+(-1-2)(3-8)+(0-2)(12-8)+(5-2)(11-8)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2)(-2)+(-3)(-5)+(-2)(4)+(3)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-4+15-8+9] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268970 :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{6,\\,4,\\,6,\\,8\\},\\,y=\\{3,\\,4,\\,5,\\,8\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=5.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(6-6)(3-5)+(4-6)(4-5)+(6-6)(5-5)+(8-6)(8-5)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(0)(-2)+(-2)(-1)+(0)(0)+(2)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[0+2+0+6] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186275207 :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(3-4)(2-3)+(4-4)(2-3)+(4-4)(2-3)+(5-4)(6-3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(-1)+(0)(-1)+(0)(-1)+(1)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[1+0+0+3] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176335 :question/problem "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $6$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Education, in years ($y$)** | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages and corresponding years of formal education completed by each of Mark's cousins.\nCompute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=11$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=6.$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(6-11)(2-6)+(10-11)(4-6)+(12-11)(8-6)+(16-11)(10-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-5)(-4)+(-1)(-2)+(1)(2)+(5)(4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[20+2+2+20] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 44 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174268 :answer-field/correct 17592186174270 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176336 :question/problem "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight, in kg ($y$)** | $30$ | $40$ | $40$ | $50$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages and corresponding weights of four students. Compute the covariance of the data sets." :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (10+11+12+15) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 48 \\\\  & =12 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (30+40+40+50) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 160 \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(10\\,-\\,12)(30\\,-\\,40)+(11\\,-\\,12)(40\\,-\\,40)+(12\\,-\\,12)(40\\,-\\,40)+(15\\,-\\,12)(50\\,-\\,40)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-2)(-10)+(-1)(0)+(0)(0)+(3)(10)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[20+0+0+30] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 50 \\\\  & =12.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174274 :answer-field/correct 17592186174278 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268987 :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4\\,-\\,10)(64\\,-\\,75)+(8\\,-\\,10)(78\\,-\\,75)+(12\\,-\\,10)(76\\,-\\,75)+(16\\,-\\,10)(82\\,-\\,75)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-6)(-11)+(-2)(3)+(2)(1)+(6)(7)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[66-6+2+42] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 104 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176950 :tutorial/content "Given two finite data sets of equal length, say,\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\},\n$$\n\nthe **covariance** between $x$ and $y,$ denoted by $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ is a measure that evaluates the extent to which $x$ and $y$ change together.\n\n- A positive covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is also likely to increase:\n\n![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n\n- A negative covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is likely to decrease:\n\n![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\n- A covariance of zero (or close to zero) indicates that there is no *linear* relationship between $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/a5/a58fa4d40d77af23df86b5bd8eaca3119b93bc9ea97cc435eb4e53ba2777b071.png)\n\n \n\n**Watch Out!** A covariance of zero does *not* mean that $x$ and $y$ are not related. It simply means the relationship between them is not linear. To illustrate, consider the image below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4bdbf92e691174644dda9b9a84acb1b896c0436afb221501dc10b4635841539.png)\n\n \n\nThe (nonlinear) relationship shown above is $y=x^2.$ However, it can be shown that $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = 0$ for this data set.\n\nTo calculate $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ we use the following formula: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$  and $\\overline{y}$ denote the means of $x$ and $y$ respectively.\n\n**Note**: This formula for the covariance assumes that $x$ and $y$ are drawn from a *population*. There is a slightly different formula for covariance when $x$ and $y$ are sample data. However, we won't worry about the difference for now." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176135 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068693 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bd240e88-fbe1-5844-ad36-74f8bed7e2a0" "question-f088ab98-ea37-5733-acee-5703f82f16fe"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-bd240e88-fbe1-5844-ad36-74f8bed7e2a0" :question/id #uuid "bd240e88-fbe1-5844-ad36-74f8bed7e2a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-109537" :question/problem "Find, in point-slope form, the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-3,-1)$ and $(4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the point $(-3,-1),$ so we can use $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-3,-1)$ in our point-slope formula. All that remains is to compute $m,$ and we can do this using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{2-(-1)}{4-(-3)}=\\frac{2+1}{4+3}=\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=\\frac{3}{7}$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-3,-1)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =\\frac{3}{7}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{3}{7}(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5537ccfc-a1e3-5cdc-8038-6152a735dd0b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5537ccfc-a1e3-5cdc-8038-6152a735dd0b" :answer-field/id #uuid "5537ccfc-a1e3-5cdc-8038-6152a735dd0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-afaf01af-827d-5890-8956-ef74d5697985" :answer-field/choices ["answer-afaf01af-827d-5890-8956-ef74d5697985" "answer-4fa5e41e-58fd-5a9c-8a80-07017c5e334c" "answer-4bf048cb-a4c7-5d36-91ca-c83dcdce6dc4" "answer-1769de88-881d-5956-99f5-096e2c3b7e1e" "answer-b7920225-86ca-5473-953d-d1acecfdfacd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afaf01af-827d-5890-8956-ef74d5697985" :answer/id #uuid "afaf01af-827d-5890-8956-ef74d5697985" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y+1=\\frac{3}{7}(x+3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fa5e41e-58fd-5a9c-8a80-07017c5e334c" :answer/id #uuid "4fa5e41e-58fd-5a9c-8a80-07017c5e334c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y+3=3(x+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bf048cb-a4c7-5d36-91ca-c83dcdce6dc4" :answer/id #uuid "4bf048cb-a4c7-5d36-91ca-c83dcdce6dc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y+3=\\frac{3}{7}(x-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1769de88-881d-5956-99f5-096e2c3b7e1e" :answer/id #uuid "1769de88-881d-5956-99f5-096e2c3b7e1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-1=3(x-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7920225-86ca-5473-953d-d1acecfdfacd" :answer/id #uuid "b7920225-86ca-5473-953d-d1acecfdfacd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-1=\\frac{3}{7}(x+3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f088ab98-ea37-5733-acee-5703f82f16fe" :question/id #uuid "f088ab98-ea37-5733-acee-5703f82f16fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-109542" :question/problem "Find, in point-slope form, the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-1,4)$ and $(5,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the point $(-1,4),$ so we can use $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,4)$ in our point-slope formula. All that remains is to compute $m,$ and we can do this using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{2-4}{5-(-1)}=\\frac{-2}{6}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,4)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-58cdb945-4167-5cc0-adcc-654b4ba82f97"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-58cdb945-4167-5cc0-adcc-654b4ba82f97" :answer-field/id #uuid "58cdb945-4167-5cc0-adcc-654b4ba82f97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fbd0be6f-c3bd-5524-aac0-617dc0483f3a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f0e8229-9e05-5b49-a5bc-1c064268bc9a" "answer-81218ca0-1739-582b-ab48-ef0aaec2d811" "answer-e304c12c-7b3f-5ccf-8257-ced519e507f9" "answer-fbd0be6f-c3bd-5524-aac0-617dc0483f3a" "answer-70ebac15-8dee-54b4-967e-d6e875941a64"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f0e8229-9e05-5b49-a5bc-1c064268bc9a" :answer/id #uuid "0f0e8229-9e05-5b49-a5bc-1c064268bc9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y+1=3(x-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81218ca0-1739-582b-ab48-ef0aaec2d811" :answer/id #uuid "81218ca0-1739-582b-ab48-ef0aaec2d811" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y+4=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e304c12c-7b3f-5ccf-8257-ced519e507f9" :answer/id #uuid "e304c12c-7b3f-5ccf-8257-ced519e507f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-1=-\\frac{1}{3}(x-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbd0be6f-c3bd-5524-aac0-617dc0483f3a" :answer/id #uuid "fbd0be6f-c3bd-5524-aac0-617dc0483f3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-4=-\\frac{1}{3}(x+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70ebac15-8dee-54b4-967e-d6e875941a64" :answer/id #uuid "70ebac15-8dee-54b4-967e-d6e875941a64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-4=3(x+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068694 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d43f31da-b163-56de-90b2-998690e787ff"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d43f31da-b163-56de-90b2-998690e787ff" :question/id #uuid "d43f31da-b163-56de-90b2-998690e787ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-109573" :question/problem "Calculate the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-5,3)$ and $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we will construct the point-slope formula for the line. Then, we will simplify the point-slope formula into slope-intercept form.\n\nWe start by computing the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{-1-3}{-1-(-5)}=\\frac{-4}{4}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=-1$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-5,3)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-(x-(-5)) \\\\ y-3 & =-(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the equation into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-(x+5) \\\\ y-3 & =-x-5 \\\\ y & =-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8edbb288-1d9d-5e4c-bfd5-15eba8ff9406"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8edbb288-1d9d-5e4c-bfd5-15eba8ff9406" :answer-field/id #uuid "8edbb288-1d9d-5e4c-bfd5-15eba8ff9406" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a044491e-9316-5e2b-828a-80ff38d98c4f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b1e543ad-cf33-5fd3-921e-3361e3489906" "answer-a044491e-9316-5e2b-828a-80ff38d98c4f" "answer-0a1f86cd-0086-5765-9ba9-5e0bcefb0ed1" "answer-bf69e2dc-5d68-5e7a-8fda-ed88c91e4da7" "answer-e78fada1-45c2-57a2-a158-de8b1d290184"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1e543ad-cf33-5fd3-921e-3361e3489906" :answer/id #uuid "b1e543ad-cf33-5fd3-921e-3361e3489906" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-x+7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a044491e-9316-5e2b-828a-80ff38d98c4f" :answer/id #uuid "a044491e-9316-5e2b-828a-80ff38d98c4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a1f86cd-0086-5765-9ba9-5e0bcefb0ed1" :answer/id #uuid "0a1f86cd-0086-5765-9ba9-5e0bcefb0ed1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-x-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf69e2dc-5d68-5e7a-8fda-ed88c91e4da7" :answer/id #uuid "bf69e2dc-5d68-5e7a-8fda-ed88c91e4da7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e78fada1-45c2-57a2-a158-de8b1d290184" :answer/id #uuid "e78fada1-45c2-57a2-a158-de8b1d290184" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-6e99407a-bbb3-5be2-9242-96bf986482bc" :activity/id #uuid "6e99407a-bbb3-5be2-9242-96bf986482bc" :activity/title "Equations of Lines in Point-Slope Form" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 175921