d/choices 17592186228563] [:db/retract 17592186228561 :answer-field/choices 17592186228564] [:db/retract 17592186228561 :answer-field/choices 17592186228565] [:db/retract 17592186228561 :answer-field/choices 17592186228566] [:db/retract 17592186228371 :answer-field/choices 17592186228372] [:db/retract 17592186228371 :answer-field/choices 17592186228373] [:db/retract 17592186228371 :answer-field/choices 17592186228374] [:db/retract 17592186228371 :answer-field/choices 17592186228375] [:db/retract 17592186228371 :answer-field/choices 17592186228376] [:db/retract 17592186229693 :question/answer-fields 17592186228339] {:db/id 17592186229181 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-23f15773-7029-5dc4-9ea6-d17c6a8daaa9" "question-63623e2d-9a1a-53b1-a242-66f71be1ef8a" "question-554f59f6-c41f-55e2-8f84-8c2aab7670ec"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229182 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-11c8e0ff-5f12-56c5-bbf7-d578f71b8d7f" "question-d0340e70-b947-5b25-8e24-a63ff0f87f38"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229183 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0b0e00ac-15e0-50ff-896e-2fb3f25eb38a" "question-c3e80580-6c31-591c-a6d6-df75a9aede3a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229724 :question/problem "A wastewater basin initially holds $750\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved pollutant.\nA solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved pollutant is pumped into the basin at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of pollutant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the basin $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of pollutant in the basin at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which pollutant enters the basin minus the rate at which pollutant leaves the basin.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the basin.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the pollutant enters the basin at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *pollutant* enters the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the basin.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the basin remains $750\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of pollutant in the basin, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *pollutant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{Q}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=0.9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{25}\\alpha =0.9\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =22.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=22.5.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25}+22.5.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved pollutant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/25}+22.5 & =6 \\\\ A & =-16.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=22.5-16.5{e}^{-t/25}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228561 :answer-field/correct "answer-dd85ac5a-dd49-59af-aa19-5e740d5694bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e796a362-8cfa-568a-9c64-27bf196de4b1" "answer-98ea8df2-9a57-5710-b7c3-195b889f7f28" "answer-dd85ac5a-dd49-59af-aa19-5e740d5694bc" "answer-e7d824a3-01e9-5812-b58b-a58e01e5be71" "answer-f48f1195-a2b9-5291-9be2-abc8416f3fc7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e796a362-8cfa-568a-9c64-27bf196de4b1" :answer/id #uuid "e796a362-8cfa-568a-9c64-27bf196de4b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=22.5+16.5{e}^{-t/25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98ea8df2-9a57-5710-b7c3-195b889f7f28" :answer/id #uuid "98ea8df2-9a57-5710-b7c3-195b889f7f28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=22.5-16.5{e}^{-t/20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd85ac5a-dd49-59af-aa19-5e740d5694bc" :answer/id #uuid "dd85ac5a-dd49-59af-aa19-5e740d5694bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=22.5-16.5{e}^{-t/25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7d824a3-01e9-5812-b58b-a58e01e5be71" :answer/id #uuid "e7d824a3-01e9-5812-b58b-a58e01e5be71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=22.5-16.5{e}^{t/25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f48f1195-a2b9-5291-9be2-abc8416f3fc7" :answer/id #uuid "f48f1195-a2b9-5291-9be2-abc8416f3fc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=6-22.5{e}^{-t/25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-23f15773-7029-5dc4-9ea6-d17c6a8daaa9" :question/id #uuid "23f15773-7029-5dc4-9ea6-d17c6a8daaa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-336752" :question/problem "A mixing tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleanser.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved cleanser is pumped into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of cleanser $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of cleanser in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which cleanser enters the tank minus the rate at which cleanser leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleanser enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *cleanser* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $600\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of cleanser in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *cleanser* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{40}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{40}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=24.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40}+24.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleanser. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/40}+24 & =10 \\\\ A & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=24-14{e}^{-t/40}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cda88114-20cf-5991-bcbf-f77da4f62dbd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cda88114-20cf-5991-bcbf-f77da4f62dbd" :answer-field/id #uuid "cda88114-20cf-5991-bcbf-f77da4f62dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a2495ea6-828d-5486-8e26-3bd05d9010fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a8cf9da-4b94-562b-ad43-2abfa2d2e1bf" "answer-31248321-fb90-5cb0-aa5a-920f043c50d2" "answer-a2495ea6-828d-5486-8e26-3bd05d9010fc" "answer-012bbc31-fce8-5143-bbdb-f134d11c9b2e" "answer-3ed776e2-31c7-5d6e-9bbc-6f6a10752d8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a8cf9da-4b94-562b-ad43-2abfa2d2e1bf" :answer/id #uuid "6a8cf9da-4b94-562b-ad43-2abfa2d2e1bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=-2+12{e}^{-t/40}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31248321-fb90-5cb0-aa5a-920f043c50d2" :answer/id #uuid "31248321-fb90-5cb0-aa5a-920f043c50d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=-20+30{e}^{t/40}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2495ea6-828d-5486-8e26-3bd05d9010fc" :answer/id #uuid "a2495ea6-828d-5486-8e26-3bd05d9010fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=24-14{e}^{-t/40}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-012bbc31-fce8-5143-bbdb-f134d11c9b2e" :answer/id #uuid "012bbc31-fce8-5143-bbdb-f134d11c9b2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=25-15{e}^{-t/40}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ed776e2-31c7-5d6e-9bbc-6f6a10752d8e" :answer/id #uuid "3ed776e2-31c7-5d6e-9bbc-6f6a10752d8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=30-20{e}^{-t/40}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-63623e2d-9a1a-53b1-a242-66f71be1ef8a" :question/id #uuid "63623e2d-9a1a-53b1-a242-66f71be1ef8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339383" :question/problem "A pharmaceutical mixer initially holds $300\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $1\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved medication.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved medication is pumped into the mixer at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of medication $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the mixer $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of medication in the mixer at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which medication enters the mixer minus the rate at which medication leaves the mixer.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the mixer.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the medication enters the mixer at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *medication* enters the mixer is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the mixer.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the mixer remains $300\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of medication in the mixer, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *medication* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=12.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+12.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the mixer initially contains $1\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved medication. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+12 & =1 \\\\ A & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12-11{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-efdf6499-b332-5612-bc05-cf92d925d07f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-efdf6499-b332-5612-bc05-cf92d925d07f" :answer-field/id #uuid "efdf6499-b332-5612-bc05-cf92d925d07f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4f46af42-cc7d-5f1f-afb2-f63457bfad29" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc8273dc-02bc-5c58-83c8-72b74f957f97" "answer-578ba4bb-a618-5ab5-8b97-78550b74205b" "answer-13f66fb4-40cf-56c6-b199-8c73d618fc37" "answer-4f46af42-cc7d-5f1f-afb2-f63457bfad29" "answer-c413a31a-8d21-5344-817f-3bab2b829337"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc8273dc-02bc-5c58-83c8-72b74f957f97" :answer/id #uuid "bc8273dc-02bc-5c58-83c8-72b74f957f97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=1-12{e}^{-t/20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-578ba4bb-a618-5ab5-8b97-78550b74205b" :answer/id #uuid "578ba4bb-a618-5ab5-8b97-78550b74205b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=12+11{e}^{-t/20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13f66fb4-40cf-56c6-b199-8c73d618fc37" :answer/id #uuid "13f66fb4-40cf-56c6-b199-8c73d618fc37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=12-11{e}^{-t/15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f46af42-cc7d-5f1f-afb2-f63457bfad29" :answer/id #uuid "4f46af42-cc7d-5f1f-afb2-f63457bfad29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=12-11{e}^{-t/20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c413a31a-8d21-5344-817f-3bab2b829337" :answer/id #uuid "c413a31a-8d21-5344-817f-3bab2b829337" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q(t)=12-11{e}^{t/20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-554f59f6-c41f-55e2-8f84-8c2aab7670ec" :question/id #uuid "554f59f6-c41f-55e2-8f84-8c2aab7670ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-336736" :question/problem "A mixing tank initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleaning agent.\nA feed solution containing $0.08\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleaning agent is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of cleaning agent in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of cleaning agent in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which cleaning agents enter the tank minus the rate at which cleaning agents leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which cleaning agents enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.08\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleaning agent enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *cleaning agent* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.08\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.08\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which cleaning agents exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $400\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of cleaning agent in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *cleaning agent* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=1.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =1.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =32.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=32.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+32.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleaning agent. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+32 & =6 \\\\ A & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=32-26{e}^{-t/20}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of cleaning agent in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=32-26{e}^{-5/20}≈11.75\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3f84c734-a5ae-5ecb-9eb0-7a39a117167f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3f84c734-a5ae-5ecb-9eb0-7a39a117167f" :answer-field/id #uuid "3f84c734-a5ae-5ecb-9eb0-7a39a117167f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-04076803-eae5-51dd-adf5-ffaa457974c6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ffc5fe1b-7116-5783-a523-4a7779acf88d" "answer-d5adba53-2823-56b8-b86a-d86b90f0927a" "answer-04076803-eae5-51dd-adf5-ffaa457974c6" "answer-da3ec5e9-ce8a-5063-b326-988d95b5ddd3" "answer-8a602200-48ad-53f0-8143-6fe9792d4181"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffc5fe1b-7116-5783-a523-4a7779acf88d" :answer/id #uuid "ffc5fe1b-7116-5783-a523-4a7779acf88d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10.94\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5adba53-2823-56b8-b86a-d86b90f0927a" :answer/id #uuid "d5adba53-2823-56b8-b86a-d86b90f0927a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11.32\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04076803-eae5-51dd-adf5-ffaa457974c6" :answer/id #uuid "04076803-eae5-51dd-adf5-ffaa457974c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11.75\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da3ec5e9-ce8a-5063-b326-988d95b5ddd3" :answer/id #uuid "da3ec5e9-ce8a-5063-b326-988d95b5ddd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.18\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a602200-48ad-53f0-8143-6fe9792d4181" :answer/id #uuid "8a602200-48ad-53f0-8143-6fe9792d4181" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.63\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229698 :question/problem "A storage tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved stabilizer.\nA feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the stabilizer is pumped into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of stabilizer in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the stabilizer enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *stabilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $15\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n600+15t-10t=(600+5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of stabilizer in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *stabilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(120+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|120+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(120+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(120+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(120+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q) & =(120+t)^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (120+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(120+t)Q & =0.6(120+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2}) & =0.6(120+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(120+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(120+t)^{2} & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(120+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)+\\frac{C}{(120+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of stabilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ 0.2(120+0)+\\frac{C}{(120+0)^{2}} & =5 \\\\ 24+\\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =-19 \\\\ C & =-19(120)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)-\\frac{19(120)^{2}}{(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.2(120+t)-19{(\\frac{120}{120+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(10) & =0.2(120+10)-19{(\\frac{120}{120+10})}^{2} \\\\  & =26-19{(\\frac{12}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈9.81\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228371 :answer-field/correct "answer-23c67163-5885-5331-b6df-e6f1abd577ec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eb7cb287-8d09-5c98-9bf7-3d208192ac9a" "answer-14f59116-2d5c-52e2-9b35-7f32ef8ca9aa" "answer-df01cbb2-ff59-5cab-a987-8313d051432e" "answer-132d6c9e-eb68-5d65-8d72-ee64a266345d" "answer-23c67163-5885-5331-b6df-e6f1abd577ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb7cb287-8d09-5c98-9bf7-3d208192ac9a" :answer/id #uuid "eb7cb287-8d09-5c98-9bf7-3d208192ac9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.99\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14f59116-2d5c-52e2-9b35-7f32ef8ca9aa" :answer/id #uuid "14f59116-2d5c-52e2-9b35-7f32ef8ca9aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8.21\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df01cbb2-ff59-5cab-a987-8313d051432e" :answer/id #uuid "df01cbb2-ff59-5cab-a987-8313d051432e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8.63\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-132d6c9e-eb68-5d65-8d72-ee64a266345d" :answer/id #uuid "132d6c9e-eb68-5d65-8d72-ee64a266345d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9.18\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23c67163-5885-5331-b6df-e6f1abd577ec" :answer/id #uuid "23c67163-5885-5331-b6df-e6f1abd577ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9.81\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-11c8e0ff-5f12-56c5-bbf7-d578f71b8d7f" :question/id #uuid "11c8e0ff-5f12-56c5-bbf7-d578f71b8d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336746" :question/problem "A rinse tank initially holds $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved dye is pumped into the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water; a solution enters at $16\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+16t-24t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q) & =(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (30-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(30-t)^{-4}Q & =0.4(30-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3}) & =0.4(30-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(30-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-3} & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(30-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+C(30-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=9,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =9 \\\\ C(30-0)^{3}+0.2(30-0) & =9 \\\\ C(30)^{3}+6 & =9 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}(30-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 30\\,(\\frac{30-t}{30})+3{(\\frac{30-t}{30})}^{3} \\\\  & =6\\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+3{(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =6,\\beta =3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =6-3=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f525641-4d00-5cf1-b519-6a6c8d3e8e86"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f525641-4d00-5cf1-b519-6a6c8d3e8e86" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f525641-4d00-5cf1-b519-6a6c8d3e8e86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e5345499-fd39-5582-a566-851fc68ed4e8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0fa46099-da71-5b6e-bf88-ae9c1cd9cdcf" "answer-3c64925c-c3fb-50bc-a4cf-26896798c5a0" "answer-e5345499-fd39-5582-a566-851fc68ed4e8" "answer-79afc2f1-79a1-5645-98b6-d23030be32d0" "answer-3a8aabfa-3b2a-546d-a525-0b6c5247d515"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fa46099-da71-5b6e-bf88-ae9c1cd9cdcf" :answer/id #uuid "0fa46099-da71-5b6e-bf88-ae9c1cd9cdcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c64925c-c3fb-50bc-a4cf-26896798c5a0" :answer/id #uuid "3c64925c-c3fb-50bc-a4cf-26896798c5a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5345499-fd39-5582-a566-851fc68ed4e8" :answer/id #uuid "e5345499-fd39-5582-a566-851fc68ed4e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79afc2f1-79a1-5645-98b6-d23030be32d0" :answer/id #uuid "79afc2f1-79a1-5645-98b6-d23030be32d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a8aabfa-3b2a-546d-a525-0b6c5247d515" :answer/id #uuid "3a8aabfa-3b2a-546d-a525-0b6c5247d515" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d0340e70-b947-5b25-8e24-a63ff0f87f38" :question/id #uuid "d0340e70-b947-5b25-8e24-a63ff0f87f38" :question/math-academy-id "q-339403" :question/problem "A wastewater treatment basin initially holds $320\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $2.2\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved neutralizing agent.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the agent is pumped into the basin at a rate of $8\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of agent in the basin $5$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of agent in the basin at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which agent enters the tank minus the rate at which agent leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which agent enters the basin.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the agent enters the basin at a rate of $8\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *agent* enters the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which agent exits the basin.\n\nTo find the rate at which agent leaves the basin, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the basin at time $t.$\n\nThe basin initially contains $320\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $8\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $4\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the basin after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n320+8t-4t=(320+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of agent in the basin, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{L/min},$ *agent* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{80+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{Q}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80+t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1/(80+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|} \\\\  & =80+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=80+t$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(80+t)\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80+t}Q) & =(80+t)\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (80+t)\\frac{dQ}{dt}+Q & =0.4(80+t) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(80+t)) & =0.4(80+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(80+t))\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(80+t)\\,dt \\\\ Q(80+t) & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(80+t)^{2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(80+t)+\\frac{C}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $2.2\\,\\text{kg}$ of agent. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=2.2,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =2.2 \\\\ 0.2(80+0)+\\frac{C}{80+0} & =2.2 \\\\ 16+\\frac{C}{80} & =2.2 \\\\ \\frac{C}{80} & =-13.8 \\\\ C & =-1104\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(80+t)-\\frac{1104}{80+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of agent in the basin $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(5) & =0.2(80+5)-\\frac{1104}{80+5} \\\\  & =17-\\frac{1104}{85} \\\\  & ≈4.01\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-71c957ef-ec71-5945-b112-c39defc2facc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-71c957ef-ec71-5945-b112-c39defc2facc" :answer-field/id #uuid "71c957ef-ec71-5945-b112-c39defc2facc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a0b4fa8a-26bd-5a01-bcb0-d0ebf5922738" :answer-field/choices ["answer-409f3cff-59d6-5382-900e-2722da60aa57" "answer-5688b277-ef03-5eaf-8c63-160fb654c7c9" "answer-a0b4fa8a-26bd-5a01-bcb0-d0ebf5922738" "answer-19631a4f-fc75-5f16-94d0-c840626309aa" "answer-5f94b143-c3c7-545e-847d-30ec918b7ae6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-409f3cff-59d6-5382-900e-2722da60aa57" :answer/id #uuid "409f3cff-59d6-5382-900e-2722da60aa57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.09\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5688b277-ef03-5eaf-8c63-160fb654c7c9" :answer/id #uuid "5688b277-ef03-5eaf-8c63-160fb654c7c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.62\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0b4fa8a-26bd-5a01-bcb0-d0ebf5922738" :answer/id #uuid "a0b4fa8a-26bd-5a01-bcb0-d0ebf5922738" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.01\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19631a4f-fc75-5f16-94d0-c840626309aa" :answer/id #uuid "19631a4f-fc75-5f16-94d0-c840626309aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.48\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f94b143-c3c7-545e-847d-30ec918b7ae6" :answer/id #uuid "5f94b143-c3c7-545e-847d-30ec918b7ae6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.90\\,\\text{kg}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0b0e00ac-15e0-50ff-896e-2fb3f25eb38a" :question/id #uuid "0b0e00ac-15e0-50ff-896e-2fb3f25eb38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336519" :question/problem "An ink reservoir for an industrial printer can hold up to $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of ink solution with $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved ink dye. A more concentrated ink solution with concentration $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir minus the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of ink dye enters the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *ink dye* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+9t-6t=3(20+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *ink dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(20+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(20+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(20+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q) & =(20+t)^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (20+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(20+t)Q & =12(20+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2}) & =12(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 12(20+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(20+t)^{2} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(20+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(20+t)+\\frac{C}{(20+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of ink dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 4(20+0)+\\frac{C}{(20+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 80+\\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{20}^{2}} & =-62 \\\\ C & =-62(20)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(20+t)-\\frac{62(20)^{2}}{(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =4(20+t)-62{(\\frac{20}{20+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 60+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 60+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =90 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =4(20+30)-62{(\\frac{20}{20+30})}^{2} \\\\  & =200-62{(\\frac{20}{50})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈190.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2eaf0f19-6003-5585-a879-f271b5fc12a9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2eaf0f19-6003-5585-a879-f271b5fc12a9" :answer-field/id #uuid "2eaf0f19-6003-5585-a879-f271b5fc12a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-727ecad7-0669-59e4-acfa-630e5b405f52" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60afaad7-5c74-5ffe-abde-22de9074b994" "answer-87cd631b-b970-5565-be2b-b669ed3ae06f" "answer-727ecad7-0669-59e4-acfa-630e5b405f52" "answer-cc782d2e-ff11-5ac5-a0c7-6ccf48af546c" "answer-e50bcdd8-481a-5a75-a0f4-6d4a10f0bb53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60afaad7-5c74-5ffe-abde-22de9074b994" :answer/id #uuid "60afaad7-5c74-5ffe-abde-22de9074b994" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "189.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87cd631b-b970-5565-be2b-b669ed3ae06f" :answer/id #uuid "87cd631b-b970-5565-be2b-b669ed3ae06f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "189.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-727ecad7-0669-59e4-acfa-630e5b405f52" :answer/id #uuid "727ecad7-0669-59e4-acfa-630e5b405f52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "190.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc782d2e-ff11-5ac5-a0c7-6ccf48af546c" :answer/id #uuid "cc782d2e-ff11-5ac5-a0c7-6ccf48af546c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "190.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e50bcdd8-481a-5a75-a0f4-6d4a10f0bb53" :answer/id #uuid "e50bcdd8-481a-5a75-a0f4-6d4a10f0bb53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "190.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c3e80580-6c31-591c-a6d6-df75a9aede3a" :question/id #uuid "c3e80580-6c31-591c-a6d6-df75a9aede3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336503" :question/problem "A fertilizer tank for an irrigation system can hold up to $210\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $90\\,\\text{gal}$ of fertilizer solution with $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved fertilizer. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of fertilizer enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *fertilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $90\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=90+18t-12t=6(15+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *fertilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(15+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|15+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(15+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(15+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q) & =(15+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (15+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(15+t)Q & =9(15+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2}) & =9(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(15+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(15+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(15+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(15+t)+\\frac{C}{(15+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of fertilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 3(15+0)+\\frac{C}{(15+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 45+\\frac{C}{(15)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{15}^{2}} & =-27 \\\\ C & =-27(15)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(15+t)-\\frac{27(15)^{2}}{(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(15+t)-27{(\\frac{15}{15+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $210\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=210$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =210 \\\\ 90+18{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 90+6{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =20\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(20) & =3(15+20)-27{(\\frac{15}{15+20})}^{2} \\\\  & =105-27{(\\frac{15}{35})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈100.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-507a90fb-d7f1-59f5-8df1-dc619644c3da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-507a90fb-d7f1-59f5-8df1-dc619644c3da" :answer-field/id #uuid "507a90fb-d7f1-59f5-8df1-dc619644c3da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f1dcaab2-0d10-522e-bd0f-9e9fdbc1a915" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f1dcaab2-0d10-522e-bd0f-9e9fdbc1a915" "answer-5cc4d99a-a70c-5a49-aae0-f3604e67d27f" "answer-0112f1fc-6de5-513e-87f9-3b66ed92fd30" "answer-26fdd352-98f8-5049-9bca-4712271dc3ef" "answer-c69e4261-0557-5f97-9e81-25b3bb4c21af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1dcaab2-0d10-522e-bd0f-9e9fdbc1a915" :answer/id #uuid "f1dcaab2-0d10-522e-bd0f-9e9fdbc1a915" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cc4d99a-a70c-5a49-aae0-f3604e67d27f" :answer/id #uuid "5cc4d99a-a70c-5a49-aae0-f3604e67d27f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0112f1fc-6de5-513e-87f9-3b66ed92fd30" :answer/id #uuid "0112f1fc-6de5-513e-87f9-3b66ed92fd30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26fdd352-98f8-5049-9bca-4712271dc3ef" :answer/id #uuid "26fdd352-98f8-5049-9bca-4712271dc3ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "99.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c69e4261-0557-5f97-9e81-25b3bb4c21af" :answer/id #uuid "c69e4261-0557-5f97-9e81-25b3bb4c21af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "99.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230205 :tutorial/content "In the previous lesson, we focused on dilution problems in which the incoming solution contained none of the substance being modeled. In those cases, the rate at which the substance entered the container was zero.\n\nIn many practical situations, however, the incoming solution *does* contain some amount of the substance. When this happens,\n\n- the rate of entry is no longer zero, and\n\n- the resulting differential equation takes the form of a first-order linear differential equation.\n\nFor example, suppose a storage tank initially holds $650\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}$ of dissolved chlorine.\n\n- A solution containing $20\\,\\textrm{g/L}$ of dissolved chlorine is pumped into the tank at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\n- At the same time, the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nTo construct the model for the amount of chlorine $Q$ in the tank, we compare the rates of chlorine entering and leaving. The derivative \n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which chlorine enters the tank minus the rate at which chlorine leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}}\n$$\n\nWe compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine enters the tank.\n\nThe rate of chlorine entering is the concentration times the flow rate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =100\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine exits the tank.\n\nSince the volume remains constant at $650\\,\\textrm{L},$ the concentration in the tank is $\\dfrac{Q}{650}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{L}}.$ So, the rate of chlorine exiting is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{130}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe net rate of change $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out}$ is the difference between the rate in and the rate out. Combining this with the initial condition $Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g},$ we can state the complete initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 100 - \\dfrac{1}{130}Q\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{130}Q = 100, \\quad Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229261 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092338 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-33d9e7d3-a57a-5882-990d-c4b03eec3609" "question-658d443c-02c1-52f6-a2b6-cd97f500f835"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-221288b9-d358-51b3-a7a7-1f68c1f5fbe9" "question-95e868f8-4928-5f0b-9ec7-afed42df8e19"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-33d9e7d3-a57a-5882-990d-c4b03eec3609" :question/id #uuid "33d9e7d3-a57a-5882-990d-c4b03eec3609" :question/math-academy-id "q-174358" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bbd8b82211b2b3a19935aae010e15083ce3553984473444ae6f7a720b682d7f5.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{p,q,r,s,t\\}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $q$ and $t$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{q,t\\}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dbb8e7c2-0363-5cb5-8d8a-c3481b00ae75"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dbb8e7c2-0363-5cb5-8d8a-c3481b00ae75" :answer-field/id #uuid "dbb8e7c2-0363-5cb5-8d8a-c3481b00ae75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a06e7d00-9ae9-55f2-aa6a-f5a68db01aa3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-867d0a72-1046-54b9-b414-cd5dbfa660a8" "answer-2438f833-da6b-51c4-8043-ebd69eed10a6" "answer-a06e7d00-9ae9-55f2-aa6a-f5a68db01aa3" "answer-778d7d49-8554-5fbf-a462-170fd01ecb05" "answer-b21165e0-d461-5c86-99ff-1d2317a6798b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-867d0a72-1046-54b9-b414-cd5dbfa660a8" :answer/id #uuid "867d0a72-1046-54b9-b414-cd5dbfa660a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{p,r,s\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2438f833-da6b-51c4-8043-ebd69eed10a6" :answer/id #uuid "2438f833-da6b-51c4-8043-ebd69eed10a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{p,r\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a06e7d00-9ae9-55f2-aa6a-f5a68db01aa3" :answer/id #uuid "a06e7d00-9ae9-55f2-aa6a-f5a68db01aa3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{q,t\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-778d7d49-8554-5fbf-a462-170fd01ecb05" :answer/id #uuid "778d7d49-8554-5fbf-a462-170fd01ecb05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{r,q,t\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b21165e0-d461-5c86-99ff-1d2317a6798b" :answer/id #uuid "b21165e0-d461-5c86-99ff-1d2317a6798b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{r,s\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-658d443c-02c1-52f6-a2b6-cd97f500f835" :question/id #uuid "658d443c-02c1-52f6-a2b6-cd97f500f835" :question/math-academy-id "q-174377" :question/problem "Which of the following is the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of the subset $A=\\{3,5\\}$ of $X=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe indicator function of the set $\\{3,5\\}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed92f46f57aefc4d9c7445f044f1b35adb89950c16f331aff35c02445b607c95.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $3$ and $5$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5206336a-5882-5861-9285-fb57b9ddc1e6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5206336a-5882-5861-9285-fb57b9ddc1e6" :answer-field/id #uuid "5206336a-5882-5861-9285-fb57b9ddc1e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d8e7f2fa-a383-5a2b-a245-5265b3274d11" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9998f62b-688a-5861-b477-715c397c2226" "answer-edf8748d-62d5-5dd2-b2cf-7abe86aa36a2" "answer-934bef13-4408-5aeb-90a6-87735671fcd5" "answer-51e212d4-4747-53c2-a3de-6c59d58b03a4" "answer-d8e7f2fa-a383-5a2b-a245-5265b3274d11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9998f62b-688a-5861-b477-715c397c2226" :answer/id #uuid "9998f62b-688a-5861-b477-715c397c2226" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/00/00fcd5c1eaef4303436e7abb8f5fba76aae7c83cf24a857e09130646c4f6e987.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edf8748d-62d5-5dd2-b2cf-7abe86aa36a2" :answer/id #uuid "edf8748d-62d5-5dd2-b2cf-7abe86aa36a2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/03/032816ddcb597abbd191309cbd3acf3d5356c95939cf71a334b281ba97eb5c44.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-934bef13-4408-5aeb-90a6-87735671fcd5" :answer/id #uuid "934bef13-4408-5aeb-90a6-87735671fcd5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/10/105680ffd47ba3e3a2aeb8d4b1788b521118b9759a228f049adce28323f3337b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51e212d4-4747-53c2-a3de-6c59d58b03a4" :answer/id #uuid "51e212d4-4747-53c2-a3de-6c59d58b03a4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/45/45a423930fb07c365f10cdfbdeb3d45ef337078fa77e1b0699388088438d4c70.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8e7f2fa-a383-5a2b-a245-5265b3274d11" :answer/id #uuid "d8e7f2fa-a383-5a2b-a245-5265b3274d11" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/61/617f3763bcc8b674d901754934a95022327f7230c3e08d509b6818c0e87946f2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-221288b9-d358-51b3-a7a7-1f68c1f5fbe9" :question/id #uuid "221288b9-d358-51b3-a7a7-1f68c1f5fbe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-174396" :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(r)=0,\\,{1}_{B}(r)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=0$\n\nIII. $r\\in A\\cap B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(r),{1}_{B}(r)\\} \\\\  & =min\\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=0,$ we conclude that $r$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3d770560-9db0-5560-9355-e7dc79cae0de"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3d770560-9db0-5560-9355-e7dc79cae0de" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d770560-9db0-5560-9355-e7dc79cae0de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-55ddd461-5a2f-57c6-b993-62a0d935299c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-313fc3c9-7590-5457-9934-cda93c6d94c4" "answer-c216a131-75a7-5cd7-bffb-fff0f617da7f" "answer-b176ee50-07eb-571e-ad55-5d2c49c89f1e" "answer-55ddd461-5a2f-57c6-b993-62a0d935299c" "answer-4784394b-3ad7-56c7-a59a-38b9a0ea2047"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-313fc3c9-7590-5457-9934-cda93c6d94c4" :answer/id #uuid "313fc3c9-7590-5457-9934-cda93c6d94c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c216a131-75a7-5cd7-bffb-fff0f617da7f" :answer/id #uuid "c216a131-75a7-5cd7-bffb-fff0f617da7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b176ee50-07eb-571e-ad55-5d2c49c89f1e" :answer/id #uuid "b176ee50-07eb-571e-ad55-5d2c49c89f1e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55ddd461-5a2f-57c6-b993-62a0d935299c" :answer/id #uuid "55ddd461-5a2f-57c6-b993-62a0d935299c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4784394b-3ad7-56c7-a59a-38b9a0ea2047" :answer/id #uuid "4784394b-3ad7-56c7-a59a-38b9a0ea2047" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-95e868f8-4928-5f0b-9ec7-afed42df8e19" :question/id #uuid "95e868f8-4928-5f0b-9ec7-afed42df8e19" :question/math-academy-id "q-174406" :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(2)=0,\\,{1}_{B}(2)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=0$\n\nIII. $2\\in A\\cup B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(2),{1}_{B}(2)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=0,$ we conclude that $2$ does not belong to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8b452e7a-b4a9-5355-886f-bd8e112df6b7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8b452e7a-b4a9-5355-886f-bd8e112df6b7" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b452e7a-b4a9-5355-886f-bd8e112df6b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-feed8f1c-d04a-5f27-b4a6-6f25785faaa3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6c956e81-ff45-5e0b-9f25-09b9d9b31488" "answer-7638f7ea-4425-5857-8b90-4445c121054f" "answer-f91bb51f-796c-5238-a7f9-e0d6cedfe524" "answer-feed8f1c-d04a-5f27-b4a6-6f25785faaa3" "answer-736ab622-ab04-58e2-9a58-9098136b55bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c956e81-ff45-5e0b-9f25-09b9d9b31488" :answer/id #uuid "6c956e81-ff45-5e0b-9f25-09b9d9b31488" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7638f7ea-4425-5857-8b90-4445c121054f" :answer/id #uuid "7638f7ea-4425-5857-8b90-4445c121054f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f91bb51f-796c-5238-a7f9-e0d6cedfe524" :answer/id #uuid "f91bb51f-796c-5238-a7f9-e0d6cedfe524" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-feed8f1c-d04a-5f27-b4a6-6f25785faaa3" :answer/id #uuid "feed8f1c-d04a-5f27-b4a6-6f25785faaa3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-736ab622-ab04-58e2-9a58-9098136b55bd" :answer/id #uuid "736ab622-ab04-58e2-9a58-9098136b55bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092604 :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(5)=1,\\,{1}_{B}(5)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=0$\n\nIII. $5\\in A\\cup B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(5),{1}_{B}(5)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{1,0\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=1,$ we conclude that $5$ belongs to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186089795 :answer-field/correct 17592186089797 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093496 :tutorial/content "Recall that the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n![](images/74/7408ad6ce2700ac59d8e6a78c511ba2b51551e6b9d039ee7e2f8548f4177802e.svg)\n\nTherefore, the indicator function of the complement $\\overline{A}$ is given by \n\n![](images/de/de1a51c00e16d836fde350e0936fb9cb63124c72b49174210be3e6f6faa152da.svg)\n\nThis means that the indicator function of $A$ and its complement are related as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the indicator functions of $A \\cap B$ and union $A \\cup B$ can be expressed through the indicator functions of $A$ and $B.$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *intersection*, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *union*, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)+{1}_{B}(x)-{1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can prove these results by splitting them into cases." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184202 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c840c5f3-0fd7-561e-88b0-406ba1f39b38" "question-ca565001-9394-5e0a-81d1-cb1231ca5afa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184099 :question/problem "What is the graph of $y = f(x),$ where $f(x) = 2x - 3?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of $y = 2x - 3$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y = 2x - 3.$\nFor example,\n\n- if $x = 0$ then $y = 2(0) - 3 = -3,$ which gives the point $(0,-3),$ and\n\n- if $x = 1$ then $y = 2(1) - 3 = -1,$ which gives the point $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs in the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](images/9f/9f3005c9fac13668b126396f11aa8f3141d6de63d6e88e90d270901a9253fbd1.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184201 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-26e491f3-a709-5e27-8211-3cc9fc3f4884" "question-13822b6c-654c-513f-8912-18bafd642a51" "question-930ee824-1c66-5c5d-8da9-b30bfa636dd4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184098 :question/problem "![](images/57/57be4d7696363594e9d100ba75a2df1acc9742b8604ead7c9d1176c51571032d.png)\n\nThe function $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Calculate $f(-2) + f(1).$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-2,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(-2)=-2.$\n\n- When $x=1,$ the function is **undefined** because the function has an open circle at this point.\n\nTherefore, since $f(1)$ is undefined, the sum $f(-2)+f(1)$ is undefined too." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c840c5f3-0fd7-561e-88b0-406ba1f39b38" :question/id #uuid "c840c5f3-0fd7-561e-88b0-406ba1f39b38" :question/math-academy-id "q-130593" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x),$ where $f(x)=3x-3?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of $y=3x-3$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=3x-3.$\nFor example,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=3(0)-3=-3,$ which gives the point $(0,-3),$ and\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=3(1)-3=0,$ which gives the point $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](images/58/58731786b9a03b6b76a0a9fa0b91563d2b4a098c8a0e8b1d5ace5690d3ea2913.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f4e31995-1049-503e-a9fc-864c3641cf3e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f4e31995-1049-503e-a9fc-864c3641cf3e" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e31995-1049-503e-a9fc-864c3641cf3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bbb611dc-7fa3-51ce-afc5-0416e9d8adac" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7b9fc9fc-b7f0-5888-9c1c-0776092763f6" "answer-a1e31324-3dd3-5a5d-a012-929cbb3f703b" "answer-1f92356c-4590-5619-8068-2113ec753adc" "answer-bbb611dc-7fa3-51ce-afc5-0416e9d8adac" "answer-b023b88f-af67-5b9d-a4dc-8ae6688ded59"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b9fc9fc-b7f0-5888-9c1c-0776092763f6" :answer/id #uuid "7b9fc9fc-b7f0-5888-9c1c-0776092763f6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4b/4b5c2e46a4918c1738024b866a059749f257a0053dc15a6b2542d95d520be73e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1e31324-3dd3-5a5d-a012-929cbb3f703b" :answer/id #uuid "a1e31324-3dd3-5a5d-a012-929cbb3f703b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/7e/7eaca86e8ea70870f0242915c3ee9bd0bd6bcc5f0c3647c2cf6f427e8f1a3639.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f92356c-4590-5619-8068-2113ec753adc" :answer/id #uuid "1f92356c-4590-5619-8068-2113ec753adc" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/8c/8c83aacabad16e7c0ca61b1e63a952222d0f3c32c5b11b1ec8045476b7888dde.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbb611dc-7fa3-51ce-afc5-0416e9d8adac" :answer/id #uuid "bbb611dc-7fa3-51ce-afc5-0416e9d8adac" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a2/a278ea47247ddfb2f3ed6e646a416a665cffdd9005db3945d2b834b4e9307079.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b023b88f-af67-5b9d-a4dc-8ae6688ded59" :answer/id #uuid "b023b88f-af67-5b9d-a4dc-8ae6688ded59" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f3/f39cd25d7aafdf95a066f27503b95906d0cf988e69f3e662409139b629e3e206.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ca565001-9394-5e0a-81d1-cb1231ca5afa" :question/id #uuid "ca565001-9394-5e0a-81d1-cb1231ca5afa" :question/math-academy-id "q-130598" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x),$ where $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\nFor example,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}(0)=2,$ which gives the point $(0,2),$ and\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}(2)=1,$ which gives the point $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](images/14/14ca58b7ec006bba9465672bb00da30e2539125f66a460ad5b5ea38521a836f5.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-adec00fc-9488-5266-85d4-ebcd586ff2c5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-adec00fc