y-id "q-130555" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c827895f55c7615d8c74b4cc2b25dabaa674108a5889ca78656d812b3a09216.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Calculate $f(-3)+f(2).$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-3,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(-3)=-2.$\n\n- When $x=2,$ the function is undefined.\n\nTherefore, since $f(2)$ is undefined, the sum $f(-3)+f(2)$ is undefined too." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b7557141-90d9-599b-a963-7c24580db883"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b7557141-90d9-599b-a963-7c24580db883" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7557141-90d9-599b-a963-7c24580db883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d9fde4db-e8cf-5651-9156-5164910baced" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dac05560-604f-59a2-a9d8-9b854a529250" "answer-00206c99-c52e-5e11-a583-8f426daa9681" "answer-be62cf62-6aea-5012-9408-1100a5054418" "answer-64f3340a-f6df-5ada-a6b8-e70d6f0f39ea" "answer-d9fde4db-e8cf-5651-9156-5164910baced"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dac05560-604f-59a2-a9d8-9b854a529250" :answer/id #uuid "dac05560-604f-59a2-a9d8-9b854a529250" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00206c99-c52e-5e11-a583-8f426daa9681" :answer/id #uuid "00206c99-c52e-5e11-a583-8f426daa9681" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be62cf62-6aea-5012-9408-1100a5054418" :answer/id #uuid "be62cf62-6aea-5012-9408-1100a5054418" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64f3340a-f6df-5ada-a6b8-e70d6f0f39ea" :answer/id #uuid "64f3340a-f6df-5ada-a6b8-e70d6f0f39ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9fde4db-e8cf-5651-9156-5164910baced" :answer/id #uuid "d9fde4db-e8cf-5651-9156-5164910baced" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-13822b6c-654c-513f-8912-18bafd642a51" :question/id #uuid "13822b6c-654c-513f-8912-18bafd642a51" :question/math-academy-id "q-130553" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0fa5339a6017befb3ac178aa474b6455a5392f79738d2faa3f8861a987c5488b.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Calculate $f(-3)+f(3).$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-3,$ the function is undefined.\n\n- When $x=3,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(3)=-2.$\n\nTherefore, since $f(-3)$ is undefined, the sum $f(-3)+f(3)$ is undefined too." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d2edd12c-7bfd-53fc-9971-080daa8a2888"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d2edd12c-7bfd-53fc-9971-080daa8a2888" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2edd12c-7bfd-53fc-9971-080daa8a2888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-77a7f61a-dc5a-542a-8df3-cadc1317aa06" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83413367-58be-5089-bb29-605318b5f7e6" "answer-aac89784-adf4-5b95-b933-f9d7b9856ca3" "answer-a8e0269f-d14a-56dc-8bd8-b5d01d7255d5" "answer-c9e0d5ed-7875-597c-99c6-46920be65b78" "answer-77a7f61a-dc5a-542a-8df3-cadc1317aa06"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83413367-58be-5089-bb29-605318b5f7e6" :answer/id #uuid "83413367-58be-5089-bb29-605318b5f7e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aac89784-adf4-5b95-b933-f9d7b9856ca3" :answer/id #uuid "aac89784-adf4-5b95-b933-f9d7b9856ca3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8e0269f-d14a-56dc-8bd8-b5d01d7255d5" :answer/id #uuid "a8e0269f-d14a-56dc-8bd8-b5d01d7255d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9e0d5ed-7875-597c-99c6-46920be65b78" :answer/id #uuid "c9e0d5ed-7875-597c-99c6-46920be65b78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77a7f61a-dc5a-542a-8df3-cadc1317aa06" :answer/id #uuid "77a7f61a-dc5a-542a-8df3-cadc1317aa06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-930ee824-1c66-5c5d-8da9-b30bfa636dd4" :question/id #uuid "930ee824-1c66-5c5d-8da9-b30bfa636dd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130517" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f744e2729965bffbf281aee4a2bb2ecbdca635035e5fefca8f992d59d2590a1.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is given by the graph above. Calculate $f(1)+f(4).$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=1,$ we have $y=2.$ So, $f(1)=2.$\n\n- When $x=4,$ we have $y=3.$ So, $f(4)=3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1)+f(4)=2+3=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-01c2f4be-574d-5a90-9082-145cbb9b7166"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-01c2f4be-574d-5a90-9082-145cbb9b7166" :answer-field/id #uuid "01c2f4be-574d-5a90-9082-145cbb9b7166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-770c4525-28c6-5ebf-b73a-afcf855636a1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f7b254fd-0be4-5a19-9d61-a7d3cbf5d222" "answer-edc9a486-f337-56d4-8c0e-9b9f2836087c" "answer-7ca7c832-7633-5f70-977c-619d612db5ec" "answer-770c4525-28c6-5ebf-b73a-afcf855636a1" "answer-c5127028-73db-5dac-907b-e57b56d7e94c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7b254fd-0be4-5a19-9d61-a7d3cbf5d222" :answer/id #uuid "f7b254fd-0be4-5a19-9d61-a7d3cbf5d222" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edc9a486-f337-56d4-8c0e-9b9f2836087c" :answer/id #uuid "edc9a486-f337-56d4-8c0e-9b9f2836087c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ca7c832-7633-5f70-977c-619d612db5ec" :answer/id #uuid "7ca7c832-7633-5f70-977c-619d612db5ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-770c4525-28c6-5ebf-b73a-afcf855636a1" :answer/id #uuid "770c4525-28c6-5ebf-b73a-afcf855636a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5127028-73db-5dac-907b-e57b56d7e94c" :answer/id #uuid "c5127028-73db-5dac-907b-e57b56d7e94c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185323 :tutorial/content "We can plot a function $f(x)$ by setting $y=f(x).$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function \n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2-1,\n$$\n\n where $x$ is any number taken from the set $\\{-2, -1, 0, 1,2 \\}.$\n\nFirst, we create a table containing the input values:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nGiven a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-value by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=x^2-1.$ For example,\n\n- if $x=-2$ then $y=(-2)^2-1=3,$\n\n- if $x=-1$ then $y=(-1)^2-1=0,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in the same way, we get the following values:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs in the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/06/0623f7a3756288a982b2e26dbbf03f6ed924f2cdea495764bc78ed547efbbcc3.png)\n\nWe don't connect the points since $f(x)$ is defined only for values of $x$ contained within the set $\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.$ \n\nLet's now take a look at an example where $x$ is unrestricted." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185324 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa86c914aeedc2ff973d22228fb11902aa7246fa751bddb75746e517d97fa60b.png)\n\nWe can determine the values of this function at particular points by reading them off its graph. \n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we know that $f(-2) = 3.$\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(-1,0),$ we know that $f(-1) = 0.$\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(0,-1),$ we know that $f(0) = -1.$\n\nNotice that the point $(2,3)$ is shown with an empty circle. This means that the function is **undefined** at the point where $x=2.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184406 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134310 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ $b=0,$ $c=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|+|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326899 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 3+0\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+7\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-\\sqrt{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{1}=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134337 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $c=-4,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{a}=\\frac{-4}{1}=-4.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186414752 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=0,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354526 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)}{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5},$ $c=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}|+|\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}|=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-c6f0a606-7357-5b62-ba3e-c7abaf986519" :activity/id #uuid "c6f0a606-7357-5b62-ba3e-c7abaf986519" :activity/title "The Gram-Schmidt Process for Two Vectors" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050578 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-f6bf6f1f-05a8-52ae-97c6-6d0881baed05" "step-560834e6-f0bc-5556-8407-8ecea32f604c" "step-8acc1726-5d26-585c-9955-88497139998b" "step-3587edc5-deb1-5629-b533-df76f3ade5d0" "step-3e0438aa-82a5-54a7-85cc-d1569f51001a"] :activity/first-step "step-f6bf6f1f-05a8-52ae-97c6-6d0881baed05" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f6bf6f1f-05a8-52ae-97c6-6d0881baed05" :step/id #uuid "f6bf6f1f-05a8-52ae-97c6-6d0881baed05" :step/content 17592186134310 :step/next "step-560834e6-f0bc-5556-8407-8ecea32f604c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-560834e6-f0bc-5556-8407-8ecea32f604c" :step/id #uuid "560834e6-f0bc-5556-8407-8ecea32f604c" :step/content 17592186326899 :step/next "step-8acc1726-5d26-585c-9955-88497139998b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8acc1726-5d26-585c-9955-88497139998b" :step/id #uuid "8acc1726-5d26